// Generated by main.py from formula_sheet.html. Do not edit.
window.FORMULAS = {"F1.1": "\n      <h4>F1.1 &nbsp;The Triple</h4>\n      <div class=\"eq\">$(\\Omega, \\mathcal F, P)$</div>\n      <div class=\"eq\">$\\Omega$ = outcomes &nbsp;|&nbsp; $\\mathcal F$ = events we may assign probability to &nbsp;|&nbsp; $P$ = the measure</div>\n      <div class=\"eq\">event $A \\subseteq \\Omega$ <b>happened</b> $\\iff \\omega \\in A$</div>\n      <div class=\"use\"><b>Use when:</b> every definition question starts here. $\\mathcal F$ is not decoration &mdash; it is <em>which questions you are allowed to ask</em>.</div>\n      <div class=\"ex\"><b>Q:</b> &ldquo;short note on the probability space&rdquo;.<br><b>Trick:</b> one line each: $\\Omega$ is what can happen, $\\mathcal F$ is what you can ask, $P$ is how likely. Then give the die example.</div>", "F1.2": "\n      <h4>F1.2 &nbsp;&sigma;-Field Axioms</h4>\n      <div class=\"eq\">(i) $\\varnothing \\in \\mathcal F$</div>\n      <div class=\"eq\">(ii) $A \\in \\mathcal F \\Rightarrow A^{c} \\in \\mathcal F$</div>\n      <div class=\"eq\">(iii) $A_1, A_2, \\ldots \\in \\mathcal F \\Rightarrow \\bigcup_i A_i \\in \\mathcal F$</div>\n      <div class=\"use\"><b>Use when:</b> &ldquo;is this collection a $\\sigma$-field?&rdquo; Check in order. <b>Closure under complement is the usual failure</b> &mdash; test it first, it is fastest.</div>\n      <div class=\"ex\"><b>Q:</b> die, is $\\{\\varnothing, \\{6\\}, \\Omega\\}$ a $\\sigma$-field? <b>No</b> &mdash; $\\{6\\}^c = \\{1,2,3,4,5\\}$ is missing.<br><b>Trick:</b> intersections come free: $A\\cap B = (A^c \\cup B^c)^c$. You never check them separately.</div>", "F1.3": "\n      <h4>F1.3 &nbsp;Probability Measure Axioms</h4>\n      <div class=\"eq\">$P(A) \\ge 0$; &nbsp; $P(\\Omega) = 1$; &nbsp; $P\\big(\\bigsqcup_i A_i\\big) = \\sum_i P(A_i)$ (disjoint)</div>\n      <div class=\"eq\">$P(A^c) = 1 - P(A)$, &nbsp; $A \\subseteq B \\Rightarrow P(A) \\le P(B)$</div>\n      <div class=\"eq\">$P(A\\cup B) = P(A)+P(B)-P(A\\cap B)$, &nbsp; $P\\big(\\bigcup A_i\\big) \\le \\sum P(A_i)$</div>\n      <div class=\"use\"><b>Use when:</b> the subadditivity (union bound) on the last line is the one that does real work &mdash; it is step 6 of Borel&ndash;Cantelli (F2.5).</div>\n      <div class=\"ex\"><b>Q:</b> die, $A=\\{1,2\\}$, $B=\\{2,3\\}$: $P(A\\cup B) = \\tfrac12 \\le \\tfrac13+\\tfrac13$.<br><b>Trick:</b> the union bound is loose exactly by the double-counted overlap.</div>\n      <details class=\"proof\"><summary>Proof</summary><div>$A$ and $A^c$ are disjoint with union $\\Omega$: $P(A)+P(A^c)=P(\\Omega)=1$.<br>$A\\subseteq B$: $B=A\\sqcup(B\\setminus A)$, so $P(B)=P(A)+P(B\\setminus A)\\ge P(A)$. With $A=\\varnothing$, $B=\\varnothing$: $P(\\varnothing)=2P(\\varnothing)$, so $P(\\varnothing)=0$.</div></details>", "F1.4": "\n      <h4>F1.4 &nbsp;Continuity of Measure</h4>\n      <div class=\"eq\">$A_n \\uparrow A \\;\\Rightarrow\\; P(A_n) \\to P(A)$ &nbsp;(from below)</div>\n      <div class=\"eq\">$A_n \\downarrow A \\;\\Rightarrow\\; P(A_n) \\to P(A)$ &nbsp;(from above)</div>\n      <div class=\"use\"><b>Use when:</b> two places on this paper &mdash; right-continuity of the d.f. (F5.3) and step 5 of Borel&ndash;Cantelli (F2.5). Both times you need the <em>decreasing</em> version.</div>\n      <div class=\"ex\"><b>Trick:</b> spot the nesting first. If the sets shrink you may swap $\\lim$ and $P$; if they neither grow nor shrink you may not.</div>\n      <details class=\"proof\"><summary>Proof</summary><div><b>From below.</b> Cut into rings: $C_1=A_1$, $C_n=A_n\\setminus A_{n-1}$. They are disjoint, $A_n=C_1\\sqcup\\dots\\sqcup C_n$ and $A=\\bigsqcup_n C_n$. So $P(A)=\\sum_nP(C_n)=\\lim_n\\sum_{k\\le n}P(C_k)=\\lim_nP(A_n)$.<br><b>From above.</b> $A_n\\downarrow A$ means $A_n^c\\uparrow A^c$; apply the first part and use $P(A^c)=1-P(A)$.</div></details>", "F1.5": "\n      <h4>F1.5 &nbsp;Counting &sigma;-Fields (finite $\\Omega$)</h4>\n      <div class=\"eq\">generated by a partition into $k$ atoms $\\Rightarrow$ exactly $2^k$ events</div>\n      <div class=\"eq\">smallest: $\\{\\varnothing,\\Omega\\}$ &nbsp;|&nbsp; largest: $2^{\\Omega}$, i.e. $2^{|\\Omega|}$ events</div>\n      <div class=\"use\"><b>Use when:</b> &ldquo;how many events are in $\\sigma(\\xi)$?&rdquo; <b>Count the distinct values of $\\xi$, call it $k$, answer $2^k$.</b> Never enumerate.</div>\n      <div class=\"ex\"><b>Q:</b> die. $\\sigma(\\text{even/odd})$: 2 atoms $\\to \\mathbf{4}$ events. $\\sigma(\\omega)$: 6 atoms $\\to 2^6 = \\mathbf{64}$. Constant r.v.: 1 atom $\\to \\mathbf{2}$.</div>\n      <details class=\"proof\"><summary>Proof</summary><div>Every event of the $\\sigma$-field is a union of some of the $k$ atoms (an atom is either fully inside or fully outside it). Each atom is in or out: $2^k$ choices, and different choices give different sets because atoms are disjoint and non-empty.<br>Largest: every point is its own atom, $k=|\\Omega|$. Smallest: one atom $\\Omega$, $k=1$: $\\{\\varnothing,\\Omega\\}$.</div></details>", "F2.1": "\n      <h4>F2.1 &nbsp;Almost Surely</h4>\n      <div class=\"eq\">&ldquo;$A$ holds a.s.&rdquo; $\\iff P(A) = 1$</div>\n      <div class=\"eq\">equivalently $P(A^c) = 0$ &mdash; exceptions allowed, but only on a null set</div>\n      <div class=\"use\"><b>Use when:</b> a.s. is <em>not</em> &ldquo;always&rdquo;. A continuous r.v. satisfies $\\xi \\ne 0.5$ a.s. even though $\\omega$ with $\\xi = 0.5$ exist.</div>\n      <div class=\"ex\"><b>Trick:</b> in a finite $\\Omega$ with all $P(\\omega) &gt; 0$ there are no null sets, so a.s. and always coincide. That is why die examples never show the distinction.</div>", "F2.2": "\n      <h4>F2.2 &nbsp;The limsup Event (&ldquo;infinitely often&rdquo;)</h4>\n      <div class=\"eq\">$E = \\limsup_n A_n = \\bigcap_{n=1}^{\\infty}\\underbrace{\\bigcup_{i=n}^{\\infty} A_i}_{B_n}$</div>\n      <div class=\"eq\">$= \\{\\omega : \\omega$ belongs to <b>infinitely many</b> $A_i\\}$</div>\n      <div class=\"use\"><b>Use when:</b> the words &ldquo;infinitely often&rdquo; or &ldquo;i.o.&rdquo; appear. Note $B_n \\downarrow$, which is what licenses F1.4.</div>\n      <div class=\"ex\"><b>Read it aloud:</b> &ldquo;for every $n$, there is some $i \\ge n$ with $\\omega \\in A_i$&rdquo; &mdash; i.e. you never run out of later hits.<br><b>Q:</b> coin, $A_i = \\{$toss $i$ is H$\\}$. $\\omega = HTHTHT\\ldots \\in E$; $\\omega = HHTTTT\\ldots \\notin E$.</div>\n      <details class=\"proof\"><summary>Proof</summary><div>$\\omega\\in\\bigcap_n\\bigcup_{i\\ge n}A_i$ $\\iff$ for every $n$ there is some $i\\ge n$ with $\\omega\\in A_i$ $\\iff$ however far you go, $\\omega$ is in a later $A_i$ $\\iff$ $\\omega$ is in infinitely many $A_i$.</div></details>", "F2.3": "\n      <h4>F2.3 &nbsp;Borel&ndash;Cantelli I</h4>\n      <div class=\"eq\">$\\displaystyle\\sum_{i=1}^{\\infty} P(A_i) &lt; \\infty \\;\\Longrightarrow\\; P(\\limsup A_n) = 0$</div>\n      <div class=\"eq\">i.e. a.s. only <b>finitely many</b> $A_i$ occur</div>\n      <div class=\"cond\">No independence needed.</div>\n      <div class=\"use\"><b>Use when:</b> the sum converges. One direction only &mdash; a <em>divergent</em> sum tells you nothing on its own.</div>\n      <div class=\"ex\"><b>Q:</b> $\\Omega=[0,1]$ uniform, $A_i = [0, 1/i^2]$. $\\sum P(A_i) = \\pi^2/6 &lt; \\infty$, so $P(E) = \\mathbf{0}$ &mdash; and indeed $E = \\{0\\}$.<br><b>Trick:</b> the conclusion is &ldquo;there is a random cutoff $N(\\omega)$ after which $A_i$ never happens again&rdquo;.</div>\n      <details class=\"proof\"><summary>Proof</summary><div>$B_n=\\bigcup_{i\\ge n}A_i$ decreases to $E=\\limsup A_n$, so $P(E)=\\lim_nP(B_n)$ (&sect;F1.4).<br>Union bound: $P(B_n)\\le\\sum_{i\\ge n}P(A_i)$, the tail of a convergent series, which $\\to0$. Hence $P(E)=0$. (This is the chain in &sect;F2.5.)</div></details>", "F2.4": "\n      <h4>F2.4 &nbsp;Borel&ndash;Cantelli II (needs independence)</h4>\n      <div class=\"eq\">$A_i$ <b>independent</b> and $\\displaystyle\\sum_i P(A_i) = \\infty \\;\\Longrightarrow\\; P(\\limsup A_n) = 1$</div>\n      <div class=\"use\"><b>Use when:</b> divergent sum <em>and</em> independence. Drop independence and the conclusion dies.</div>\n      <div class=\"ex\"><b>Counterexample:</b> $A_i = [0, 1/i]$. $\\sum 1/i = \\infty$, but the $A_i$ are nested (maximally dependent) and $E = \\{0\\}$, so $P(E) = 0$, not $1$.<br><b>Trick:</b> state which lemma you are using and verify <em>its</em> hypothesis &mdash; that is where the mark is.</div>\n      <details class=\"proof\"><summary>Proof</summary><div>$E^c=\\bigcup_n\\bigcap_{i\\ge n}A_i^c$, so $P(E^c)\\le\\sum_nP\\big(\\bigcap_{i\\ge n}A_i^c\\big)$. Fix $n$. By independence and $1-x\\le e^{-x}$:<br>$P\\big(\\bigcap_{i=n}^{m}A_i^c\\big)=\\prod_{i=n}^{m}\\big(1-P(A_i)\\big)\\le\\exp\\Big(-\\sum_{i=n}^{m}P(A_i)\\Big)\\to0$ as $m\\to\\infty$, since the sum diverges.<br>So every term is $0$, $P(E^c)=0$, $P(E)=1$.</div></details>", "F2.5": "\n      <h4>F2.5 &nbsp;The Proof Chain (BC&nbsp;I)</h4>\n      <div class=\"eq\">$0 \\le P(E) = \\lim_n P(B_n) \\le \\lim_n \\sum_{i\\ge n} P(A_i) = 0$</div>\n      <div class=\"use\"><b>Use when:</b> asked to <em>prove</em> it. Exactly three ingredients, in this order:<br>1. $B_n \\downarrow$ &nbsp; 2. continuity from above (F1.4) &nbsp; 3. union bound (F1.3).<br>The final $0$ is because a convergent series has vanishing tails.</div>\n      <div class=\"ex\"><b>Trick:</b> write the sandwich $0 \\le \\cdots \\le 0$ explicitly. It is worth full marks on its own and takes four lines.</div>", "F3.1": "\n      <h4>F3.1 &nbsp;Definition</h4>\n      <div class=\"eq\">$X:\\Omega\\to\\mathbb R$ is $\\mathcal F$-measurable if</div>\n      <div class=\"eq\">$[X \\in B] := X^{-1}(B) = \\{\\omega : X(\\omega)\\in B\\} \\in \\mathcal F \\quad \\forall B \\in \\mathcal B(\\mathbb R)$</div>\n      <div class=\"eq\">A r.v. = an $\\mathcal F$-measurable function on a probability space</div>\n      <div class=\"use\"><b>Use when:</b> the <em>reason</em> for the definition is the marked point: it is exactly what makes $P(X\\in B)$ well defined.</div>\n      <div class=\"ex\"><b>Trick:</b> a random variable is not random and is not a variable &mdash; it is a deterministic function of $\\omega$. All the randomness lives in which $\\omega$ occurs.</div>", "F3.2": "\n      <h4>F3.2 &nbsp;You Only Check the Generators</h4>\n      <div class=\"eq\">$X$ is $\\mathcal F$-measurable $\\iff \\{X \\le x\\} \\in \\mathcal F$ for every $x \\in \\mathbb R$</div>\n      <div class=\"cond\">Because $\\{(-\\infty,x] : x\\in\\mathbb R\\}$ generates $\\mathcal B(\\mathbb R)$.</div>\n      <div class=\"use\"><b>Use when:</b> <b>always.</b> Never attempt to test every Borel set &mdash; test the half-lines and cite that they generate.</div>\n      <div class=\"ex\"><b>Q:</b> $\\Omega=\\{H,T\\}$, $X$ = #heads, $\\mathcal F = 2^{\\Omega}$. Pre-images of $(-\\infty,x]$: $\\varnothing$ ($x&lt;0$), $\\{T\\}$ ($0\\le x&lt;1$), $\\Omega$ ($x\\ge1$). All in $\\mathcal F$ ✓</div>\n      <details class=\"proof\"><summary>Proof</summary><div>(&rArr;) $(-\\infty,x]$ is a Borel set.<br>(&lArr;) Let $\\mathcal C=\\{B:\\ X^{-1}(B)\\in\\mathcal F\\}$. Preimages respect complements and countable unions, so $\\mathcal C$ is a $\\sigma$-field. It contains every $(-\\infty,x]$, so it contains the $\\sigma$-field they generate, which is all Borel sets.</div></details>", "F3.3": "\n      <h4>F3.3 &nbsp;Measurability Is Joint in $(X,\\mathcal F)$</h4>\n      <div class=\"eq\">the same $X$ can be $\\mathcal F$-measurable and <b>not</b> $\\mathcal G$-measurable</div>\n      <div class=\"use\"><b>Use when:</b> the question shrinks the $\\sigma$-field (&ldquo;you are only told whether the die was low or high&rdquo;). Measurability is a statement about <em>information</em>, not about the formula for $X$.</div>\n      <div class=\"ex\"><b>Q:</b> die, $\\mathcal G = \\{\\varnothing,\\{1,2,3\\},\\{4,5,6\\},\\Omega\\}$.<br>$Y = \\mathbb 1\\{\\omega\\ge4\\}$: $Y^{-1}(\\{1\\}) = \\{4,5,6\\}\\in\\mathcal G$ ✓ measurable.<br>$X = \\mathbb 1\\{\\omega$ even$\\}$: $X^{-1}(\\{1\\}) = \\{2,4,6\\}\\notin\\mathcal G$ ✗. Being told &ldquo;high&rdquo; does not tell you &ldquo;even&rdquo;.</div>\n      <details class=\"proof\"><summary>Proof</summary><div>Example: $\\Omega=\\{1,2\\}$, $X(\\omega)=\\omega$. With $\\mathcal F=2^\\Omega$, every $\\{X\\in B\\}$ is in $\\mathcal F$. With $\\mathcal G=\\{\\varnothing,\\Omega\\}$, $\\{X\\le1\\}=\\{1\\}\\notin\\mathcal G$. Same $X$, different answer.</div></details>", "F3.4": "\n      <h4>F3.4 &nbsp;⭐ The Atom Test</h4>\n      <div class=\"eq\">$X$ is $\\mathcal G$-measurable $\\iff$ $X$ is <b>constant on every atom of $\\mathcal G$</b></div>\n      <div class=\"cond\">Atoms = the finest blocks $\\mathcal G$ can distinguish.</div>\n      <div class=\"use\"><b>Use when:</b> every discrete measurability question on the paper. It is also, unchanged, the test for <em>adapted</em> (M1.2) and for Doob&ndash;Dynkin (F4.4). <b>Learn this one test and you get three topics.</b></div>\n      <div class=\"ex\"><b>Trick:</b> list the atoms, then scan $X$ down each block. Two different values inside one block $\\Rightarrow$ not measurable, and you have your counterexample for free.</div>\n      <details class=\"proof\"><summary>Proof</summary><div>(&rArr;) If $X$ took two values $x\\ne y$ on one atom $A$, the event $\\{X=x\\}\\in\\mathcal G$ would cut $A$ into two non-empty pieces, but an atom is the smallest non-empty block of $\\mathcal G$.<br>(&lArr;) Then $X=\\sum_kc_k\\mathbf 1_{A_k}$ and $\\{X\\in B\\}=\\bigcup\\{A_k: c_k\\in B\\}$, a union of atoms, which is in $\\mathcal G$.</div></details>", "F4.1": "\n      <h4>F4.1 &nbsp;$\\sigma(\\xi)$</h4>\n      <div class=\"eq\">$\\sigma(\\xi) = \\{\\xi^{-1}(B) : B \\in \\mathcal B(\\mathbb R)\\}$</div>\n      <div class=\"eq\">the smallest $\\sigma$-field making $\\xi$ measurable</div>\n      <div class=\"eq\">atoms of $\\sigma(\\xi)$ = the level sets $\\{\\xi = v\\}$</div>\n      <div class=\"use\"><b>Use when:</b> &ldquo;find $\\sigma(\\xi)$&rdquo; means &ldquo;find the level sets, then take all unions of them&rdquo;. Pre-images automatically satisfy the $\\sigma$-field axioms, so nothing needs checking.</div>\n      <div class=\"ex\"><b>Q:</b> die, $\\xi(\\omega) = \\omega \\bmod 3$. Atoms $\\{3,6\\},\\{1,4\\},\\{2,5\\}$, so $|\\sigma(\\xi)| = 2^3 = \\mathbf 8$.<br>Check: $\\xi^{-1}([0.5,5]) = \\{1,4\\}\\cup\\{2,5\\} = \\{1,2,4,5\\}$ ✓</div>\n      <details class=\"proof\"><summary>Proof</summary><div>Preimages respect complements and countable unions, so $\\{\\xi^{-1}(B)\\}$ is a $\\sigma$-field, and $\\xi$ is measurable for it by construction. Any $\\mathcal G$ making $\\xi$ measurable must contain every $\\xi^{-1}(B)$, so it contains $\\sigma(\\xi)$: smallest.<br>Atoms: two points can be separated by some $\\xi^{-1}(B)$ exactly when $\\xi$ differs at them, so the atoms are the level sets $\\{\\xi=x\\}$.</div></details>", "F4.2": "\n      <h4>F4.2 &nbsp;&sigma;-Field of a Family &amp; the Separation Principle</h4>\n      <div class=\"eq\">$\\sigma(\\xi_i, i\\in I) = \\sigma\\Big(\\bigcup_{i\\in I}\\sigma(\\xi_i)\\Big)$</div>\n      <div class=\"eq\">$\\omega_a,\\omega_b$ separable $\\iff \\exists\\, j$ with $\\xi_j(\\omega_a)\\ne\\xi_j(\\omega_b)$</div>\n      <div class=\"use\"><b>Use when:</b> comparing how much information different observables carry. Count atoms via the separation principle, then use F1.5.</div>\n      <div class=\"ex\"><b>Q:</b> two tosses, $\\xi_1,\\xi_2$ the indicators and $S = \\xi_1+\\xi_2$.<br>$\\sigma(\\xi_1)$: 2 atoms $\\to$ <b>4</b> events. $\\sigma(\\xi_1,\\xi_2)$: 4 atoms $\\to$ <b>16</b>. $\\sigma(S)$: $HT,TH$ both give $S{=}1$ so they merge &rarr; 3 atoms $\\to$ <b>8</b>.</div>\n      <details class=\"proof\"><summary>Proof</summary><div>The joint $\\sigma$-field must contain every $\\sigma(\\xi_i)$; the smallest $\\sigma$-field containing all of them is $\\sigma\\big(\\bigcup_i\\sigma(\\xi_i)\\big)$.<br>Separation: if $\\xi_j(\\omega_a)\\ne\\xi_j(\\omega_b)$, the event $\\{\\xi_j=\\xi_j(\\omega_a)\\}$ holds at $\\omega_a$, not at $\\omega_b$. If every $\\xi_j$ agrees, the sets containing both points or neither form a $\\sigma$-field holding all generators, so no event can separate them.</div></details>", "F4.3": "\n      <h4>F4.3 &nbsp;Borel Functions Compose</h4>\n      <div class=\"eq\">$f$ Borel, $\\xi$ a r.v. $\\Longrightarrow f(\\xi)$ is $\\sigma(\\xi)$-measurable</div>\n      <div class=\"eq\">proof: $\\{f(\\xi)\\in B\\} = \\xi^{-1}\\big(f^{-1}(B)\\big)$</div>\n      <div class=\"use\"><b>Use when:</b> two-line proof, always the same two lines. Note it says $\\sigma(f(\\xi)) \\subseteq \\sigma(\\xi)$ &mdash; applying a function can only <em>lose</em> information.</div>\n      <div class=\"ex\"><b>Q:</b> die, $\\xi(\\omega)=\\omega$, $f(x)=x^2$. $\\{f(\\xi)=16\\} = \\xi^{-1}(\\{-4,4\\}) = \\{4\\} \\in \\sigma(\\xi)$ ✓<br><b>Trick:</b> $f$ can merge atoms ($f(x)=x^2$ merges $\\pm$), never split one.</div>", "F4.4": "\n      <h4>F4.4 &nbsp;⭐ Doob&ndash;Dynkin Lemma</h4>\n      <div class=\"eq\">$\\eta$ is $\\sigma(\\xi)$-measurable $\\iff \\exists$ Borel $f$ with $\\eta = f(\\xi)$</div>\n      <div class=\"use\"><b>Use when:</b> &ldquo;show $\\eta$ is a function of $\\xi$&rdquo; or &ldquo;is $\\eta$ $\\sigma(\\xi)$-measurable?&rdquo;<br><b>Meaning:</b> knowing the <em>value</em> $\\xi(\\omega)$ pins down $\\eta(\\omega)$ &mdash; no leftover randomness.</div>\n      <div class=\"ex\"><b>The one-line test:</b> does $\\xi$ ever take the <em>same</em> value at two $\\omega$ where $\\eta$ <em>differs</em>? If yes &rarr; not measurable, and those two $\\omega$ are your counterexample.<br><b>Q:</b> $\\xi(\\omega_1){=}\\xi(\\omega_2){=}2$, $\\xi(\\omega_3){=}5$; $\\eta = 9,9,25$. Ties in $\\xi$ match ties in $\\eta$ ✓, so $\\eta = f(\\xi)$ with $f(2){=}9$, $f(5){=}25$.</div>\n      <details class=\"proof\"><summary>Proof</summary><div>(&lArr;) $\\{\\eta\\in B\\} = \\{\\xi \\in f^{-1}(B)\\}$ and $f^{-1}(B)$ is Borel, so the event is in $\\sigma(\\xi)$.<br>\n(&rArr;, discrete $\\xi$) $\\xi$ takes values $x_1,x_2,\\dots$; the atoms $A_k=\\{\\xi=x_k\\}$ generate $\\sigma(\\xi)$. A $\\sigma(\\xi)$-measurable $\\eta$ is constant on each atom, say $\\eta=c_k$ on $A_k$. Put $f(x_k)=c_k$ (and $f=0$ elsewhere). Then $\\eta=f(\\xi)$.<br>\nGeneral $\\xi$: approximate $\\eta$ by simple functions, apply the discrete case to each, pass to the limit.</div></details>", "F5.1": "\n      <h4>F5.1 &nbsp;Push-Forward (Distribution) Measure</h4>\n      <div class=\"eq\">$P_\\xi(B) = P(\\xi\\in B) = P\\big(\\{\\omega : \\xi(\\omega)\\in B\\}\\big)$</div>\n      <div class=\"cond\">A probability measure on $(\\mathbb R, \\mathcal B(\\mathbb R))$.</div>\n      <div class=\"use\"><b>Use when:</b> $P_\\xi$ transports probability from the abstract $\\Omega$ onto the real line. Once you have $P_\\xi$ you never need $\\Omega$ again (that is E2.1).</div>\n      <div class=\"ex\"><b>Q:</b> two tosses, $\\xi$ = #heads, vs. a uniform $\\omega\\in[0,1]$ cut at $0.25$ and $0.75$. Completely different $\\Omega$, <b>identical</b> $P_\\xi$.<br><b>Trick:</b> $P_\\xi$ records values and their probabilities and forgets everything else.</div>\n      <details class=\"proof\"><summary>Proof</summary><div>$P_\\xi(B)=P(\\xi^{-1}(B))\\ge0$; $P_\\xi(\\mathbb R)=P(\\Omega)=1$. Disjoint $B_i$ have disjoint preimages and $\\xi^{-1}(\\bigcup B_i)=\\bigcup\\xi^{-1}(B_i)$, so $P_\\xi(\\bigcup B_i)=\\sum P_\\xi(B_i)$.</div></details>", "F5.2": "\n      <h4>F5.2 &nbsp;Distribution Function</h4>\n      <div class=\"eq\">$F_\\xi(x) = P(\\xi\\le x) = P_\\xi\\big((-\\infty,x]\\big)$</div>\n      <div class=\"eq\">$P(a &lt; \\xi \\le b) = F(b) - F(a)$</div>\n      <div class=\"use\"><b>Use when:</b> note the interval formula is for $(a,b\\,]$ &mdash; half-open, matching the $\\le$ in the definition. Getting this wrong only matters at a jump (F5.4).</div>\n      <div class=\"ex\"><b>Q:</b> #heads in two tosses: $F = 0,\\ \\tfrac14,\\ \\tfrac34,\\ 1$ on $x&lt;0$, $[0,1)$, $[1,2)$, $x\\ge2$.<br>$F(1.99) = \\tfrac34$, $F(1) = \\tfrac34$, $F(0.5) = \\tfrac14$.</div>\n      <details class=\"proof\"><summary>Proof</summary><div>For $a&lt;b$: $\\{\\xi\\le b\\}=\\{\\xi\\le a\\}\\sqcup\\{a&lt;\\xi\\le b\\}$, so $F(b)=F(a)+P(a&lt;\\xi\\le b)$.</div></details>", "F5.3": "\n      <h4>F5.3 &nbsp;The Three Properties</h4>\n      <div class=\"eq\">(i) non-decreasing</div>\n      <div class=\"eq\">(ii) right-continuous: $\\lim_{h\\downarrow0}F(x+h) = F(x)$</div>\n      <div class=\"eq\">(iii) $F(-\\infty) = 0$, &nbsp; $F(+\\infty) = 1$</div>\n      <div class=\"use\"><b>Use when:</b> &ldquo;is this function a d.f.?&rdquo; or &ldquo;prove the properties&rdquo;. All three, and <b>right-continuity is the one they mark</b>.</div>\n      <div class=\"ex\"><b>Proof of (ii):</b> $x_n\\downarrow x$ gives $A_n = \\{\\xi\\le x_n\\}\\downarrow\\{\\xi\\le x\\}$; apply continuity from above (F1.4). That is the whole argument.<br><b>Note:</b> left limits may differ &mdash; $F(1^-) = \\tfrac14 \\ne \\tfrac34 = F(1)$ above.</div>\n      <details class=\"proof\"><summary>Proof</summary><div>(i) $x&lt;y\\Rightarrow\\{\\xi\\le x\\}\\subseteq\\{\\xi\\le y\\}$. (ii) $\\{\\xi\\le x+\\tfrac1n\\}\\downarrow\\{\\xi\\le x\\}$; continuity from above (&sect;F1.4). (iii) $\\{\\xi\\le-n\\}\\downarrow\\varnothing$ and $\\{\\xi\\le n\\}\\uparrow\\Omega$.</div></details>", "F5.4": "\n      <h4>F5.4 &nbsp;Jump Size = Point Mass</h4>\n      <div class=\"eq\">$P(\\xi = x) = F(x) - F(x^-)$</div>\n      <div class=\"eq\">$F$ continuous at $x \\iff P(\\xi = x) = 0$</div>\n      <div class=\"use\"><b>Use when:</b> deciding whether $&lt;$ and $\\le$ can be swapped. <b>At a jump they cannot.</b> For an absolutely continuous r.v. there are no jumps, so they always can.</div>\n      <div class=\"ex\"><b>Q:</b> #heads: $P(\\xi\\le1) = \\tfrac34$ but $P(\\xi&lt;1) = F(1^-) = \\tfrac14$.<br><b>Trick:</b> this is why every Brownian-motion formula on this sheet ignores strict vs non-strict &mdash; those laws are continuous.</div>\n      <details class=\"proof\"><summary>Proof</summary><div>$\\{\\xi\\le x-\\tfrac1n\\}\\uparrow\\{\\xi&lt;x\\}$, so $F(x^-)=P(\\xi&lt;x)$. Then $F(x)-F(x^-)=P(\\xi\\le x)-P(\\xi&lt;x)=P(\\xi=x)$. $F$ is always right-continuous, so it is continuous at $x$ iff this jump is $0$.</div></details>", "F6.1": "\n      <h4>F6.1 &nbsp;Absolutely Continuous</h4>\n      <div class=\"eq\">$P(\\xi\\in B) = \\displaystyle\\int_B f_\\xi(x)\\,dx$, &nbsp; $f_\\xi \\ge 0$, &nbsp; $\\int_{\\mathbb R} f_\\xi = 1$</div>\n      <div class=\"eq\">$f_\\xi(x) = F_\\xi'(x)$ wherever $F$ is differentiable</div>\n      <div class=\"use\"><b>Use when:</b> two traps live here. <b>(a)</b> a density is not a probability and may exceed $1$ (uniform on $[0,0.5]$ has $f=2$). <b>(b)</b> $P(\\xi = x_0) = 0$ for every single point.</div>\n      <div class=\"ex\"><b>Q:</b> $\\xi\\sim\\mathrm{Exp}(1)$: $P(\\xi&gt;1) = e^{-1} \\approx 0.368$, $P(1&lt;\\xi\\le2) = e^{-1}-e^{-2}\\approx 0.233$.</div>\n      <details class=\"proof\"><summary>Proof</summary><div>$F(x)=P(\\xi\\le x)=\\int_{-\\infty}^xf(u)\\,du$. By the fundamental theorem of calculus $F'(x)=f(x)$ wherever $f$ is continuous (in general at almost every $x$). Taking $B=\\mathbb R$: $\\int f=1$.</div></details>", "F6.2": "\n      <h4>F6.2 &nbsp;Discrete</h4>\n      <div class=\"eq\">$P(\\xi\\in B) = \\displaystyle\\sum_{x_i\\in B} P(\\xi = x_i)$</div>\n      <div class=\"use\"><b>Use when:</b> mass sits at isolated points. Only the listed values inside $B$ count &mdash; anything between them contributes nothing.</div>\n      <div class=\"ex\"><b>Q:</b> fair die, $P(\\xi\\in[2,4.5]) = P(2)+P(3)+P(4) = \\tfrac12$. The endpoint $4.5$ carries no mass.</div>\n      <details class=\"proof\"><summary>Proof</summary><div>$\\{\\xi\\in B\\}=\\bigsqcup_{x_i\\in B}\\{\\xi=x_i\\}$, a countable disjoint union; add the probabilities.</div></details>", "F6.3": "\n      <h4>F6.3 &nbsp;Joint Density in $\\mathbb R^n$</h4>\n      <div class=\"eq\">$P\\big((\\xi_1,\\ldots,\\xi_n)\\in B\\big) = \\displaystyle\\int\\!\\cdots\\!\\int_B f(x_1,\\ldots,x_n)\\,dx_1\\cdots dx_n$</div>\n      <div class=\"eq\">marginal: $f_{\\xi_1}(x) = \\int_{\\mathbb R} f(x,y)\\,dy$</div>\n      <div class=\"eq\">independent $\\iff f(x,y) = f_{\\xi}(x)\\,f_{\\eta}(y)$ (factorises)</div>\n      <div class=\"use\"><b>Use when:</b> independence is tested by <em>factorisation</em>, not by computing a correlation. Zero correlation does not imply independence &mdash; except inside a MVN (&sect;1.5).</div>\n      <div class=\"ex\"><b>Q:</b> unit square, $f\\equiv1$: $P(\\xi_1+\\xi_2\\le1)$ = area of the triangle = $\\tfrac12$. $P(\\xi_1=\\xi_2) = 0$ (a line has zero area).</div>\n      <details class=\"proof\"><summary>Proof</summary><div>Marginal: $P(\\xi_1\\le x)=P\\big((\\xi_1,\\xi_2)\\in(-\\infty,x]\\times\\mathbb R\\big)=\\int_{-\\infty}^{x}\\Big(\\int f(u,y)\\,dy\\Big)du$, so the inner integral is the density of $\\xi_1$.<br>Independence: if $f=f_\\xi f_\\eta$, then $P(\\xi\\in A,\\eta\\in B)=\\int_Af_\\xi\\int_Bf_\\eta=P(\\xi\\in A)P(\\eta\\in B)$. Conversely $P(\\xi\\le x,\\eta\\le y)=F_\\xi(x)F_\\eta(y)$; differentiate in $x$ and $y$.</div></details>", "F6.4": "\n      <h4>F6.4 &nbsp;Events as Regions</h4>\n      <div class=\"eq\">$\\{\\xi_1 \\le a,\\ \\xi_2 &gt; b\\} \\;\\longleftrightarrow\\; B = (-\\infty,a]\\times(b,\\infty)$</div>\n      <div class=\"use\"><b>Use when:</b> translate the worded event into a region first, <em>then</em> integrate. For a uniform density the integral is just the area.</div>\n      <div class=\"ex\"><b>Q:</b> unit square, $P(\\xi_1\\le0.3,\\ \\xi_2&gt;0.5) = 0.3\\times0.5 = \\mathbf{0.15}$.<br><b>Trick:</b> this is exactly the skill &sect;14.3 needs &mdash; a joint probability is a region, and a rectangle is four corner evaluations.</div>", "F7.1": "\n      <h4>F7.1 &nbsp;MVN Construction</h4>\n      <div class=\"eq\">$\\mathbf X = A\\mathbf Z + \\boldsymbol\\mu$, &nbsp; $\\mathbf Z \\sim N(\\mathbf 0, I_n)$ i.i.d. standard normals</div>\n      <div class=\"eq\">$E[\\mathbf X] = \\boldsymbol\\mu$, &nbsp; $\\Sigma = \\operatorname{Cov}(\\mathbf X) = A A^{\\!\\top}$</div>\n      <div class=\"use\"><b>Use when:</b> the full toolkit &mdash; linear combinations, joint MGF, conditional law &mdash; is in <b>&sect;1.5&ndash;&sect;1.9</b>. This card is the construction those rest on.</div>\n      <div class=\"ex\"><b>Q:</b> show Brownian motion is MVN. $W_1 = Z_1$ and $W_3 = W_1 + (W_3-W_1) = Z_1 + \\sqrt2 Z_2$, so<br>$A = \\begin{pmatrix}1&amp;0\\\\1&amp;\\sqrt2\\end{pmatrix}$, $\\Sigma = AA^{\\!\\top} = \\begin{pmatrix}1&amp;1\\\\1&amp;3\\end{pmatrix}$ &mdash; exactly $\\min(s,t)$ ✓</div>\n      <details class=\"proof\"><summary>Proof</summary><div>$E\\mathbf X=AE\\mathbf Z+\\boldsymbol\\mu=\\boldsymbol\\mu$. $\\operatorname{Cov}(\\mathbf X)=E[(A\\mathbf Z)(A\\mathbf Z)^{\\!\\top}]=A\\,E[\\mathbf Z\\mathbf Z^{\\!\\top}]A^{\\!\\top}=AIA^{\\!\\top}=AA^{\\!\\top}$.</div></details>", "F7.2": "\n      <h4>F7.2 &nbsp;Joint MGF Determines Everything</h4>\n      <div class=\"eq\">$\\phi_{\\mathbf X}(\\mathbf t) = E\\big[e^{\\mathbf t^{\\!\\top}\\mathbf X}\\big] = \\exp\\!\\big(\\mathbf t^{\\!\\top}\\boldsymbol\\mu + \\tfrac12\\mathbf t^{\\!\\top}\\Sigma\\,\\mathbf t\\big)$</div>\n      <div class=\"use\"><b>Use when:</b> an MVN is completely specified by $(\\boldsymbol\\mu,\\Sigma)$ &mdash; so to identify a distribution you need only match mean and covariance. That is the whole of the &sect;5.2 Wiener checklist.</div>\n      <div class=\"ex\"><b>Derivation:</b> $Y = \\mathbf t^{\\!\\top}\\mathbf X$ is scalar normal with mean $\\mathbf t^{\\!\\top}\\boldsymbol\\mu$ and variance $\\mathbf t^{\\!\\top}\\Sigma\\mathbf t$; apply $E[e^Y] = e^{\\mu_Y + \\sigma_Y^2/2}$ (&sect;1.2). Two lines.</div>\n      <details class=\"proof\"><summary>Proof</summary><div>$\\mathbf t^{\\!\\top}\\mathbf X=\\mathbf c^{\\!\\top}\\mathbf Z+\\mathbf t^{\\!\\top}\\boldsymbol\\mu$ with $\\mathbf c=A^{\\!\\top}\\mathbf t$: a sum of independent normals, so $N\\big(\\mathbf t^{\\!\\top}\\boldsymbol\\mu,\\ |\\mathbf c|^2\\big)$ and $|\\mathbf c|^2=\\mathbf t^{\\!\\top}AA^{\\!\\top}\\mathbf t=\\mathbf t^{\\!\\top}\\Sigma\\mathbf t$. Then $E[e^{Y}]=e^{m+v/2}$ for $Y\\sim N(m,v)$ (&sect;1.2). &ldquo;Determines everything&rdquo; is the MGF uniqueness theorem (quoted).</div></details>", "E1.1": "\n      <h4>E1.1 &nbsp;Integrability</h4>\n      <div class=\"eq\">$\\xi \\in L^1 \\iff E|\\xi| = \\displaystyle\\int_\\Omega |\\xi|\\,dP &lt; \\infty$</div>\n      <div class=\"eq\">then $E(\\xi) = \\displaystyle\\int_\\Omega \\xi\\,dP$</div>\n      <div class=\"use\"><b>Use when:</b> &ldquo;is $\\xi$ integrable?&rdquo; <b>Always test $E|\\xi|$, never $E\\xi$.</b> A valid distribution can still fail to be integrable.</div>\n      <div class=\"ex\"><b>Q:</b> St Petersburg, $P(\\xi = 2^k) = 2^{-k}$: $E|\\xi| = \\sum 1 = \\infty$. <b>Not integrable.</b><br><b>Q:</b> $P(\\xi = k) = c/k^2$, $c = 6/\\pi^2$: $E|\\xi| = c\\sum 1/k = \\infty$. <b>Not integrable</b>, though $P$ is a perfectly good distribution.<br><b>Trick:</b> bounded $\\Rightarrow$ integrable, free.</div>", "E1.2": "\n      <h4>E1.2 &nbsp;Why the Absolute Value</h4>\n      <div class=\"eq\">$\\xi = \\xi^{+} - \\xi^{-}$, &nbsp; $\\xi^{+} = \\max(\\xi,0)$, &nbsp; $\\xi^{-} = \\max(-\\xi,0)$</div>\n      <div class=\"eq\">$|\\xi| = \\xi^{+} + \\xi^{-}$, so $E|\\xi| &lt; \\infty \\iff$ both parts finite</div>\n      <div class=\"use\"><b>Use when:</b> if both parts are $\\infty$ then $E\\xi$ is $\\infty - \\infty$, which is <b>undefined</b> &mdash; not $0$, even when the distribution is symmetric.</div>\n      <div class=\"ex\"><b>Q:</b> $P(\\xi = 2^k) = P(\\xi = -2^k) = 2^{-k-1}$. Perfectly symmetric, yet $E\\xi^{+} = E\\xi^{-} = \\infty$ and $E\\xi$ does not exist.</div>\n      <details class=\"proof\"><summary>Proof</summary><div>$|\\xi|=\\xi^++\\xi^-$ with both parts $\\ge0$, so $E|\\xi|=E\\xi^++E\\xi^-$, finite iff both are finite.</div></details>", "E1.3": "\n      <h4>E1.3 &nbsp;Indicators</h4>\n      <div class=\"eq\">$E[\\mathbb 1_A] = P(A)$</div>\n      <div class=\"use\"><b>Use when:</b> the hinge between probability and expectation. It turns any probability into an expectation and back, and it is step one of the construction ladder.</div>\n      <div class=\"ex\"><b>Trick:</b> it needs no $\\omega$-by-$\\omega$ sum, so it works identically on a die and on $[0,1]$. That generality is the point of the Lebesgue integral.</div>\n      <details class=\"proof\"><summary>Proof</summary><div>$\\mathbf 1_A$ is the simple function with value $1$ on $A$ and $0$ off it: $E[\\mathbf 1_A]=1\\cdot P(A)+0\\cdot P(A^c)=P(A)$.</div></details>", "E1.4": "\n      <h4>E1.4 &nbsp;Simple Functions</h4>\n      <div class=\"eq\">$\\eta = \\displaystyle\\sum_{i=1}^{n}\\eta_i\\,\\mathbb 1_{A_i}$, &nbsp; $A_i$ pairwise disjoint</div>\n      <div class=\"eq\">$E(\\eta) = \\displaystyle\\sum_{i=1}^{n}\\eta_i\\,P(A_i)$</div>\n      <div class=\"use\"><b>Use when:</b> disjointness is for the <em>representation</em> to be unambiguous; linearity of the integral holds regardless. The $A_i$ need not cover $\\Omega$ &mdash; $\\eta = 0$ off their union.</div>\n      <div class=\"ex\"><b>Q:</b> die, $\\eta = 10\\cdot\\mathbb 1_{\\{1,2\\}} + 5\\cdot\\mathbb 1_{\\{3\\}}$: $E\\eta = 10(\\tfrac26) + 5(\\tfrac16) = \\tfrac{25}{6} \\approx \\mathbf{4.17}$ ✓</div>", "E1.5": "\n      <h4>E1.5 &nbsp;The Construction Ladder</h4>\n      <div class=\"eq\">indicators $\\to$ simple $\\to$ non-negative (MCT) $\\to$ general ($\\xi^{+}-\\xi^{-}$)</div>\n      <div class=\"use\"><b>Use when:</b> &ldquo;construct / define the Lebesgue integral&rdquo;. Name all four rungs and say which theorem carries you up each one &mdash; MCT for rung three.</div>\n      <div class=\"ex\"><b>Q:</b> approximate $U\\sim\\mathrm{Unif}[0,1]$ by $\\eta_n = \\lfloor 2^n U\\rfloor/2^n$. $E\\eta_n = \\tfrac12(1-2^{-n}) \\uparrow \\tfrac12 = EU$ ✓</div>", "E1.6": "\n      <h4>E1.6 &nbsp;Markov, Chebyshev, Jensen</h4>\n      <div class=\"eq\">$P(|\\xi|\\ge a) \\le \\dfrac{E|\\xi|}{a}$</div>\n      <div class=\"eq\">$P(|\\xi-\\mu|\\ge a) \\le \\dfrac{\\sigma^2}{a^2}$</div>\n      <div class=\"eq\">$h$ convex $\\Rightarrow E[h(\\xi)] \\ge h(E\\xi)$</div>\n      <div class=\"use\"><b>Use when:</b> a bound is asked instead of an exact value, or a comparison between $E[h(\\xi)]$ and $h(E\\xi)$.</div>\n      <div class=\"ex\"><b>Q:</b> $U\\sim\\mathrm{Unif}[0,1]$. $E(U^2) = \\tfrac13 &gt; \\tfrac14 = (EU)^2$ (convex ✓). $E\\sqrt U = \\tfrac23 \\approx 0.667 &lt; \\sqrt{0.5}\\approx0.707$ (concave, inequality flips).</div>\n      <details class=\"proof\"><summary>Proof</summary><div><b>Markov:</b> $a\\,\\mathbf 1_{\\{|\\xi|\\ge a\\}}\\le|\\xi|$ pointwise; take $E$.<br><b>Chebyshev:</b> Markov on $(\\xi-\\mu)^2$ with level $a^2$.<br><b>Jensen:</b> a convex $h$ lies above its tangent line at $m=E\\xi$: $h(x)\\ge h(m)+c(x-m)$. Put $x=\\xi$, take $E$: $E[h(\\xi)]\\ge h(m)+0$.</div></details>", "E2.1": "\n      <h4>E2.1 &nbsp;The Theorem</h4>\n      <div class=\"eq\">$E\\big(h(\\xi)\\big) = \\displaystyle\\int_\\Omega h(\\xi(\\omega))\\,dP(\\omega) = \\int_{\\mathbb R} h(x)\\,dP_\\xi(x)$</div>\n      <div class=\"cond\">$h$ Borel, $h(\\xi)$ integrable.</div>\n      <div class=\"use\"><b>Use when:</b> this is the theorem that lets you <b>stop working on $\\Omega$</b>. Everything downstream (densities, MGFs, the whole BM sheet) is this identity.</div>\n      <div class=\"ex\"><b>Q:</b> two tosses, $\\xi$ = #heads, $h(x)=x^2$.<br>On $\\Omega$: $\\tfrac14(4+1+1+0) = 1.5$. On $\\mathbb R$: $0(\\tfrac14)+1(\\tfrac12)+4(\\tfrac14) = 1.5$ ✓<br><b>Trick:</b> the $\\mathbb R$ version merges $HT$ and $TH$ &mdash; fewer terms, same answer.</div>\n      <details class=\"proof\"><summary>Proof</summary><div>Climb the ladder (&sect;E1.5). $h=\\mathbf 1_B$: $E[\\mathbf 1_B(\\xi)]=P(\\xi\\in B)=P_\\xi(B)=\\int\\mathbf 1_B\\,dP_\\xi$. Linearity gives simple $h$; monotone limits (MCT) give $h\\ge0$; $h=h^+-h^-$ gives general $h$.</div></details>", "E2.2": "\n      <h4>E2.2 &nbsp;The Two Working Forms</h4>\n      <div class=\"eq\">continuous: $E[h(\\xi)] = \\displaystyle\\int_{-\\infty}^{\\infty} h(x) f_\\xi(x)\\,dx$</div>\n      <div class=\"eq\">discrete: $E[h(\\xi)] = \\displaystyle\\sum_i h(x_i)\\,P(\\xi = x_i)$</div>\n      <div class=\"use\"><b>Use when:</b> every numerical expectation you will ever compute. The measure-theoretic statement collapses to one of these two lines.</div>\n      <div class=\"ex\"><b>Q:</b> $Z\\sim N(0,1)$, $E[e^Z] = \\int e^x\\tfrac{1}{\\sqrt{2\\pi}}e^{-x^2/2}dx = e^{1/2} \\approx \\mathbf{1.649}$.<br><b>Trick:</b> complete the square &mdash; that single computation is &sect;1.2 and powers all of GBM.</div>\n      <details class=\"proof\"><summary>Proof</summary><div>Continuous: $dP_\\xi=f_\\xi(x)\\,dx$, so $\\int h\\,dP_\\xi=\\int h(x)f_\\xi(x)\\,dx$. Discrete: $P_\\xi$ puts mass $P(\\xi=x_i)$ at each $x_i$, so the integral is $\\sum_ih(x_i)P(\\xi=x_i)$.</div></details>", "E2.3": "\n      <h4>E2.3 &nbsp;$E[h(\\xi)] \\ne h(E\\xi)$</h4>\n      <div class=\"eq\">apply $h$ to each value <b>before</b> averaging, never after</div>\n      <div class=\"use\"><b>Use when:</b> the single most common conceptual error in the subject. It is why GBM has an $e^{\\sigma^2 t/2}$ and why the mean of a log-normal exceeds its median.</div>\n      <div class=\"ex\"><b>Q:</b> die, $E(1/\\xi) = \\tfrac16(1+\\tfrac12+\\cdots+\\tfrac16) \\approx \\mathbf{0.408}$, whereas $1/E\\xi = 1/3.5 \\approx 0.286$. Not equal.</div>\n      <details class=\"proof\"><summary>Proof</summary><div>Counterexample: $Z\\sim N(0,1)$, $h(x)=x^2$: $E[h(Z)]=1$ but $h(EZ)=0$. For convex $h$ Jensen (&sect;E1.6) gives the direction: $E[h]\\ge h(E)$.</div></details>", "E2.4": "\n      <h4>E2.4 &nbsp;Evaluations Worth Memorizing</h4>\n      <div class=\"eq\">$E|Z| = \\sqrt{2/\\pi} \\approx 0.798$, &nbsp; $Z\\sim N(0,1)$</div>\n      <div class=\"eq\">$\\xi \\ge 0$: &nbsp; $E\\xi = \\displaystyle\\int_0^{\\infty} P(\\xi &gt; t)\\,dt$ &nbsp;(tail formula)</div>\n      <div class=\"eq\">$\\xi\\sim\\mathrm{Poisson}(\\lambda)$: &nbsp; $E[\\xi(\\xi-1)] = \\lambda^2$</div>\n      <div class=\"use\"><b>Use when:</b> the tail formula turns an awkward density integral into a survival-function integral &mdash; it is how $E[\\tau]=\\infty$ is shown in &sect;12.5.</div>\n      <div class=\"ex\"><b>Q:</b> $\\xi\\sim\\mathrm{Exp}(1)$: $E\\xi = \\int_0^\\infty e^{-t}dt = \\mathbf 1$ with no integration by parts.<br><b>Note:</b> $E|Z| = \\sqrt{2/\\pi}$ reappears as $E[M(t)] = \\sqrt{2t/\\pi}$ in &sect;13.4, because $M(t)\\overset{d}{=}|W(t)|$.</div>\n      <details class=\"proof\"><summary>Proof</summary><div>$E|Z|=2\\int_0^\\infty x\\frac{e^{-x^2/2}}{\\sqrt{2\\pi}}dx=\\frac{2}{\\sqrt{2\\pi}}\\big[-e^{-x^2/2}\\big]_0^\\infty=\\sqrt{2/\\pi}$.<br>Tail: $\\xi=\\int_0^\\infty\\mathbf 1_{\\{\\xi&gt;t\\}}dt$ for $\\xi\\ge0$; take $E$ and swap $E$ and $\\int$ (everything $\\ge0$).<br>Poisson: $\\sum_kk(k-1)e^{-\\lambda}\\frac{\\lambda^k}{k!}=\\lambda^2\\sum_{k\\ge2}e^{-\\lambda}\\frac{\\lambda^{k-2}}{(k-2)!}=\\lambda^2$.</div></details>", "E3.1": "\n      <h4>E3.1 &nbsp;Definition</h4>\n      <div class=\"eq\">$P(A\\mid B) = \\dfrac{P(A\\cap B)}{P(B)}$, &nbsp; $P(B) &gt; 0$</div>\n      <div class=\"use\"><b>Use when:</b> conditioning <em>rescales</em>: outcomes outside $B$ get probability $0$, and $B$ becomes the new sample space of total mass $1$.</div>\n      <div class=\"ex\"><b>Q:</b> die, told &ldquo;even&rdquo;. $P(\\{6\\}\\mid B) = \\frac{1/6}{1/2} = \\tfrac13$, $P(\\{\\ge4\\}\\mid B) = \\frac{2/6}{1/2} = \\tfrac23$.</div>", "E3.2": "\n      <h4>E3.2 &nbsp;Subset &amp; Disjoint Special Cases</h4>\n      <div class=\"eq\">$A\\subseteq B$: &nbsp; $P(A\\mid B) = \\dfrac{P(A)}{P(B)}$ &nbsp;<b>(not 1)</b>, &nbsp; $P(B\\mid A) = 1$</div>\n      <div class=\"eq\">$A\\cap B = \\varnothing$: &nbsp; $P(A\\mid B) = 0$</div>\n      <div class=\"use\"><b>Use when:</b> the $A\\subseteq B$ case is a standard trap &mdash; conditioning on the <em>larger</em> event makes $A$ more likely, not certain.</div>\n      <div class=\"ex\"><b>Q:</b> $A = \\{6\\} \\subseteq B = \\{2,4,6\\}$: $P(A\\mid B) = \\tfrac13$ but $P(B\\mid A) = 1$ (a six is always even).</div>\n      <details class=\"proof\"><summary>Proof</summary><div>$A\\subseteq B$: $A\\cap B=A$, so $P(A\\mid B)=P(A)/P(B)$ and $P(B\\mid A)=P(A)/P(A)=1$. Disjoint: $P(A\\cap B)=P(\\varnothing)=0$.</div></details>", "E3.3": "\n      <h4>E3.3 &nbsp;Total Probability &amp; Bayes</h4>\n      <div class=\"eq\">$P(A) = \\displaystyle\\sum_i P(A\\mid B_i)\\,P(B_i)$ &nbsp; ($\\{B_i\\}$ a partition)</div>\n      <div class=\"eq\">$P(B_j\\mid A) = \\dfrac{P(A\\mid B_j)P(B_j)}{\\sum_i P(A\\mid B_i)P(B_i)}$</div>\n      <div class=\"use\"><b>Use when:</b> the problem describes a two-stage experiment (&ldquo;pick a machine, then a part&rdquo;). <b>Write the denominator as the full sum first</b> &mdash; that is the whole method.</div>\n      <div class=\"ex\"><b>Q:</b> prevalence 1%, sensitivity 99%, false-positive rate 5%. $P(+) = .99(.01)+.05(.99) = .0594$, so $P(\\text{sick}\\mid+) = .0099/.0594 \\approx \\mathbf{0.167}$.<br><b>Trick:</b> only ~17% &mdash; the base rate dominates, because healthy people vastly outnumber sick ones.</div>\n      <details class=\"proof\"><summary>Proof</summary><div>$A=\\bigsqcup_i(A\\cap B_i)$, so $P(A)=\\sum_iP(A\\cap B_i)=\\sum_iP(A\\mid B_i)P(B_i)$. Bayes: $P(B_j\\mid A)=\\frac{P(A\\cap B_j)}{P(A)}$; write the top as $P(A\\mid B_j)P(B_j)$ and the bottom by total probability.</div></details>", "E3.4": "\n      <h4>E3.4 &nbsp;⭐ Pairwise $\\ne$ Mutual Independence</h4>\n      <div class=\"eq\">two events: $P(A\\cap B) = P(A)P(B)$</div>\n      <div class=\"eq\">$n$ events: the product rule for <b>every</b> sub-collection of size $2,\\ldots,n$</div>\n      <div class=\"eq\">that is $2^n - n - 1$ equations ($n{=}3 \\to 4$, $n{=}4 \\to 11$)</div>\n      <div class=\"use\"><b>Use when:</b> the question says &ldquo;mutually independent&rdquo;. Checking only the pairs is the intended mistake.</div>\n      <div class=\"ex\"><b>Q:</b> two dice. $A$ = first even, $B$ = second even, $C$ = sum even. All three pairs give $\\tfrac14 = \\tfrac12\\cdot\\tfrac12$ ✓, but $P(A\\cap B\\cap C) = \\tfrac14 \\ne \\tfrac18$ ✗.<br><b>Trick:</b> any two of them determine the third &mdash; that is the giveaway.</div>\n      <details class=\"proof\"><summary>Proof</summary><div>Sub-collections of size $\\ge2$: all $2^n$ subsets minus the empty one minus the $n$ singletons, $2^n-n-1$.<br>Pairwise but not mutual: two fair coins, $A$ = first is H, $B$ = second is H, $C$ = both the same. Each pair: $P=\\tfrac14=\\tfrac12\\cdot\\tfrac12$. But $P(A\\cap B\\cap C)=\\tfrac14\\ne\\tfrac18$.</div></details>", "E3.5": "\n      <h4>E3.5 &nbsp;Disjoint Is the Opposite of Independent</h4>\n      <div class=\"eq\">$P(A),P(B) &gt; 0$ and $A\\cap B = \\varnothing \\Rightarrow$ <b>dependent</b></div>\n      <div class=\"eq\">$A \\perp B \\Rightarrow A \\perp B^c$, &nbsp; $A^c \\perp B$, &nbsp; $A^c \\perp B^c$</div>\n      <div class=\"use\"><b>Use when:</b> disjoint means knowing $A$ <em>rules out</em> $B$ &mdash; maximal dependence, not independence. Students swap these two words constantly.</div>\n      <div class=\"ex\"><b>Proof of the second line:</b> $P(A\\cap B^c) = P(A)-P(A\\cap B) = P(A)(1-P(B)) = P(A)P(B^c)$ ✓ One line.</div>\n      <details class=\"proof\"><summary>Proof</summary><div>Disjoint: $P(A\\cap B)=0&lt;P(A)P(B)$. Complement: $P(A\\cap B^c)=P(A)-P(A\\cap B)=P(A)-P(A)P(B)=P(A)P(B^c)$. Apply again to get the other two.</div></details>", "E4.1": "\n      <h4>E4.1 &nbsp;It Is a Random Variable</h4>\n      <div class=\"eq\">$E(X\\mid Y{=}y)$ &mdash; a <b>number</b></div>\n      <div class=\"eq\">$E(X\\mid Y) = E(X\\mid\\sigma(Y))$ &mdash; a <b>random variable</b></div>\n      <div class=\"eq\">$E(X\\mid\\mathcal G)$ &mdash; the general version, $\\mathcal G\\subseteq\\mathcal F$</div>\n      <div class=\"use\"><b>Use when:</b> know which one is being asked for. Martingale theory is entirely about the random-variable version.</div>\n      <div class=\"ex\"><b>Q:</b> two dice, $X$ = sum, $Y$ = first die.<br>Number: $E(X\\mid Y{=}4) = 4 + 3.5 = \\mathbf{7.5}$.<br>Random variable: $E(X\\mid Y) = \\mathbf{Y + 3.5}$, which equals $7.5$ at $\\omega=(4,1)$ and $5.5$ at $\\omega=(2,6)$.</div>", "E4.2": "\n      <h4>E4.2 &nbsp;Defining Properties</h4>\n      <div class=\"eq\">(i) $E(X\\mid\\mathcal G)$ is $\\mathcal G$-measurable</div>\n      <div class=\"eq\">(ii) $\\displaystyle\\int_G E(X\\mid\\mathcal G)\\,dP = \\int_G X\\,dP \\quad \\forall\\,G\\in\\mathcal G$</div>\n      <div class=\"eq\">unique a.s.</div>\n      <div class=\"use\"><b>Use when:</b> &ldquo;define&rdquo; or &ldquo;prove&rdquo; questions. Every rule below is derived from these two, and a proof is always: check (i), then check (ii).</div>\n      <div class=\"ex\"><b>Reading it:</b> (i) says it cannot see finer detail than $\\mathcal G$ allows; (ii) says it must have the right average on every block. Together: <b>it is the block-average of $X$</b>.</div>", "E4.3": "\n      <h4>E4.3 &nbsp;⭐ Partition Formula</h4>\n      <div class=\"eq\">$\\mathcal G = \\sigma(A_1,\\ldots,A_k)$ a partition, $P(A_i)&gt;0$:</div>\n      <div class=\"eq\">$E(X\\mid\\mathcal G)(\\omega) = \\dfrac{E[X\\,\\mathbb 1_{A_i}]}{P(A_i)} = \\dfrac{\\int_{A_i}X\\,dP}{P(A_i)}$ &nbsp; for $\\omega\\in A_i$</div>\n      <div class=\"use\"><b>Use when:</b> <b>every finite or discrete problem.</b> No integration &mdash; it is the plain average of $X$ over the atom containing $\\omega$.</div>\n      <div class=\"ex\"><b>Q:</b> die, $X=\\omega$, $\\mathcal G = \\sigma(\\{1,2,3\\},\\{4,5,6\\})$. Low: $\\tfrac{1+2+3}{3} = \\mathbf 2$. High: $\\tfrac{4+5+6}{3} = \\mathbf 5$.<br><b>Trick:</b> write the atoms, average $X$ inside each, done. Derivation: put $Y = c_i$ on $A_i$ into (ii) and divide by $P(A_i)$.</div>\n      <details class=\"proof\"><summary>Proof</summary><div>$Y=\\sum_ic_i\\mathbf 1_{A_i}$ with $c_i=E[X\\mathbf 1_{A_i}]/P(A_i)$ is constant on atoms, so $\\mathcal G$-measurable. On an atom: $E[Y\\mathbf 1_{A_j}]=c_jP(A_j)=E[X\\mathbf 1_{A_j}]$. Every $G\\in\\mathcal G$ is a union of atoms; add. So $Y$ passes the defining test (&sect;E4.2), and by uniqueness $Y=E(X\\mid\\mathcal G)$.</div></details>", "E4.4": "\n      <h4>E4.4 &nbsp;Tower Property</h4>\n      <div class=\"eq\">$E\\big[E(X\\mid\\mathcal G)\\big] = E(X)$</div>\n      <div class=\"eq\">$\\mathcal H \\subseteq \\mathcal G \\Rightarrow E\\big[E(X\\mid\\mathcal G)\\mid\\mathcal H\\big] = E(X\\mid\\mathcal H)$</div>\n      <div class=\"use\"><b>Use when:</b> <b>the smaller $\\sigma$-field always wins.</b> This one line proves the multi-step martingale property (M2.3) and constant mean (M2.4).</div>\n      <div class=\"ex\"><b>Q:</b> die low/high: $\\tfrac12(2)+\\tfrac12(5) = 3.5 = EX$ ✓<br><b>Q:</b> random coin, bias $p\\sim\\mathrm{Unif}[0,1]$, $X$ = heads in 10 tosses. $E(X\\mid p) = 10p$, so $E(X) = E[10p] = \\mathbf 5$.</div>\n      <details class=\"proof\"><summary>Proof</summary><div>Let $Y=E(X\\mid\\mathcal G)$. Its defining property: $E[Y\\mathbf 1_A]=E[X\\mathbf 1_A]$ for every $A\\in\\mathcal G$.<br>\n(1) Take $A=\\Omega$: $E[Y]=E[X]$.<br>\n(2) Let $Z=E(Y\\mid\\mathcal H)$. For $A\\in\\mathcal H\\subseteq\\mathcal G$: $E[Z\\mathbf 1_A]=E[Y\\mathbf 1_A]=E[X\\mathbf 1_A]$. $Z$ is $\\mathcal H$-measurable and passes the defining test of $E(X\\mid\\mathcal H)$, so by uniqueness $Z=E(X\\mid\\mathcal H)$.</div></details>", "E4.5": "\n      <h4>E4.5 &nbsp;⭐ The Three Operational Rules</h4>\n      <div class=\"eq\">1. linearity: $E(aX+bY\\mid\\mathcal G) = aE(X\\mid\\mathcal G)+bE(Y\\mid\\mathcal G)$</div>\n      <div class=\"eq\">2. taking out what is known: $Z$ $\\mathcal G$-measurable $\\Rightarrow E(ZX\\mid\\mathcal G) = Z\\,E(X\\mid\\mathcal G)$</div>\n      <div class=\"eq\">3. independence: $X \\perp \\mathcal G \\Rightarrow E(X\\mid\\mathcal G) = E(X)$</div>\n      <div class=\"use\"><b>Use when:</b> <b>this trio solves every conditional-expectation computation on the paper</b>, including all the Brownian ones in &sect;7.6. Name the rule you are using at each step.</div>\n      <div class=\"ex\"><b>Q:</b> two dice: $E(X_1X_2\\mid X_1) = X_1E(X_2) = \\mathbf{3.5X_1}$ (rules 2 then 3).<br><b>Q:</b> $E\\big[(X_1{+}X_2)^2\\mid X_1\\big] = X_1^2 + 7X_1 + \\tfrac{91}{6}$ (expand, then 1, 2, 3).</div>\n      <details class=\"proof\"><summary>Proof</summary><div>Each time, check the candidate is $\\mathcal G$-measurable and has the right integral over every $G\\in\\mathcal G$ (&sect;E4.2).<br>1. $aE(X\\mid\\mathcal G)+bE(Y\\mid\\mathcal G)$: integrals add linearly.<br>2. $Z=\\mathbf 1_C$, $C\\in\\mathcal G$: $\\int_G\\mathbf 1_CE(X\\mid\\mathcal G)=\\int_{G\\cap C}E(X\\mid\\mathcal G)=\\int_{G\\cap C}X=\\int_G\\mathbf 1_CX$. Extend to simple, then general $Z$.<br>3. The constant $EX$ is $\\mathcal G$-measurable and $\\int_GX\\,dP=E[X\\mathbf 1_G]=EX\\cdot P(G)=\\int_GEX\\,dP$ by independence.</div></details>", "E4.6": "\n      <h4>E4.6 &nbsp;The Two Extremes</h4>\n      <div class=\"eq\">$X$ is $\\mathcal G$-measurable $\\Rightarrow E(X\\mid\\mathcal G) = X$ &nbsp;(full information)</div>\n      <div class=\"eq\">$\\mathcal G = \\{\\varnothing,\\Omega\\} \\Rightarrow E(X\\mid\\mathcal G) = E(X)$ &nbsp;(no information)</div>\n      <div class=\"use\"><b>Use when:</b> every martingale proof is these two extremes plus E4.5. Split $X_{t+1}$ into a known part (extreme 1) and an independent increment (extreme 2), and you are done.</div>\n      <div class=\"ex\"><b>Trick:</b> that split is exactly the increment trick of &sect;6.3, seen from the $\\sigma$-field side.</div>\n      <details class=\"proof\"><summary>Proof</summary><div>Full information: $X$ itself is $\\mathcal G$-measurable and trivially $\\int_GX=\\int_GX$. No information: a $\\{\\varnothing,\\Omega\\}$-measurable variable is a constant $c$; $G=\\Omega$ forces $c=EX$.</div></details>", "E4.7": "\n      <h4>E4.7 &nbsp;Conditional Jensen &amp; Variance Decomposition</h4>\n      <div class=\"eq\">$h$ convex $\\Rightarrow E[h(X)\\mid\\mathcal G] \\ge h\\big(E[X\\mid\\mathcal G]\\big)$</div>\n      <div class=\"eq\">$\\operatorname{Var}(X) = E\\big[\\operatorname{Var}(X\\mid\\mathcal G)\\big] + \\operatorname{Var}\\big(E[X\\mid\\mathcal G]\\big)$</div>\n      <div class=\"use\"><b>Use when:</b> conditional Jensen is why $|X_t|$ and $X_t^2$ are <b>sub</b>-martingales whenever $X_t$ is a martingale &mdash; a standard T/F.</div>\n      <div class=\"ex\"><b>Q:</b> $X_t$ MG $\\Rightarrow E[X_{t+1}^2\\mid\\mathcal F_t] \\ge (E[X_{t+1}\\mid\\mathcal F_t])^2 = X_t^2$. So $X_t^2$ is a sub-MG, and $X_t^2 - t$ is the correction that restores the martingale (&sect;7.2).</div>\n      <details class=\"proof\"><summary>Proof</summary><div>Jensen: a convex $h$ is the max of lines below it, $h(x)=\\sup(a+bx)$. For each line, $E[h(X)\\mid\\mathcal G]\\ge a+bE[X\\mid\\mathcal G]$; take the sup.<br>Variance: $\\operatorname{Var}(X\\mid\\mathcal G)=E[X^2\\mid\\mathcal G]-E[X\\mid\\mathcal G]^2$. So $E[\\operatorname{Var}(X\\mid\\mathcal G)]=EX^2-E\\big[E[X\\mid\\mathcal G]^2\\big]$ and $\\operatorname{Var}(E[X\\mid\\mathcal G])=E\\big[E[X\\mid\\mathcal G]^2\\big]-(EX)^2$. Add: $EX^2-(EX)^2$.</div></details>", "M1.1": "\n      <h4>M1.1 &nbsp;Filtration</h4>\n      <div class=\"eq\">$\\mathcal F_0 \\subseteq \\mathcal F_1 \\subseteq \\cdots \\subseteq \\mathcal F$</div>\n      <div class=\"eq\">$\\mathcal F_t$ = everything observable by time $t$</div>\n      <div class=\"use\"><b>Use when:</b> information only accumulates &mdash; the inclusion never reverses. That nesting is what every tower-property argument leans on.</div>\n      <div class=\"ex\"><b>Q:</b> two tosses. $\\mathcal F_0 = \\{\\varnothing,\\Omega\\}$ (2 events); $\\mathcal F_1 = \\{\\varnothing,\\{HH,HT\\},\\{TH,TT\\},\\Omega\\}$ (4); $\\mathcal F_2 = 2^\\Omega$ (16).<br><b>Note:</b> $\\{HT\\}\\notin\\mathcal F_1$ &mdash; after one toss you cannot tell $HT$ from $HH$.</div>", "M1.2": "\n      <h4>M1.2 &nbsp;Adapted</h4>\n      <div class=\"eq\">$(X_t)$ is adapted $\\iff X_t$ is $\\mathcal F_t$-measurable for every $t$</div>\n      <div class=\"eq\">atom test: $X_t$ is <b>constant on every atom of $\\mathcal F_t$</b></div>\n      <div class=\"use\"><b>Use when:</b> &ldquo;is $X_t$ adapted?&rdquo; This is F3.4 with $\\mathcal G = \\mathcal F_t$ &mdash; the same test, relabelled.</div>\n      <div class=\"ex\"><b>Q:</b> two tosses. $X_1$ = heads so far: $1,1,0,0$ on $HH,HT,TH,TT$ &mdash; constant on each atom ✓ <b>adapted</b>.<br>$Y_1$ = heads in <em>both</em> tosses: $2,1,1,0$ &mdash; differs across $HH$ and $HT$, one atom ✗ <b>not adapted</b> (it needs toss 2).</div>", "M1.3": "\n      <h4>M1.3 &nbsp;Natural Filtration</h4>\n      <div class=\"eq\">$\\mathcal F_t = \\sigma(X_0, X_1, \\ldots, X_t)$</div>\n      <div class=\"use\"><b>Use when:</b> no filtration is stated. It is the smallest one to which $X$ is adapted, so &ldquo;adapted&rdquo; becomes automatic and the real content moves to the martingale equality.</div>\n      <div class=\"ex\"><b>Trick:</b> if the question says &ldquo;let $\\mathcal F_n = \\sigma(\\eta_1,\\ldots,\\eta_n)$&rdquo;, it has handed you the natural filtration and is telling you not to waste time on condition (i).</div>", "M1.4": "\n      <h4>M1.4 &nbsp;Functions Preserve Adaptedness</h4>\n      <div class=\"eq\">$f$ Borel, $(X_t)$ adapted $\\Rightarrow (f(X_t))$ adapted</div>\n      <div class=\"use\"><b>Use when:</b> justifying that $S_t^2$, $e^{S_t}$, $|S_t|$ etc. are adapted. Same two-line pre-image proof as F4.3.</div>\n      <div class=\"ex\"><b>Trick:</b> $f$ can <em>merge</em> atoms but can never <em>split</em> one, so information can only be lost. That is the whole content.</div>\n      <details class=\"proof\"><summary>Proof</summary><div>$\\{f(X_t)\\in B\\}=\\{X_t\\in f^{-1}(B)\\}$, and $f^{-1}(B)$ is Borel, so the event is in $\\mathcal F_t$ (&sect;F4.3).</div></details>", "M1.5": "\n      <h4>M1.5 &nbsp;Increments Are NOT $\\mathcal F_t$-Measurable</h4>\n      <div class=\"eq\">$Y_t := X_{t+1} - X_t \\in \\mathcal F_{t+1}$, &nbsp;<b>not</b> $\\mathcal F_t$</div>\n      <div class=\"use\"><b>Use when:</b> this is precisely why a betting stake must be <em>predictable</em> (M7.2) and why $E[X_{t+1}\\mid\\mathcal F_t]$ is a real computation rather than a substitution.</div>\n      <div class=\"ex\"><b>Q:</b> binomial $S_0{=}100$, $u{=}1.2$, $d{=}0.8$. On the atom $A_u = \\{uu, ud\\}$ of $\\mathcal F_1$: $S_2 - S_1 = +24$ on $uu$ but $-24$ on $ud$. Not constant on the atom ✗</div>\n      <details class=\"proof\"><summary>Proof</summary><div>$Y_t=X_{t+1}-X_t$ uses $X_{t+1}$, which is in $\\mathcal F_{t+1}$. Counterexample for $\\mathcal F_t$: coin-flip walk, $Y_t$ = the $(t{+}1)$-th flip; $\\{Y_t=1\\}$ has probability $\\tfrac12$ and is independent of $\\mathcal F_t$, so it is not one of $\\mathcal F_t$&rsquo;s events (those are decided by the first $t$ flips).</div></details>", "M2.1": "\n      <h4>M2.1 &nbsp;⭐ Definition — Three Conditions</h4>\n      <div class=\"eq\">(i) $(X_t)$ adapted</div>\n      <div class=\"eq\">(ii) $E|X_t| &lt; \\infty$ &nbsp;(integrable)</div>\n      <div class=\"eq\">(iii) $E[X_{t+1}\\mid\\mathcal F_t] = X_t$ &nbsp; $P$-a.s.</div>\n      <div class=\"use\"><b>Use when:</b> &ldquo;Is $X_n$ a martingale? (Yes/No)&rdquo;. State all three, but <b>(iii) is what carries the marks</b> &mdash; show the one-line conditional computation.</div>\n      <div class=\"ex\"><b>Paper:</b> CT-2&rsquo;25 Q3 &mdash; $X_0{=}1$, $X_n = 2^nU_1\\cdots U_n$, $U_i\\sim\\mathrm{Unif}(0,1)$.<br>$E[X_{n+1}\\mid\\mathcal F_n] = 2X_n\\,E[U_{n+1}] = 2X_n\\cdot\\tfrac12 = X_n$ ✓ <b>Yes</b>.<br><b>Trick:</b> take out $X_n$ (E4.5 rule 2), then independence (rule 3). Two steps, every time.</div>", "M2.2": "\n      <h4>M2.2 &nbsp;Sub- and Super-Martingales</h4>\n      <div class=\"eq\">sub-MG: $E[X_{t+1}\\mid\\mathcal F_t] \\ge X_t$ &nbsp;(favourable to you)</div>\n      <div class=\"eq\">super-MG: $E[X_{t+1}\\mid\\mathcal F_t] \\le X_t$ &nbsp;(unfavourable)</div>\n      <div class=\"use\"><b>Use when:</b> the naming is counter-intuitive, so fix it once: <b>a SUPERmartingale goes DOWN.</b> Sub goes up.</div>\n      <div class=\"ex\"><b>Q:</b> roulette on red (18/37): $E\\eta = -\\tfrac1{37}$, a <b>super</b>-MG &mdash; the casino&rsquo;s game, and you lose about 2.7 paise per rupee staked.</div>", "M2.3": "\n      <h4>M2.3 &nbsp;Multi-Step Property</h4>\n      <div class=\"eq\">$E[X_{t+s}\\mid\\mathcal F_t] = X_t$ &nbsp; for every $s \\ge 1$</div>\n      <div class=\"use\"><b>Use when:</b> conditioning across more than one step. Proof is the tower property applied repeatedly, peeling one step at a time.</div>\n      <div class=\"ex\"><b>Proof:</b> $E[X_{t+s}\\mid\\mathcal F_t] = E\\big[E[X_{t+s}\\mid\\mathcal F_{t+s-1}]\\mid\\mathcal F_t\\big] = E[X_{t+s-1}\\mid\\mathcal F_t] = \\cdots = X_t$ ✓</div>\n      <details class=\"proof\"><summary>Proof</summary><div>Tower: $E[X_{t+s}\\mid\\mathcal F_t]=E\\big[E(X_{t+s}\\mid\\mathcal F_{t+s-1})\\mid\\mathcal F_t\\big]=E[X_{t+s-1}\\mid\\mathcal F_t]$. Repeat $s$ times to reach $X_t$.</div></details>", "M2.4": "\n      <h4>M2.4 &nbsp;⭐ Constant Mean</h4>\n      <div class=\"eq\">$E[X_t] = E[X_0]$ for every $t$</div>\n      <div class=\"use\"><b>Use when:</b> the recurring MCQ. Proof: take $E[\\cdot]$ of M2.3 and use the tower property with $\\mathcal H = \\{\\varnothing,\\Omega\\}$.</div>\n      <div class=\"ex\"><b>Paper:</b> CT-1&rsquo;26 Q8, <b>all four sets</b> &mdash; &ldquo;Which statement is TRUE?&rdquo; The correct option is always worded &ldquo;<b>the mean of a martingale process is constant with respect to time</b>&rdquo;; only its letter moves: Set-1 <b>(c)</b>, Set-2 <b>(a)</b>, Set-3 <b>(d)</b>, Set-4 <b>(b)</b>.<br><b>Trick:</b> don&rsquo;t read the distractors &mdash; find that sentence.</div>\n      <details class=\"proof\"><summary>Proof</summary><div>$E[X_{t+1}] = E\\big[E(X_{t+1}\\mid\\mathcal F_t)\\big] = E[X_t]$ (tower, then the MG property). Repeat: $E[X_t]=E[X_{t-1}]=\\dots=E[X_0]$.</div></details>", "M2.5": "\n      <h4>M2.5 &nbsp;The Standard Examples</h4>\n      <div class=\"eq\">$S_n = \\sum_{i\\le n} Y_i$, $\\;E[Y]=0$ &nbsp;&rarr;&nbsp; MG</div>\n      <div class=\"eq\">$S_n - n\\,E[Y]$ &nbsp;&rarr;&nbsp; MG for any i.i.d. $Y$ (drift removed)</div>\n      <div class=\"eq\">$S_n^2 - n\\operatorname{Var}(Y)$ &nbsp;&rarr;&nbsp; MG when $E[Y]=0$</div>\n      <div class=\"eq\">$\\prod_{i\\le n} Y_i$, $\\;E[Y]=1$ &nbsp;&rarr;&nbsp; MG</div>\n      <div class=\"use\"><b>Use when:</b> the first and last are &sect;2.1 / &sect;2.2; the middle two are the discrete twins of $W_t$ and $W_t^2 - t$ (&sect;7.1, &sect;7.2).</div>\n      <div class=\"ex\"><b>Q:</b> biased walk $p=0.6$: $E[Y] = 0.2$, so $S_n$ is <b>not</b> a MG &mdash; but $S_n - 0.2n$ <b>is</b>.<br><b>Trick:</b> if it is not a martingale, subtract the drift and it becomes one. That is the whole idea behind the $-t$ in $W_t^2-t$.</div>\n      <details class=\"proof\"><summary>Proof</summary><div>$\\mathcal F_n=\\sigma(Y_1..Y_n)$; $Y_{n+1}$ is independent of $\\mathcal F_n$, so $E[Y_{n+1}\\mid\\mathcal F_n]=EY$ (&sect;E4.5).<br>1&ndash;2. $E[S_{n+1}\\mid\\mathcal F_n]=S_n+EY$; subtract $(n+1)EY$ and you get $S_n-nEY$.<br>3. $S_{n+1}^2=S_n^2+2S_nY_{n+1}+Y_{n+1}^2$. Conditional mean: $S_n^2+2S_n\\cdot0+\\operatorname{Var}Y$. So $S_{n+1}^2-(n+1)\\operatorname{Var}Y\\mapsto S_n^2-n\\operatorname{Var}Y$.<br>4. $\\Pi_{n+1}=\\Pi_nY_{n+1}$; take out $\\Pi_n$: $\\Pi_n\\,EY=\\Pi_n$.<br>Integrability needs $E|Y|&lt;\\infty$ ($EY^2&lt;\\infty$ for 3).</div></details>", "M3.1": "\n      <h4>M3.1 &nbsp;The Tree</h4>\n      <div class=\"eq\">$S_t = v_t S_{t-1}$, &nbsp; $v_t \\in \\{u,d\\}$, &nbsp; $0 &lt; d &lt; u$</div>\n      <div class=\"eq\">$\\Omega = \\{u,d\\}^T$; &nbsp; after $k$ up-moves: $S_t = S_0\\,u^{k}d^{\\,t-k}$</div>\n      <div class=\"use\"><b>Use when:</b> draw the tree with numbers before anything else. Almost every error in this topic is a mis-drawn tree, not a mis-applied formula.</div>\n      <div class=\"ex\"><b>Q:</b> $S_0{=}100$, $u{=}1.2$, $d{=}0.8$, $T{=}2$: $S_2 \\in \\{144, 96, 96, 64\\}$ on $uu,ud,du,dd$.</div>", "M3.2": "\n      <h4>M3.2 &nbsp;Filtration &amp; Recombination</h4>\n      <div class=\"eq\">$\\mathcal F_t = \\sigma(S_0,\\ldots,S_t)$; &nbsp; $2^t$ atoms at time $t$</div>\n      <div class=\"use\"><b>Use when:</b> the tree <em>recombines</em> ($ud$ and $du$ give the same price) but the <em>filtration does not</em> &mdash; $\\mathcal F_t$ records the whole path, not just the current price.</div>\n      <div class=\"ex\"><b>Q:</b> above, $S_2 = 96$ on both $ud$ and $du$. $\\sigma(S_2)$ alone cannot separate them; $\\mathcal F_2 = \\sigma(S_0,S_1,S_2)$ can, because $S_1$ differs ($120$ vs $80$).<br><b>Trick:</b> that distinction is exactly why path-dependent options exist.</div>\n      <details class=\"proof\"><summary>Proof</summary><div>Each time-$t$ history is a word of $t$ letters from $\\{u,d\\}$; since $u\\ne d$ the price path tells you the word, so all $2^t$ histories are distinguishable: $2^t$ atoms. (The <em>prices</em> recombine into $t+1$ values, but $\\mathcal F_t$ remembers the path.)</div></details>", "M3.3": "\n      <h4>M3.3 &nbsp;Martingale Condition for the Stock ($r = 0$)</h4>\n      <div class=\"eq\">$E[S_{t+1}\\mid\\mathcal F_t] = S_t\\big(pu + (1-p)d\\big)$</div>\n      <div class=\"eq\">MG $\\iff pu+(1-p)d = 1 \\iff p = \\dfrac{1-d}{u-d}$</div>\n      <div class=\"use\"><b>Use when:</b> &ldquo;for which $p$ is $S_t$ a martingale?&rdquo; Larger $p$ &rarr; sub-MG; smaller &rarr; super-MG.</div>\n      <div class=\"ex\"><b>Q:</b> $u{=}1.2,d{=}0.8$: $p = \\tfrac{0.2}{0.4} = \\mathbf{1/2}$. &nbsp; $u{=}2,d{=}0.5$: $p = \\tfrac{0.5}{1.5} = \\mathbf{1/3}$.<br><b>Check:</b> $\\tfrac13(2)+\\tfrac23(0.5) = 1$ ✓</div>\n      <details class=\"proof\"><summary>Proof</summary><div>$S_{t+1}=v_{t+1}S_t$ with $S_t$ known and $v_{t+1}$ independent of $\\mathcal F_t$: $E[S_{t+1}\\mid\\mathcal F_t]=S_tE[v_{t+1}]=S_t(pu+(1-p)d)$. Equals $S_t$ iff $pu+(1-p)d=1$ iff $p(u-d)=1-d$.</div></details>", "M3.4": "\n      <h4>M3.4 &nbsp;⭐ Risk-Neutral Probability (with interest $r$)</h4>\n      <div class=\"eq\">$p^{*} = \\dfrac{1+r-d}{u-d}$</div>\n      <div class=\"eq\">$\\dfrac{S_t}{(1+r)^t}$ is a martingale under $p^{*}$</div>\n      <div class=\"use\"><b>Use when:</b> an interest rate is given. <b>$p^{*}$ has nothing to do with the real probability</b> &mdash; the real $p$ never enters a price. With $r=0$ this reduces to M3.3.</div>\n      <div class=\"ex\"><b>Paper:</b> CT-2&rsquo;25 Q1 &mdash; $u{=}1.25$, $d{=}0.25$, $r{=}0.15$: $p^{*} = \\tfrac{1.15-0.25}{1.00} = \\mathbf{0.9}$.</div>\n      <details class=\"proof\"><summary>Proof</summary><div>We need $E^{*}\\!\\Big[\\dfrac{S_{t+1}}{(1+r)^{t+1}}\\,\\Big|\\,\\mathcal F_t\\Big]=\\dfrac{S_t}{(1+r)^t}$.<br>\nLeft side $=\\dfrac{S_t\\,\\big(p\\,u+(1-p)\\,d\\big)}{(1+r)^{t+1}}$, since $S_{t+1}$ is $uS_t$ or $dS_t$.<br>\nEqual to the right side $\\iff p\\,u+(1-p)\\,d = 1+r \\iff p=\\dfrac{1+r-d}{u-d}$. This is in $(0,1)$ exactly when $d&lt;1+r&lt;u$.</div></details>", "M3.5": "\n      <h4>M3.5 &nbsp;Growth of the Mean</h4>\n      <div class=\"eq\">$E[S_t] = S_0\\big(pu+(1-p)d\\big)^{t}$</div>\n      <div class=\"use\"><b>Use when:</b> &ldquo;find $E[S_3]$&rdquo;. Compute $E[v] = pu+(1-p)d$ once, then raise it to the power $t$. Never expand the tree.</div>\n      <div class=\"ex\"><b>Q:</b> $S_0{=}50$, $u{=}1.1$, $d{=}0.9$. $p{=}0.5$: $E[v]=1$, MG, $E[S_3] = \\mathbf{50}$. &nbsp; $p{=}0.7$: $E[v]=1.04$, $E[S_3] = 50(1.04)^3 = \\mathbf{56.24}$.</div>\n      <details class=\"proof\"><summary>Proof</summary><div>$E[S_t]=E\\big[E(S_t\\mid\\mathcal F_{t-1})\\big]=(pu+(1-p)d)\\,E[S_{t-1}]$ by &sect;M3.3; iterate $t$ times.</div></details>", "M4.1": "\n      <h4>M4.1 &nbsp;No-Arbitrage Condition</h4>\n      <div class=\"eq\">$d &lt; 1 + r &lt; u$</div>\n      <div class=\"use\"><b>Use when:</b> &ldquo;show the market is arbitrage-free&rdquo;. If $1+r \\le d$ the stock beats the bond for sure; if $1+r \\ge u$ the bond beats the stock for sure. Equivalent to $0 &lt; p^{*} &lt; 1$.</div>\n      <div class=\"ex\"><b>Q:</b> Endsem&rsquo;25 Q5(b)(i) &mdash; $S_0{=}5$, $S_1\\in\\{6, 40/9\\}$, $r{=}1/9$. Then $u = 6/5$, $d = 8/9$, $1+r = 10/9$. Check $\\tfrac89 &lt; \\tfrac{10}9 &lt; \\tfrac65$ ✓</div>\n      <details class=\"proof\"><summary>Proof</summary><div>If $1+r\\le d$: borrow $S_0$, buy the stock. At time 1 you hold $\\ge dS_0\\ge(1+r)S_0$ = what you owe, with strict gain in the up state: arbitrage.<br>If $1+r\\ge u$: short the stock, lend $S_0$. You receive $(1+r)S_0\\ge uS_0\\ge S_1$, strict in the down state: arbitrage.<br>If $d&lt;1+r&lt;u$: $p^{*}\\in(0,1)$ is an EMM, so no arbitrage (&sect;D3.3).</div></details>", "M4.2": "\n      <h4>M4.2 &nbsp;Risk-Neutral Pricing Formula</h4>\n      <div class=\"eq\">$V_0 = \\dfrac{1}{(1+r)^{T}}\\,E^{*}\\big[\\text{payoff}\\big]$</div>\n      <div class=\"eq\">one period: $V_0 = \\dfrac{p^{*}V_u + (1-p^{*})V_d}{1+r}$</div>\n      <div class=\"use\"><b>Use when:</b> pricing anything. Multi-period: work <b>backwards</b> node by node, discounting one step at a time.</div>\n      <div class=\"ex\"><b>Q:</b> CT-2&rsquo;25, put with $K{=}225$, $S_0{=}200$, $u{=}1.25$, $d{=}0.25$, $r{=}0.15$.<br>$V_u = (225-250)^+ = 0$, $V_d = (225-50)^+ = 175$, $p^{*}=0.9$.<br>$V_0 = \\tfrac{0.9(0)+0.1(175)}{1.15} = \\tfrac{17.5}{1.15} = \\mathbf{15.22}$ ✓</div>\n      <details class=\"proof\"><summary>Proof</summary><div>One period: the replicating portfolio costs $\\frac{p^{*}V_u+(1-p^{*})V_d}{1+r}$ (&sect;M4.4), and any other price is an arbitrage (&sect;A1.5 proof). Many periods: repeat at every node, backwards (&sect;D4.2); the tower property collapses the nested averages to $(1+r)^{-T}E^{*}[\\text{payoff}]$.</div></details>", "M4.3": "\n      <h4>M4.3 &nbsp;⭐ Replicating Portfolio</h4>\n      <div class=\"eq\">$\\Delta = \\dfrac{V_u - V_d}{(u-d)\\,S_0}$ &nbsp;(shares)</div>\n      <div class=\"eq\">$B = \\dfrac{u\\,V_d - d\\,V_u}{(1+r)(u-d)}$ &nbsp;(cash / bond)</div>\n      <div class=\"eq\">$V_0 = \\Delta S_0 + B$</div>\n      <div class=\"use\"><b>Use when:</b> &ldquo;construct the replicating portfolio, of the form $(\\Delta, B)$&rdquo;. $\\Delta$ is the hedge ratio; it is negative for a put.</div>\n      <div class=\"ex\"><b>Paper:</b> CT-2&rsquo;25 Q1 &mdash; put, $V_u{=}0$, $V_d{=}175$, $u-d = 1$, $S_0{=}200$, $r{=}0.15$.<br>$\\Delta = \\tfrac{0-175}{1\\cdot200} = \\mathbf{-0.875}$; &nbsp; $B = \\tfrac{1.25(175)-0.25(0)}{1.15(1)} = \\tfrac{218.75}{1.15} = \\mathbf{190.22}$.<br>Answer $(\\Delta,B) = (-0.875,\\,190.22)$ ✓ and $V_0 = -0.875(200)+190.22 = \\mathbf{15.22}$ ✓</div>\n      <details class=\"proof\"><summary>Proof</summary><div>Match both states: $\\Delta uS_0+B(1+r)=V_u$ and $\\Delta dS_0+B(1+r)=V_d$. Subtract: $\\Delta(u-d)S_0=V_u-V_d$. Then $B(1+r)=V_u-u\\frac{V_u-V_d}{u-d}=\\frac{uV_d-dV_u}{u-d}$.</div></details>", "M4.4": "\n      <h4>M4.4 &nbsp;Two Routes, One Answer</h4>\n      <div class=\"eq\">$\\Delta S_0 + B \\;=\\; \\dfrac{p^{*}V_u + (1-p^{*})V_d}{1+r}$</div>\n      <div class=\"use\"><b>Use when:</b> they are algebraically the same. <b>Use replication when the question asks for $(\\Delta,B)$; use risk-neutral when it asks only for the price</b> &mdash; and use the other one to check.</div>\n      <div class=\"ex\"><b>Trick:</b> in a two-part question (&ldquo;price it&rdquo;, then &ldquo;replicate it&rdquo;) do replication first &mdash; you get the price for free from $V_0 = \\Delta S_0 + B$.</div>\n      <details class=\"proof\"><summary>Proof</summary><div>$\\Delta S_0+B=\\frac{V_u-V_d}{u-d}+\\frac{uV_d-dV_u}{(1+r)(u-d)}=\\frac{(1+r-d)V_u+(u-1-r)V_d}{(1+r)(u-d)}=\\frac{p^{*}V_u+(1-p^{*})V_d}{1+r}$, using $p^{*}=\\frac{1+r-d}{u-d}$, $1-p^{*}=\\frac{u-1-r}{u-d}$.</div></details>", "M4.5": "\n      <h4>M4.5 &nbsp;⭐ The &ldquo;no sure win&rdquo; / arbitrage-$c$ question</h4>\n      <div class=\"eq\">$S_0$ today; at time 1 either $S_u$ or $S_d$ (already present values)</div>\n      <div class=\"eq\">option to buy $y$ shares at price $Ky$ costs $cy$</div>\n      <div class=\"eq\">1. $\\;p = \\dfrac{S_0 - S_d}{S_u - S_d}$</div>\n      <div class=\"eq\">2. payoffs $(S_u-K)^{+}$ and $(S_d-K)^{+}$</div>\n      <div class=\"eq\">3. $\\;c^{*} = p\\,(S_u-K)^{+} + (1-p)(S_d-K)^{+}$</div>\n      <div class=\"eq\">no sure win $\\iff c = c^{*}$; &nbsp; arbitrage $\\iff c \\ne c^{*}$</div>\n      <div class=\"use\"><b>Use when:</b> this exact question has appeared in <b>three</b> consecutive papers. Prices are given as present values, so <b>there is no discounting</b> &mdash; three lines, no $r$.</div>\n      <div class=\"ex\"><b>Paper — all three instances:</b><br>Midsem&rsquo;25 Q1(c): $50\\to\\{25,100\\}$, $K{=}85$. $p = \\tfrac{25}{75}=\\tfrac13$, payoff $15$ or $0$, $c^{*}=\\mathbf 5$. Key: &ldquo;$c \\ne 5$&rdquo; ✓<br>CT-2&rsquo;25 Q5: $40\\to\\{25,100\\}$, $K{=}75$. $p = \\tfrac{15}{75}=\\tfrac15$, payoff $25$ or $0$, $c = \\mathbf 5$ ✓<br>Midsem&rsquo;24 Q1(a): $75\\to\\{50,200\\}$, $K{=}120$. $p = \\tfrac{25}{150}=\\tfrac16$, payoff $80$ or $0$, $c = \\tfrac{80}{6} = \\mathbf{13.33}$</div>\n      <details class=\"proof\"><summary>Proof</summary><div>Step 1 makes the stock fair: $pS_u+(1-p)S_d=S_0$. Any position ($x$ shares, $y$ options) then has $p$-expected gain $x\\cdot0+y(c^{*}-c)$.<br>If $c=c^{*}$: every position has expected gain $0$ with $p\\in(0,1)$, so it cannot be positive in both states: no sure win.<br>If $c\\ne c^{*}$: choose $x$ so the gain is the same in both states (&sect;A1.2). A constant equals its own expectation $y(c^{*}-c)$; pick the sign of $y$ to make it positive: sure win.</div></details>", "M4.6": "\n      <h4>M4.6 &nbsp;Put&ndash;Call Parity</h4>\n      <div class=\"eq\">$C - P = S_0 - \\dfrac{K}{(1+r)^{T}}$</div>\n      <div class=\"cond\">Same strike, same maturity, European.</div>\n      <div class=\"use\"><b>Use when:</b> the paper asks for both a call and a put on the same tree (Endsem&rsquo;25 Q4 does). Price one properly, get the other in one line instead of rebuilding the tree.</div>\n      <div class=\"ex\"><b>Proof in one line:</b> the portfolios &ldquo;call + cash $K/(1+r)^T$&rdquo; and &ldquo;put + one share&rdquo; have identical payoff $\\max(S_T,K)$ at $T$, so they must cost the same today.</div>\n      <details class=\"proof\"><summary>Proof</summary><div>Hold: 1 share + 1 put &minus; 1 call. At $T$ it is worth $S_T+(K-S_T)^+-(S_T-K)^+$.<br>\nIf $S_T\\ge K$: $S_T+0-(S_T-K)=K$. If $S_T&lt;K$: $S_T+(K-S_T)-0=K$. So it pays $K$ for sure.<br>\nA sure $K$ at $T$ costs $K/(1+r)^T$ today (otherwise buy the cheaper of the two and sell the dearer: arbitrage). Hence $S_0+P-C=K/(1+r)^T$.</div></details>", "M4.7": "\n      <h4>M4.7 &nbsp;Self-Financing</h4>\n      <div class=\"eq\">$\\Delta_t S_{t+1} + B_t(1+r) \\;=\\; \\Delta_{t+1}S_{t+1} + B_{t+1}$</div>\n      <div class=\"use\"><b>Use when:</b> &ldquo;verify the replicating strategy is self-financing&rdquo;. Meaning: at each rebalancing date the new portfolio costs exactly what the old one is now worth &mdash; no money in, none out.</div>\n      <div class=\"ex\"><b>Paper:</b> Endsem&rsquo;25 Q4(iii).<br><b>Trick:</b> compute the old portfolio&rsquo;s value at the new prices, then the new portfolio&rsquo;s cost, and show they match at <em>every</em> node.</div>", "M5.1": "\n      <h4>M5.1 &nbsp;Definition</h4>\n      <div class=\"eq\">$\\tau : \\Omega \\to \\{0,1,2,\\ldots\\}\\cup\\{\\infty\\}$ with</div>\n      <div class=\"eq\">$\\{\\tau \\le t\\} \\in \\mathcal F_t$ &nbsp; for every $t$</div>\n      <div class=\"use\"><b>Use when:</b> plain meaning: at time $t$ you can already tell whether you have stopped, using only what you have seen. <b>No peeking at the future.</b> $\\infty$ is allowed &mdash; the event may never happen.</div>\n      <div class=\"ex\"><b>Q:</b> three tosses, $\\tau$ = first H. $\\{\\tau\\le1\\} = H\\_\\_ \\in\\mathcal F_1$ ✓, $\\{\\tau\\le2\\} = H\\_\\_\\cup TH\\_ \\in\\mathcal F_2$ ✓ <b>Yes</b>.<br><b>Q:</b> $\\tau = 1$ if toss 2 is H else $2$: $\\{\\tau\\le1\\} = \\{\\_H\\_\\}\\notin\\mathcal F_1$ ✗ <b>No</b>.</div>", "M5.2": "\n      <h4>M5.2 &nbsp;Equivalent Characterisation</h4>\n      <div class=\"eq\">$\\{\\tau\\le t\\}\\in\\mathcal F_t\\ \\forall t \\iff \\{\\tau = t\\}\\in\\mathcal F_t\\ \\forall t$</div>\n      <div class=\"use\"><b>Use when:</b> $\\{\\tau = t\\}$ is almost always the easier one to write down. Prove the equivalence with the two identities below &mdash; it is a standard 3-mark part.</div>\n      <div class=\"ex\"><b>($\\Rightarrow$)</b> $\\{\\tau=t\\} = \\{\\tau\\le t\\}\\cap\\{\\tau\\le t-1\\}^{c}$, both in $\\mathcal F_t$ by nesting.<br><b>($\\Leftarrow$)</b> $\\{\\tau\\le t\\} = \\bigcup_{s=0}^{t}\\{\\tau=s\\}$, a finite union of $\\mathcal F_t$-sets ✓</div>\n      <details class=\"proof\"><summary>Proof</summary><div>(&rArr;) $\\{\\tau=t\\}=\\{\\tau\\le t\\}\\setminus\\{\\tau\\le t-1\\}$, and $\\{\\tau\\le t-1\\}\\in\\mathcal F_{t-1}\\subseteq\\mathcal F_t$.<br>(&lArr;) $\\{\\tau\\le t\\}=\\bigcup_{k\\le t}\\{\\tau=k\\}$, each in $\\mathcal F_k\\subseteq\\mathcal F_t$.</div></details>", "M5.3": "\n      <h4>M5.3 &nbsp;First Entry Time Is a Stopping Time</h4>\n      <div class=\"eq\">$\\tau = \\min\\{n : X_n \\in B\\}$ &nbsp; is a ST for adapted $(X_n)$</div>\n      <div class=\"eq\">$\\{\\tau = n\\} = \\{X_1\\notin B\\}\\cap\\cdots\\cap\\{X_{n-1}\\notin B\\}\\cap\\{X_n\\in B\\}$</div>\n      <div class=\"use\"><b>Use when:</b> covers every $\\tau$ on this paper &mdash; first hit of a level, first hit of a moving line, ruin time. Each piece of the intersection sits in $\\mathcal F_n$ by adaptedness plus nesting.</div>\n      <div class=\"ex\"><b>Q:</b> walk with steps $+1,-1,+1,+1$, $B=\\{2\\}$: $X = 1,0,1,2$ so $\\tau = \\mathbf 4$. At time 4 you check only $X_1,\\ldots,X_4$ ✓<br>Steps $-1,-1,-1,\\ldots$ never reach 2, so $\\tau = \\infty$ &mdash; which is why $\\infty$ is in the codomain.</div>\n      <details class=\"proof\"><summary>Proof</summary><div>Each $\\{X_k\\notin B\\}\\in\\mathcal F_k\\subseteq\\mathcal F_n$ (adapted) and $\\{X_n\\in B\\}\\in\\mathcal F_n$, so the intersection $\\{\\tau=n\\}\\in\\mathcal F_n$. Done by &sect;M5.2.</div></details>", "M5.4": "\n      <h4>M5.4 &nbsp;⭐ Last Exit Time Is NOT a Stopping Time</h4>\n      <div class=\"eq\">$\\tau = \\max\\{n : X_n\\in B\\}$ &nbsp; is <b>not</b> a ST</div>\n      <div class=\"use\"><b>Use when:</b> the standard counterexample question. Also not stopping times: &ldquo;the time at which $X_n$ attains its maximum over $n\\le N$&rdquo;, &ldquo;the day before the crash&rdquo;. All need the future.</div>\n      <div class=\"ex\"><b>Q:</b> three-step walk, $\\tau$ = last $n\\le3$ with $X_n = 1$.<br>Steps $(+1,-1,-1)$: $X = 1,0,-1$, so $\\tau = 1$. &nbsp; Steps $(+1,-1,+1)$: $X = 1,0,1$, so $\\tau = 3$.<br>Both begin $+1$, so they lie in the <b>same atom of $\\mathcal F_1$</b>, yet $\\tau$ differs $\\Rightarrow \\{\\tau=1\\}\\notin\\mathcal F_1$ ✗<br><b>Trick:</b> exhibit two paths agreeing up to time $t$ with different $\\tau$. That is a complete proof.</div>\n      <details class=\"proof\"><summary>Proof</summary><div>Counterexample: two coin flips, $X_n$ = number of heads after $n$ flips, $B=\\{0\\}$, $\\tau$ = last $n\\le2$ with $X_n=0$. Then $\\{\\tau=0\\}=\\{$first flip is H$\\}$, which is not in $\\mathcal F_0=\\{\\varnothing,\\Omega\\}$: at time 0 you cannot know it.</div></details>", "M5.5": "\n      <h4>M5.5 &nbsp;Closure Operations</h4>\n      <div class=\"eq\">$\\tau_1\\wedge\\tau_2$ ✓ &nbsp; $\\tau_1\\vee\\tau_2$ ✓ &nbsp; $\\tau+1$ ✓ &nbsp; $\\tau\\wedge c$ ✓</div>\n      <div class=\"eq\">$\\tau - 1$ ✗ &nbsp;(stopping one step <em>before</em> something you cannot yet see)</div>\n      <div class=\"use\"><b>Use when:</b> $\\tau\\wedge c$ is the workhorse &mdash; it is how you manufacture the <b>bounded</b> stopping time that the Optional Stopping Theorem requires (M6.3).</div>\n      <div class=\"ex\"><b>Proofs, one line each:</b> $\\{\\tau_1\\wedge\\tau_2\\le t\\} = \\{\\tau_1\\le t\\}\\cup\\{\\tau_2\\le t\\}$; &nbsp; $\\{\\tau_1\\vee\\tau_2\\le t\\} = \\{\\tau_1\\le t\\}\\cap\\{\\tau_2\\le t\\}$; &nbsp; $\\{\\tau+1\\le t\\} = \\{\\tau\\le t-1\\}\\in\\mathcal F_{t-1}\\subseteq\\mathcal F_t$.</div>\n      <details class=\"proof\"><summary>Proof</summary><div>$\\{\\tau_1\\wedge\\tau_2\\le t\\}=\\{\\tau_1\\le t\\}\\cup\\{\\tau_2\\le t\\}$; $\\{\\tau_1\\vee\\tau_2\\le t\\}=\\{\\tau_1\\le t\\}\\cap\\{\\tau_2\\le t\\}$; $\\{\\tau+1\\le t\\}=\\{\\tau\\le t-1\\}\\in\\mathcal F_{t-1}\\subseteq\\mathcal F_t$; $\\{\\tau\\wedge c\\le t\\}$ is $\\Omega$ if $t\\ge c$, else $\\{\\tau\\le t\\}$.<br>$\\tau-1$ fails: $\\{\\tau-1\\le t\\}=\\{\\tau\\le t+1\\}$ needs $\\mathcal F_{t+1}$. E.g. $\\tau$ = first head: $\\{\\tau-1=0\\}=\\{$flip 1 is H$\\}\\notin\\mathcal F_0$.</div></details>", "M6.1": "\n      <h4>M6.1 &nbsp;The Stopped Process</h4>\n      <div class=\"eq\">$Z_t = X_{t\\wedge\\tau} = \\begin{cases} X_t & \\tau &gt; t \\ \\text{(running)}\\\\ X_\\tau & \\tau \\le t \\ \\text{(frozen)}\\end{cases}$</div>\n      <div class=\"eq\">$Z_{t+1} = \\displaystyle\\sum_{k=0}^{t} X_k\\mathbb 1_{\\{\\tau=k\\}} + X_{t+1}\\mathbb 1_{\\{\\tau&gt;t\\}}$</div>\n      <div class=\"use\"><b>Use when:</b> the explicit formula is the one you differentiate the proof from &mdash; every term in it is $\\mathcal F_t$-measurable except $X_{t+1}$.</div>\n      <div class=\"ex\"><b>Q:</b> steps $+1,+1,-1,+1$ so $X = 0,1,2,1,2$; $\\tau$ = first hit of 2, so $\\tau=2$.<br>$Z = 0,1,2,2,2$. After $\\tau$, $Z$ ignores $X$: $X_3 = 1$ but $Z_3 = 2$.</div>\n      <details class=\"proof\"><summary>Proof</summary><div>If $\\tau=k\\le t$, the process froze at $X_k$: only the term $X_k\\mathbf 1_{\\{\\tau=k\\}}$ survives. If $\\tau&gt;t$ it is still running and equals $X_{t+1}$. Exactly one indicator is $1$.</div></details>", "M6.2": "\n      <h4>M6.2 &nbsp;Preservation Theorem</h4>\n      <div class=\"eq\">$(X_t)$ MG $\\Longrightarrow (X_{t\\wedge\\tau})$ MG</div>\n      <div class=\"eq\">sub-MG $\\to$ sub-MG; &nbsp; super-MG $\\to$ super-MG &nbsp;(type is inherited)</div>\n      <div class=\"use\"><b>Use when:</b> the key step is $\\mathbb 1_{\\{\\tau&gt;t\\}} \\in \\mathcal F_t$, which holds because $\\{\\tau\\le t\\}\\in\\mathcal F_t$ &mdash; <b>this is the only place the stopping-time property is used</b>. Say that sentence.</div>\n      <div class=\"ex\"><b>Proof:</b> pull the $\\mathcal F_t$-measurable pieces out of M6.1, then $E[X_{t+1}\\mid\\mathcal F_t] = X_t$ gives<br>$\\sum_{k\\le t}X_k\\mathbb 1_{\\{\\tau=k\\}} + \\mathbb 1_{\\{\\tau&gt;t\\}}X_t = Z_t$ ✓</div>\n      <details class=\"proof\"><summary>Proof</summary><div>The stopped process moves only while running: $Z_{t+1}-Z_t=\\mathbf 1_{\\{\\tau&gt;t\\}}(X_{t+1}-X_t)$, and $\\{\\tau&gt;t\\}\\in\\mathcal F_t$. So $E[Z_{t+1}-Z_t\\mid\\mathcal F_t]=\\mathbf 1_{\\{\\tau&gt;t\\}}E[X_{t+1}-X_t\\mid\\mathcal F_t]$: $=0$ for a MG, $\\ge0$ for a sub-MG, $\\le0$ for a super-MG (the indicator is $\\ge0$). Integrable: $|Z_t|\\le|X_0|+\\dots+|X_t|$.</div></details>", "M6.3": "\n      <h4>M6.3 &nbsp;⭐ Optional Stopping Theorem</h4>\n      <div class=\"eq\">$(X_t)$ MG, $\\tau$ a <b>bounded</b> ST ($\\tau\\le c$ a.s.) $\\Longrightarrow E[X_\\tau] = E[X_0]$</div>\n      <div class=\"use\"><b>Use when:</b> <b>no stopping rule beats a fair game.</b> Its power on this paper: it converts an unknown distribution of $\\tau$ into one line of algebra.</div>\n      <div class=\"ex\"><b>Four-line proof:</b> 1. $Z_t = X_{t\\wedge\\tau}$ is a MG (M6.2). 2. So $E[Z_t] = E[Z_0] = E[X_0]$. 3. Put $t = c$; since $\\tau\\le c$, $Z_c = X_\\tau$. 4. Hence $E[X_\\tau] = E[X_0]$ ∎</div>\n      <details class=\"proof\"><summary>Proof</summary><div>Write the stopped value as a sum of steps: $X_\\tau = X_0+\\sum_{k=1}^{c}\\mathbf 1_{\\{\\tau\\ge k\\}}(X_k-X_{k-1})$.<br>\n$\\{\\tau\\ge k\\}=\\{\\tau\\le k-1\\}^c\\in\\mathcal F_{k-1}$, so it is known one step early:<br>\n$E\\big[\\mathbf 1_{\\{\\tau\\ge k\\}}(X_k-X_{k-1})\\big]=E\\big[\\mathbf 1_{\\{\\tau\\ge k\\}}\\,E(X_k-X_{k-1}\\mid\\mathcal F_{k-1})\\big]=E[\\mathbf 1_{\\{\\tau\\ge k\\}}\\cdot 0]=0.$<br>\nSum the $c$ terms: $E[X_\\tau]=E[X_0]$.</div></details>", "M6.4": "\n      <h4>M6.4 &nbsp;⚠ When OST Fails</h4>\n      <div class=\"eq\">boundedness of $\\tau$ is <b>essential</b></div>\n      <div class=\"use\"><b>Use when:</b> if your algebra returns something impossible &mdash; a negative expected time, or $E[X_\\tau]\\ne E[X_0]$ &mdash; the hypothesis failed, not the arithmetic. Check before you write.</div>\n      <div class=\"ex\"><b>Counterexample:</b> symmetric walk from $0$, $\\tau$ = first hit of $+1$. Then $X_\\tau = 1$ always, so $E[X_\\tau] = 1 \\ne 0$. $\\tau$ is finite a.s. but $E[\\tau] = \\infty$.<br><b>Q:</b> $\\tau = \\min\\{t: W_t = 1+2t\\}$ would give $E[\\tau] = -\\tfrac12 &lt; 0$. Impossible &mdash; the line escapes upward and $P(\\tau=\\infty)&gt;0$.</div>\n      <details class=\"proof\"><summary>Proof</summary><div>Counterexample: fair $\\pm1$ walk from $0$, $\\tau$ = first time it hits $1$. $\\tau&lt;\\infty$ a.s., but $E[X_\\tau]=1\\ne0=E[X_0]$. $\\tau$ is unbounded and $X_{t\\wedge\\tau}$ is not bounded below, so neither the bounded nor the dominated version (&sect;M6.7) applies.</div></details>", "M6.5": "\n      <h4>M6.5 &nbsp;OST Recipe #1 — hitting a moving line</h4>\n      <div class=\"eq\">$\\tau = \\min\\{t : W_t = a + bt\\}$</div>\n      <div class=\"eq\">$E[W_\\tau] = 0$ and $W_\\tau = a + b\\tau \\;\\Rightarrow\\; E[\\tau] = -\\dfrac{a}{b}$</div>\n      <div class=\"cond\">Needs $ab &lt; 0$.</div>\n      <div class=\"use\"><b>Use when:</b> three lines: apply OST, substitute the barrier equation, solve. See also &sect;10.7.</div>\n      <div class=\"ex\"><b>Paper:</b> Endsem&rsquo;25 Q1(d) &mdash; $W_t = 2 - 3t$: $0 = 2 - 3E[T]$, so $E[T] = \\mathbf{2/3}$.<br><b>Q:</b> $W_t = 3 - 6t$ gives $E[\\tau] = \\mathbf{1/2}$.</div>\n      <details class=\"proof\"><summary>Proof</summary><div>OST on the MG $W$ (justified as in &sect;M6.7; when the line slopes back towards $0$, $E\\tau&lt;\\infty$): $E[W_\\tau]=0$. At $\\tau$ the path is on the line: $W_\\tau=a+b\\tau$. So $0=a+bE\\tau$, $E\\tau=-a/b$. If $ab&gt;0$ this would be negative, impossible: then the line may never be hit.</div></details>", "M6.6": "\n      <h4>M6.6 &nbsp;⭐ OST Recipe #2 — gambler&rsquo;s ruin with two martingales</h4>\n      <div class=\"eq\">walk from $0$, absorbed at $+A$ or $-B$:</div>\n      <div class=\"eq\">use $X_n$: &nbsp; $Ap - B(1-p) = 0 \\Rightarrow p = \\dfrac{B}{A+B}$</div>\n      <div class=\"eq\">use $X_n^2 - n$: &nbsp; $E[\\tau] = E[X_\\tau^2] = A^2p + B^2(1-p) = A\\,B$</div>\n      <div class=\"use\"><b>Use when:</b> both parts of the ruin question in one framework. <b>$E[\\tau]$ always collapses to the product $AB$</b> &mdash; compute it either way as a check.</div>\n      <div class=\"ex\"><b>Paper:</b> CT-1&rsquo;26 Q10 &mdash; stop at $+10$ or $-5$, so $A{=}10$, $B{=}5$.<br>$p = \\tfrac{5}{15} = \\mathbf{1/3}$ ✓ &nbsp;|&nbsp; $E[\\tau] = 100(\\tfrac13)+25(\\tfrac23) = \\mathbf{50}$ ✓ $= 10\\times5$ ✓<br>Set-2 ($20,5$): $\\tfrac15$, $100$. Set-3 ($30,10$): $\\tfrac14$, $300$. Set-4 ($6,5$): $\\tfrac5{11}$, $30$. All match &sect;3.1&ndash;3.2.</div>\n      <details class=\"proof\"><summary>Proof</summary><div>$X_n$ is a MG trapped in $[-B,A]$, so OST holds (&sect;M6.7): $0=E[X_\\tau]=Ap-B(1-p)$, $p=\\frac{B}{A+B}$.<br>$X_n^2-n$ is a MG (&sect;M2.5): $E[X_{\\tau\\wedge n}^2]=E[\\tau\\wedge n]$. Let $n\\to\\infty$ (left side bounded, right side increasing): $E\\tau=E[X_\\tau^2]=A^2p+B^2(1-p)=\\frac{A^2B+B^2A}{A+B}=AB$.</div></details>", "M6.7": "\n      <h4>M6.7 &nbsp;Justifying an Unbounded $\\tau$</h4>\n      <div class=\"eq\">$\\tau$ unbounded but $X_{t\\wedge\\tau}$ <b>bounded</b> (trapped between barriers)</div>\n      <div class=\"eq\">$\\Rightarrow$ dominated convergence lets you pass $t\\to\\infty$ in $E[X_{t\\wedge\\tau}] = E[X_0]$</div>\n      <div class=\"use\"><b>Use when:</b> the gambler&rsquo;s-ruin $\\tau$ is <em>not</em> bounded, so M6.3 does not literally apply. Write this one sentence and the marks are safe.</div>\n      <div class=\"ex\"><b>Trick:</b> the path is confined to $[-B, A]$, which is exactly the domination you need. Contrast M6.4, where the walk can sink arbitrarily low &mdash; no bound, no theorem.</div>\n      <details class=\"proof\"><summary>Proof</summary><div>$\\tau\\wedge t$ is bounded, so $E[X_{\\tau\\wedge t}]=E[X_0]$ (&sect;M6.3). As $t\\to\\infty$, $X_{\\tau\\wedge t}\\to X_\\tau$ (since $\\tau&lt;\\infty$), and $|X_{\\tau\\wedge t}|\\le C$. Dominated convergence: $E[X_\\tau]=\\lim_tE[X_{\\tau\\wedge t}]=E[X_0]$.</div></details>", "M7.1": "\n      <h4>M7.1 &nbsp;The Game Process</h4>\n      <div class=\"eq\">$\\eta_n$ = winnings per unit stake in game $n$; &nbsp; $\\Xi_n = \\sum_{k\\le n}\\eta_k$; &nbsp; $\\mathcal F_n = \\sigma(\\eta_1,\\ldots,\\eta_n)$</div>\n      <div class=\"eq\">fair $\\iff$ MG &nbsp;|&nbsp; favourable $\\iff$ sub-MG &nbsp;|&nbsp; unfavourable $\\iff$ super-MG</div>\n      <div class=\"use\"><b>Use when:</b> the dictionary between the gambling words and the martingale words. Translate first, then it is M2.</div>\n      <div class=\"ex\"><b>Q:</b> roulette on red, 18 of 37 pockets: $E\\eta = \\tfrac{18}{37}-\\tfrac{19}{37} = -\\tfrac1{37} \\approx -0.027$. <b>Super-MG</b> &mdash; you lose 2.7 paise per rupee staked, whatever you do.</div>", "M7.2": "\n      <h4>M7.2 &nbsp;⭐ Predictable Strategy</h4>\n      <div class=\"eq\">the stake $\\alpha_n$ must be $\\mathcal F_{n-1}$-measurable &nbsp;(<b>predictable</b>)</div>\n      <div class=\"use\"><b>Use when:</b> the whole subject rests on this. $\\mathcal F_n$-measurable would mean placing the bet <em>after</em> seeing the outcome. Say why, not just what.</div>\n      <div class=\"ex\"><b>Allowed ✓:</b> $\\alpha_n = 2$ if you won game $n{-}1$ else $1$; $\\alpha_n = n$; doubling after each loss; $\\alpha_n = |\\Xi_{n-1}|+1$.<br><b>Forbidden ✗:</b> $\\alpha_n = \\mathbb 1\\{\\eta_n = +1\\}$ (&ldquo;bet only on games you will win&rdquo;) &mdash; it gives $E[Z_1] = \\tfrac12 &gt; 0$ in a fair game. Predictability is exactly what rules it out.</div>", "M7.3": "\n      <h4>M7.3 &nbsp;Wealth Process (martingale transform)</h4>\n      <div class=\"eq\">$Z_n = \\displaystyle\\sum_{k=1}^{n}\\alpha_k\\,\\eta_k = \\sum_{k=1}^{n}\\alpha_k(\\Xi_k - \\Xi_{k-1})$</div>\n      <div class=\"eq\">recursive: $Z_n = Z_{n-1} + \\alpha_n(\\Xi_n - \\Xi_{n-1})$</div>\n      <div class=\"use\"><b>Use when:</b> this is the discrete It&ocirc; integral &mdash; a predictable integrand against a martingale increment. &sect;7.5 is the same statement in continuous time.</div>\n      <div class=\"ex\"><b>Q:</b> doubling after each loss, results $+1,-1,-1,+1$: stakes $1,1,2,4$, gains $+1,-1,-2,+4$, so $Z = 1,0,-2,\\mathbf 2$.</div>\n      <details class=\"proof\"><summary>Proof</summary><div>Telescoping: $Z_n-Z_{n-1}=\\alpha_n\\eta_n=\\alpha_n(\\Xi_n-\\Xi_{n-1})$, since $\\eta_n=\\Xi_n-\\Xi_{n-1}$. Sum from $1$ to $n$.</div></details>", "M7.4": "\n      <h4>M7.4 &nbsp;Preservation Theorem</h4>\n      <div class=\"eq\">$\\Xi$ MG, $\\alpha$ predictable and bounded $\\Rightarrow Z$ MG</div>\n      <div class=\"eq\">$\\Xi$ sub-MG, $\\alpha$ predictable, bounded <b>and $\\alpha_n \\ge 0$</b> $\\Rightarrow Z$ sub-MG</div>\n      <div class=\"use\"><b>Use when:</b> &ldquo;why is $\\alpha_n\\ge0$ needed?&rdquo; is a standard follow-up. Because a <em>negative</em> stake against a favourable game turns a positive expected gain into a loss.</div>\n      <div class=\"ex\"><b>Proof:</b> $E(Z_n\\mid\\mathcal F_{n-1}) = Z_{n-1} + \\alpha_n\\underbrace{E(\\Xi_n-\\Xi_{n-1}\\mid\\mathcal F_{n-1})}_{=\\,0} = Z_{n-1}$ ✓<br>Both $Z_{n-1}$ and $\\alpha_n$ pull out because both are $\\mathcal F_{n-1}$-measurable &mdash; that is where predictability is used.</div>\n      <details class=\"proof\"><summary>Proof</summary><div>$E[Z_n-Z_{n-1}\\mid\\mathcal F_{n-1}]=\\alpha_nE[\\Xi_n-\\Xi_{n-1}\\mid\\mathcal F_{n-1}]$ (take out what is known, $\\alpha_n$ is predictable). This is $0$ for a MG, and $\\ge0$ for a sub-MG when $\\alpha_n\\ge0$. Integrable: $|\\alpha_k|\\le C\\Rightarrow|Z_n|\\le C\\sum_k|\\eta_k|$.</div></details>", "M7.5": "\n      <h4>M7.5 &nbsp;You Cannot Beat a Fair Game</h4>\n      <div class=\"eq\">$E[Z_n] = 0$ for every predictable bounded strategy</div>\n      <div class=\"use\"><b>Use when:</b> a betting system changes the <b>shape</b> of the payoff distribution, never its <b>mean</b>. That sentence is the answer to most conceptual parts here.</div>\n      <div class=\"ex\"><b>Q:</b> doubling until the first win, capped at 3 games. Win $+1$ with probability $\\tfrac78$; lose $1{+}2{+}4 = 7$ with probability $\\tfrac18$. $E = \\tfrac78 - \\tfrac78 = \\mathbf 0$ ✓<br><b>Trick:</b> a frequent small win paid for by a rare huge loss. Uncapped it appears to beat the game &mdash; but then $\\tau$ is unbounded and M6.3 does not apply.</div>\n      <details class=\"proof\"><summary>Proof</summary><div>$Z_n=\\sum_{k=1}^n \\alpha_k(X_k-X_{k-1})$ with $\\alpha_k$ known at time $k-1$ and bounded.<br>\n$E[\\alpha_k(X_k-X_{k-1})]=E\\big[\\alpha_k\\,E(X_k-X_{k-1}\\mid\\mathcal F_{k-1})\\big]=E[\\alpha_k\\cdot0]=0$. Sum over $k$: $E[Z_n]=0$. (Same step as the OST proof, &sect;M6.3.)</div></details>", "M7.6": "\n      <h4>M7.6 &nbsp;American vs European (short-note)</h4>\n      <div class=\"eq\">European: exercise only at $T$. &nbsp; American: exercise at any ST $\\tau \\le T$.</div>\n      <div class=\"eq\">American $\\ge$ European; &nbsp; <b>equal</b> for a call on a non-dividend stock</div>\n      <div class=\"use\"><b>Use when:</b> the short-note question. Say why they are equal for a call: the discounted price is a MG, early exercise throws away time value, and OST says no stopping rule improves a fair game. For a <b>put</b> early exercise can be optimal.</div>\n      <div class=\"ex\"><b>Paper:</b> Midsem&rsquo;25 Q2(a) &mdash; &ldquo;short note on American and European call options&rdquo; [2 marks].<br><b>Trick:</b> definition, the inequality, the call-equality with its one-line reason, and the put caveat. Four sentences.</div>\n      <details class=\"proof\"><summary>Proof</summary><div>American $\\ge$ European: $\\tau=T$ is one of the allowed stopping times.<br>Equal for a call ($r\\ge0$, no dividends): $Y_t=(1+r)^{-t}(S_t-K)^+$ is a sub-MG under $P^{*}$. By conditional Jensen, $E^{*}[Y_{t+1}\\mid\\mathcal F_t]\\ge(1+r)^{-(t+1)}\\big((1+r)S_t-K\\big)^+=(1+r)^{-t}\\big(S_t-\\tfrac{K}{1+r}\\big)^+\\ge Y_t$.<br>For a sub-MG and $\\tau\\le T$, $E^{*}[Y_\\tau]\\le E^{*}[Y_T]$ (OST, sub-MG version). So the best stopping time is $T$.</div></details>", "1.1": "\n      <h4>1.1 &nbsp;Standard Normal cdf</h4>\n      <div class=\"eq\">$\\Phi(-x) = 1 - \\Phi(x), \\qquad \\Phi(0) = 0.5$</div>\n      <div class=\"eq\">$P(a &lt; Z \\le b) = \\Phi(b) - \\Phi(a)$</div>\n      <div class=\"use\"><b>Use when:</b> always. Standardize first: $X \\sim N(\\mu,\\sigma^2) \\Rightarrow P(X\\le x) = \\Phi\\!\\big(\\frac{x-\\mu}{\\sigma}\\big)$.</div>\n      <div class=\"ex\"><b>Table values worth memorizing:</b> $\\Phi(1){=}.8413$, $\\Phi(1.5){=}.9332$, $\\Phi(2){=}.9772$, $\\Phi(2.5){=}.9938$, $\\Phi(3){=}.9987$.</div>\n      <details class=\"proof\"><summary>Proof</summary><div>The density is symmetric: $\\Phi(-x)=P(Z\\le-x)=P(Z\\ge x)=1-\\Phi(x)$. At $x=0$: $\\Phi(0)=1-\\Phi(0)$, so $\\tfrac12$. Interval: &sect;F5.2.</div></details>", "1.2": "\n      <h4>1.2 &nbsp;Exponential Moment (log-normal mean)</h4>\n      <div class=\"eq\">$X \\sim N(\\mu,\\sigma^2) \\;\\Longrightarrow\\; E[e^{X}] = e^{\\mu + \\sigma^2/2}$</div>\n      <div class=\"eq\">$E[e^{aZ}] = e^{a^2/2}, \\qquad Z\\sim N(0,1)$</div>\n      <div class=\"use\"><b>Use when:</b> an exponential of a normal appears &mdash; GBM means, $\\operatorname{Cov}(e^{W_s},e^{W_t})$, exponential martingale, Black&ndash;Scholes.</div>\n      <div class=\"ex\"><b>Trick:</b> the whole GBM section is this one identity. $E[e^{W_t}] = e^{t/2}$ because $W_t\\sim N(0,t)$.</div>\n      <details class=\"proof\"><summary>Proof</summary><div>$E[e^{aZ}]=\\int e^{az}\\frac{e^{-z^2/2}}{\\sqrt{2\\pi}}dz$. Complete the square: $az-\\tfrac{z^2}2=\\tfrac{a^2}2-\\tfrac{(z-a)^2}2$. So it is $e^{a^2/2}\\int\\frac{e^{-(z-a)^2/2}}{\\sqrt{2\\pi}}dz=e^{a^2/2}$ (a density integrates to 1).<br>$X=\\mu+\\sigma Z$: $E[e^X]=e^{\\mu}E[e^{\\sigma Z}]=e^{\\mu+\\sigma^2/2}$.</div></details>", "1.3": "\n      <h4>1.3 &nbsp;Log-normal Variance</h4>\n      <div class=\"eq\">$\\operatorname{Var}(e^X) = e^{2\\mu+\\sigma^2}\\big(e^{\\sigma^2}-1\\big)$</div>\n      <div class=\"cond\">$X \\sim N(\\mu,\\sigma^2)$.</div>\n      <div class=\"use\"><b>Use when:</b> asked for $\\operatorname{Var}$ or $\\operatorname{Cov}$ of a GBM / of $e^{W_t}$.</div>\n      <div class=\"ex\"><b>Q:</b> $Y=e^{W_2}$, find $\\operatorname{Var}(Y)$. Here $\\mu{=}0,\\sigma^2{=}2$: $e^{2}(e^{2}-1)=\\mathbf{47.21}$.<br><b>Trick:</b> $\\operatorname{Var} = (\\text{mean})^2(e^{\\sigma^2}-1)$ &mdash; compute the mean first, square it.</div>\n      <details class=\"proof\"><summary>Proof</summary><div>$\\operatorname{Var}(e^X)=E[e^{2X}]-(E[e^X])^2$. $2X\\sim N(2\\mu,4\\sigma^2)$, so $E[e^{2X}]=e^{2\\mu+2\\sigma^2}$ (&sect;1.2). Result: $e^{2\\mu+2\\sigma^2}-e^{2\\mu+\\sigma^2}=e^{2\\mu+\\sigma^2}(e^{\\sigma^2}-1)$.</div></details>", "1.4": "\n      <h4>1.4 &nbsp;Even Moments of a Normal</h4>\n      <div class=\"eq\">$E[Z^{2n}] = (2n-1)!! = 1\\cdot3\\cdot5\\cdots(2n-1)$</div>\n      <div class=\"eq\">$E[Z]{=}0,\\ E[Z^2]{=}1,\\ E[Z^3]{=}0,\\ E[Z^4]{=}3$</div>\n      <div class=\"eq\">$E[W_t^{2n}] = (2n-1)!!\\,t^{\\,n}, \\qquad E[W_t^4] = 3t^2$</div>\n      <div class=\"use\"><b>Use when:</b> any odd power &rarr; $0$ by symmetry. Any 4th moment &rarr; $3t^2$.</div>\n      <div class=\"ex\"><b>Paper:</b> CT-1 prints &ldquo;$E(Z^3)=0$, $E(Z^4)=3$&rdquo; at the top of the sheet &mdash; they expect you to need it in Q2.</div>\n      <details class=\"proof\"><summary>Proof</summary><div>Use $z\\varphi(z)=-\\varphi'(z)$ and integrate by parts: $E[Z^{2n}]=\\int z^{2n-1}\\,z\\varphi(z)\\,dz=(2n-1)\\int z^{2n-2}\\varphi(z)\\,dz=(2n-1)E[Z^{2n-2}]$. Iterate down to $E[Z^0]=1$. Odd moments are $0$ by symmetry. $W_t=\\sqrt t\\,Z$ gives the factor $t^n$.</div></details>", "1.5": "\n      <h4>1.5 &nbsp;MVN — Definition by Linear Combinations</h4>\n      <div class=\"eq\">$\\mathbf{X}$ is MVN $\\iff$ $\\mathbf{a}^{\\!\\top}\\mathbf{X} = \\sum_i a_i X_i$ is univariate normal for <em>every</em> $\\mathbf{a}$</div>\n      <div class=\"use\"><b>Use when:</b> proving something is a Gaussian process. This <em>is</em> the definition &mdash; do not try to write a joint density.</div>\n      <div class=\"ex\"><b>Q:</b> CT-1&rsquo;26 Q3 &mdash; is $e^{-t}W(e^{2t})$ a Gaussian process?<br><b>Trick:</b> it is a fixed linear combination of BM values, so every $\\sum a_i X(t_i)$ is normal. <b>Yes</b>, with zero computation.</div>", "1.6": "\n      <h4>1.6 &nbsp;Variance of a Linear Combination</h4>\n      <div class=\"eq\">$\\operatorname{Var}\\!\\Big(\\sum_i a_i X_i\\Big) = \\sum_i\\sum_j a_i a_j \\operatorname{Cov}(X_i,X_j)$</div>\n      <div class=\"use\"><b>Use when:</b> the master identity for every &ldquo;$aW_s+bW_t\\sim\\,$?&rdquo; question. Plug $\\operatorname{Cov}(W_s,W_t)=\\min(s,t)$.</div>\n      <div class=\"ex\"><b>Q:</b> $\\operatorname{Var}(W_1 - W_2 + 3W_4)$.<br><b>Trick:</b> write the $\\min$ matrix $\\begin{pmatrix}1&amp;1&amp;1\\\\1&amp;2&amp;2\\\\1&amp;2&amp;4\\end{pmatrix}$, sandwich with $\\mathbf a = (1,-1,3)$ &rarr; $\\mathbf{31}$.</div>\n      <details class=\"proof\"><summary>Proof</summary><div>$\\operatorname{Var}\\big(\\sum a_iX_i\\big)=E\\Big[\\big(\\sum_ia_i(X_i-\\mu_i)\\big)^2\\Big]=\\sum_i\\sum_ja_ia_jE[(X_i-\\mu_i)(X_j-\\mu_j)]$. Expand the square, then use linearity.</div></details>", "1.7": "\n      <h4>1.7 &nbsp;MVN Linear Transformation</h4>\n      <div class=\"eq\">$\\mathbf{X}\\sim N_n(\\boldsymbol\\mu,\\Sigma) \\Rightarrow A\\mathbf{X}+\\mathbf{b}\\sim N_m(A\\boldsymbol\\mu+\\mathbf{b},\\,A\\Sigma A^{\\!\\top})$</div>\n      <div class=\"use\"><b>Use when:</b> a process is built as a fixed matrix times BM values &mdash; e.g. the Brownian bridge $B(s)=W(s)-\\frac st W(t)$.</div>\n      <div class=\"ex\"><b>Q:</b> show $B(s) = W(s) - \\tfrac st W(t)$ is Gaussian with $\\operatorname{Cov} = \\min(u,v)-\\tfrac{uv}{t}$.<br><b>Trick:</b> $B$ is a <em>fixed</em> matrix times $(W(s),W(t))$, so MVN is automatic &mdash; then just compute $A\\Sigma A^{\\!\\top}$.</div>\n      <details class=\"proof\"><summary>Proof</summary><div>Any combination $\\mathbf c^{\\!\\top}(A\\mathbf X+\\mathbf b)=(A^{\\!\\top}\\mathbf c)^{\\!\\top}\\mathbf X+\\mathbf c^{\\!\\top}\\mathbf b$ is a combination of $\\mathbf X$ plus a constant, so normal: MVN (&sect;1.5). Mean $A\\boldsymbol\\mu+\\mathbf b$; covariance $E[A(\\mathbf X-\\boldsymbol\\mu)(\\mathbf X-\\boldsymbol\\mu)^{\\!\\top}A^{\\!\\top}]=A\\Sigma A^{\\!\\top}$.</div></details>", "1.8": "\n      <h4>1.8 &nbsp;MVN Joint MGF</h4>\n      <div class=\"eq\">$M_{\\mathbf X}(\\mathbf t) = \\exp\\!\\big(\\mathbf t^{\\!\\top}\\boldsymbol\\mu + \\tfrac12 \\mathbf t^{\\!\\top}\\Sigma\\,\\mathbf t\\big)$</div>\n      <div class=\"use\"><b>Use when:</b> you need $E[e^{W_s+W_t}]$ &mdash; set $\\mathbf t = (1,1)$. Fastest route to $\\operatorname{Cov}(e^{W_s},e^{W_t})$.</div>\n      <div class=\"ex\"><b>Q:</b> $E[e^{W_1+W_2}]$. &nbsp;$W_1+W_2 \\sim N(0,\\,1+2+2(1)) = N(0,5)$ &rarr; $\\mathbf{e^{5/2}}$.<br><b>Trick:</b> set $\\mathbf t = (1,1)$; the MGF hands you $\\exp(\\tfrac12\\sum_i\\sum_j\\Sigma_{ij})$ in one step.</div>\n      <details class=\"proof\"><summary>Proof</summary><div>$\\mathbf t^{\\!\\top}\\mathbf X\\sim N(\\mathbf t^{\\!\\top}\\boldsymbol\\mu,\\ \\mathbf t^{\\!\\top}\\Sigma\\mathbf t)$ (&sect;1.5, &sect;1.6), and $E[e^Y]=e^{m+v/2}$ (&sect;1.2).</div></details>", "1.9": "\n      <h4>1.9 &nbsp;MVN Conditional Distribution</h4>\n      <div class=\"eq\">$\\mathbf X \\mid \\mathbf Y{=}\\mathbf y \\sim N\\big(\\boldsymbol\\mu_X + \\Sigma_{XY}\\Sigma_{YY}^{-1}(\\mathbf y - \\boldsymbol\\mu_Y),\\ \\Sigma_{XX} - \\Sigma_{XY}\\Sigma_{YY}^{-1}\\Sigma_{YX}\\big)$</div>\n      <div class=\"use\"><b>Use when:</b> any &ldquo;given $W(t)=b$, find the law of $W(s)$&rdquo;. Conditional variance <em>never</em> depends on the observed value.</div>\n      <div class=\"ex\"><b>Trick:</b> for BM this collapses to &sect;9.4 &mdash; don&rsquo;t invert matrices, quote the bridge result.</div>\n      <details class=\"proof\"><summary>Proof</summary><div>Let $C=\\Sigma_{XY}\\Sigma_{YY}^{-1}$ and $\\mathbf R=\\mathbf X-\\boldsymbol\\mu_X-C(\\mathbf Y-\\boldsymbol\\mu_Y)$. Then $\\operatorname{Cov}(\\mathbf R,\\mathbf Y)=\\Sigma_{XY}-C\\Sigma_{YY}=0$; jointly normal and uncorrelated means independent.<br>So given $\\mathbf Y=\\mathbf y$: $\\mathbf X=\\boldsymbol\\mu_X+C(\\mathbf y-\\boldsymbol\\mu_Y)+\\mathbf R$, with $\\mathbf R\\sim N(0,\\operatorname{Cov}\\mathbf R)$ and $\\operatorname{Cov}\\mathbf R=\\Sigma_{XX}-C\\Sigma_{YX}-\\Sigma_{XY}C^{\\!\\top}+C\\Sigma_{YY}C^{\\!\\top}=\\Sigma_{XX}-\\Sigma_{XY}\\Sigma_{YY}^{-1}\\Sigma_{YX}$.</div></details>", "2.1": "\n      <h4>2.1 &nbsp;Additive Random Walk</h4>\n      <div class=\"eq\">$X_0 = 0,\\quad X_n = Y_1 + \\cdots + Y_n,\\quad Y_i \\text{ i.i.d.}$</div>\n      <div class=\"eq\">$\\{X_n\\}$ is a martingale $\\iff E[Y_1] = 0$</div>\n      <div class=\"cond\">Needs $E|Y_1| &lt; \\infty$.</div>\n      <div class=\"use\"><b>Use when:</b> the walk is built by <em>adding</em> i.i.d. steps. Sub-MG if $E[Y]&gt;0$, super-MG if $E[Y]&lt;0$.</div>\n      <div class=\"ex\"><b>Paper:</b> Midsem&rsquo;24 Q1(b) &mdash; &ldquo;$\\{X_n\\}$ is a martingale if $E(Y_1) = \\underline{\\ \\ }$&rdquo; &rarr; <b>$0$</b>.</div>\n      <details class=\"proof\"><summary>Proof</summary><div>$E[X_{n+1}\\mid\\mathcal F_n]=X_n+E[Y_{n+1}]=X_n+EY_1$ ($Y_{n+1}$ independent of the past). Equal to $X_n$ iff $EY_1=0$.</div></details>", "2.2": "\n      <h4>2.2 &nbsp;Geometric Random Walk</h4>\n      <div class=\"eq\">$X_n = X_0 \\prod_{i=1}^{n} Y_i, \\quad Y_i &gt; 0 \\text{ i.i.d.}$</div>\n      <div class=\"eq\">$\\{X_n\\}$ is a martingale $\\iff E[Y_1] = 1$</div>\n      <div class=\"use\"><b>Use when:</b> the walk <em>multiplies</em>. Proof is one line: $E[X_{n+1}\\mid\\mathcal F_n] = X_n E[Y_{n+1}]$ by independence + taking out what is known.</div>\n      <div class=\"ex\"><b>Paper:</b> CT-2&rsquo;25 Q3 &mdash; $X_n = 2^n U_1\\cdots U_n$, $U_i\\sim\\mathrm{Unif}(0,1)$. Step is $Y_i = 2U_i$, $E[2U]=2\\cdot\\tfrac12=1$ &rArr; <b>Yes, MG</b>.</div>\n      <details class=\"proof\"><summary>Proof</summary><div>$E[X_{n+1}\\mid\\mathcal F_n]=X_nE[Y_{n+1}]=X_nEY_1$ (take out $X_n$). Since $X_n&gt;0$, this equals $X_n$ iff $EY_1=1$.</div></details>", "2.3": "\n      <h4>2.3 &nbsp;Log-normal Steps — Drift Correction</h4>\n      <div class=\"eq\">$Y_i = e^{Z_i},\\ Z_i\\sim N(\\mu,\\sigma^2) \\Rightarrow E[Y_i] = e^{\\mu+\\sigma^2/2}$</div>\n      <div class=\"eq\">martingale $\\iff \\mu = -\\dfrac{\\sigma^2}{2}$</div>\n      <div class=\"use\"><b>Use when:</b> geometric walk with normal log-steps. The $-\\sigma^2/2$ is the <em>same</em> correction that appears in GBM (&sect;11.4) and Black&ndash;Scholes.</div>\n      <div class=\"ex\"><b>Q:</b> log-price steps $Z_i \\sim N(\\mu, 0.04)$; for what $\\mu$ is the price a martingale? &rarr; $\\mu = \\mathbf{-0.02}$.<br><b>Trick:</b> this $-\\sigma^2/2$ reappears in GBM (&sect;11.4) and as $d_2$ in Black&ndash;Scholes. Same correction, three places.</div>\n      <details class=\"proof\"><summary>Proof</summary><div>$E[e^{Z}]=e^{\\mu+\\sigma^2/2}$ (&sect;1.2). By &sect;2.2 MG $\\iff e^{\\mu+\\sigma^2/2}=1\\iff\\mu=-\\sigma^2/2$.</div></details>", "2.4": "\n      <h4>2.4 &nbsp;Moments of a Symmetric $\\pm1$ Walk</h4>\n      <div class=\"eq\">$E[X_n] = 0,\\qquad \\operatorname{Var}(X_n) = n$</div>\n      <div class=\"eq\">$\\operatorname{Cov}(X_m, X_n) = \\min(m,n)$</div>\n      <div class=\"use\"><b>Use when:</b> discrete analogue of $\\operatorname{Cov}(W_s,W_t)=\\min(s,t)$. Same proof: overlap of independent increments.</div>\n      <div class=\"ex\"><b>Q:</b> $\\operatorname{Cov}(X_3, X_7)$ for a $\\pm1$ walk $= \\mathbf{3}$.<br><b>Trick:</b> only the shared first $\\min(m,n)$ steps contribute; everything after is independent and mean zero.</div>\n      <details class=\"proof\"><summary>Proof</summary><div>Each step has mean $0$ and variance $1$, independent: $EX_n=0$, $\\operatorname{Var}X_n=n$. For $m\\le n$: $\\operatorname{Cov}(X_m,X_n)=\\operatorname{Cov}\\big(X_m,X_m+(X_n-X_m)\\big)=\\operatorname{Var}X_m+0=m$.</div></details>", "3.0": "\n      <h4>3.0 &nbsp;Setting Up the Word Problem</h4>\n      <div class=\"eq\">&ldquo;stops when up $\\,W\\,$ or down $\\,L$&rdquo; $\\;\\Longrightarrow\\; i = L,\\quad N = L + W$</div>\n      <div class=\"use\"><b>Use when:</b> the exam always phrases it as &ldquo;won $W$ rupees or lost $L$ rupees&rdquo;, never as &ldquo;start at $i$, barriers $0$ and $N$&rdquo;. <b>Do this translation first</b> &mdash; it is where marks are lost.</div>\n      <div class=\"ex\"><b>Paper:</b> CT-1&rsquo;26 Set-1 &mdash; &ldquo;won 10 or lost 5&rdquo; &rArr; $i=5$, $N=15$. Set-3 &mdash; &ldquo;won 30 or lost 10&rdquo; &rArr; $i=10$, $N=40$.</div>", "3.1": "\n      <h4>3.1 &nbsp;Ruin Probability — Fair Game</h4>\n      <div class=\"eq\">$P_i = P(\\text{hit } N \\text{ before } 0 \\mid X_0 = i) = \\dfrac{i}{N}$</div>\n      <div class=\"cond\">$p = \\tfrac12$.</div>\n      <div class=\"use\"><b>Use when:</b> &ldquo;equal chances&rdquo;, &ldquo;fair coin&rdquo;, &ldquo;wins or loses one rupee with equal probability&rdquo;.</div>\n      <div class=\"ex\"><b>Paper:</b> CT-1&rsquo;26 all four sets. Set-1: $5/15 = \\mathbf{1/3}$. Set-2 (won 20, lost 5): $5/25 = \\mathbf{1/5}$. Set-3: $10/40=\\mathbf{1/4}$. Set-4 (won 6, lost 5): $\\mathbf{5/11}$.</div>\n      <details class=\"proof\"><summary>Proof</summary><div>First step: from $i$ you go to $i\\pm1$ with probability $\\tfrac12$ each, so $P_i=\\tfrac12P_{i+1}+\\tfrac12P_{i-1}$, with $P_0=0$, $P_N=1$.<br>\nRearranged: $P_{i+1}-P_i=P_i-P_{i-1}$, so the gaps are equal and $P_i$ is a straight line through $(0,0)$ and $(N,1)$: $P_i=i/N$.<br>\n(OST route: $X_n$ is a MG, so $i=E[X_\\tau]=N\\cdot P_i+0$.)</div></details>", "3.2": "\n      <h4>3.2 &nbsp;Expected Duration — Fair Game</h4>\n      <div class=\"eq\">$D_i = E[\\text{number of rounds}] = i\\,(N - i)$</div>\n      <div class=\"cond\">$p=\\tfrac12$. Equivalently $D = L \\cdot W$ in the word-problem variables.</div>\n      <div class=\"use\"><b>Use when:</b> part (b) of the ruin question &mdash; &ldquo;expected number of rounds before stopping&rdquo;. It is <em>always</em> asked alongside 3.1.</div>\n      <div class=\"ex\"><b>Paper:</b> CT-1&rsquo;26 Set-1: $5\\times10 = \\mathbf{50}$. Set-2: $5\\times20=\\mathbf{100}$. Set-3: $10\\times30=\\mathbf{300}$. Set-4: $5\\times6=\\mathbf{30}$. <b>Trick:</b> it is literally $L\\times W$ &mdash; no formula needed.</div>\n      <details class=\"proof\"><summary>Proof</summary><div>$X_n^2-n$ is a martingale for the fair $\\pm1$ walk (&sect;M6.6). OST at the exit time $\\tau$:<br>\n$E[X_\\tau^2]-E[\\tau]=i^2$. Now $X_\\tau=N$ with probability $i/N$ and $0$ otherwise, so $E[X_\\tau^2]=N^2\\cdot\\tfrac iN=iN$.<br>\nHence $D_i=E[\\tau]=iN-i^2=i(N-i)$.</div></details>", "3.3": "\n      <h4>3.3 &nbsp;Ruin Probability — Unfair Game</h4>\n      <div class=\"eq\">$P_i = \\dfrac{1-(q/p)^i}{1-(q/p)^N}, \\qquad q = 1-p,\\ p\\neq\\tfrac12$</div>\n      <div class=\"use\"><b>Use when:</b> win probability is given as anything other than $1/2$ (e.g. $2/3$ and $1/3$). Let $r = q/p$ and evaluate numerically &mdash; do not expand.</div>\n      <div class=\"ex\"><b>Q:</b> $p = 2/3$, stop at $+3$ or $-2$. Translate: $i{=}2$, $N{=}5$, $r = q/p = \\tfrac12$.<br>$P = \\dfrac{1-(1/2)^2}{1-(1/2)^5} = \\dfrac{0.75}{0.96875} = \\mathbf{0.774}$.<br><b>Trick:</b> compute $r$ once, then it is two powers and a division.</div>\n      <details class=\"proof\"><summary>Proof</summary><div>First step (&sect;3.6): $P_i=pP_{i+1}+qP_{i-1}$, i.e. $p(P_{i+1}-P_i)=q(P_i-P_{i-1})$. With $\\rho=q/p$ the gaps grow geometrically: $P_{i+1}-P_i=\\rho^i P_1$. Add up: $P_i=P_1(1+\\rho+\\dots+\\rho^{i-1})=P_1\\frac{1-\\rho^i}{1-\\rho}$. $P_N=1$ fixes $P_1$, giving $P_i=\\frac{1-\\rho^i}{1-\\rho^N}$.</div></details>", "3.4": "\n      <h4>3.4 &nbsp;Expected Duration — Unfair Game</h4>\n      <div class=\"eq\">$D_i = \\dfrac{i}{q-p} - \\dfrac{N}{q-p}\\cdot\\dfrac{1-(q/p)^i}{1-(q/p)^N}$</div>\n      <div class=\"eq\">$= \\dfrac{1}{q-p}\\big(i - N P_i\\big)$</div>\n      <div class=\"use\"><b>Use when:</b> duration with $p\\ne\\tfrac12$. <b>Trick:</b> compute $P_i$ first (3.3), then this is one subtraction.</div>\n      <div class=\"ex\"><b>Q:</b> same numbers. $q-p = -\\tfrac13$, so $D = \\dfrac{2 - 5(0.774)}{-1/3} = \\mathbf{5.61}$ rounds.<br><b>Trick:</b> always get $P_i$ from 3.3 first &mdash; never expand the full expression.</div>\n      <details class=\"proof\"><summary>Proof</summary><div>$X_n-n(p-q)$ is a MG (&sect;M2.5, drift removed). OST at the exit time: $E[X_\\tau]-(p-q)E\\tau=i$. Since $E[X_\\tau]=N\\,P_i+0$: $D_i=E\\tau=\\frac{i-NP_i}{q-p}$.</div></details>", "3.5": "\n      <h4>3.5 &nbsp;Infinite Bankroll Limit</h4>\n      <div class=\"eq\">$\\lim_{N\\to\\infty} P_i = \\begin{cases} 1-(q/p)^i & p &gt; \\tfrac12 \\\\[2pt] 0 & p \\le \\tfrac12\\end{cases}$</div>\n      <div class=\"use\"><b>Use when:</b> &ldquo;plays forever against an infinitely rich opponent&rdquo; / &ldquo;$N\\to\\infty$&rdquo;. Ruin is a.s. certain unless there is a genuine edge.</div>\n      <div class=\"ex\"><b>Q:</b> &ldquo;plays forever against the house with $p = 0.49$, starting with 10&rdquo;. $p &lt; \\tfrac12$ &rarr; ruin probability $\\mathbf{1}$.<br><b>Trick:</b> any fair-or-worse game against an infinite bankroll ends in ruin almost surely. No arithmetic needed.</div>\n      <details class=\"proof\"><summary>Proof</summary><div>$\\rho=q/p$. If $p&gt;\\tfrac12$: $\\rho&lt;1$, $\\rho^N\\to0$, limit $1-\\rho^i$. If $p&lt;\\tfrac12$: $\\rho&gt;1$, $\\frac{\\rho^i-1}{\\rho^N-1}\\to0$. If $p=\\tfrac12$: $\\frac iN\\to0$.</div></details>", "3.6": "\n      <h4>3.6 &nbsp;Derivation Hook (first-step analysis)</h4>\n      <div class=\"eq\">$P_i = p\\,P_{i+1} + q\\,P_{i-1}, \\quad P_0 = 0,\\ P_N = 1$</div>\n      <div class=\"eq\">$D_i = 1 + p\\,D_{i+1} + q\\,D_{i-1}, \\quad D_0 = D_N = 0$</div>\n      <div class=\"use\"><b>Use when:</b> the question says &ldquo;derive&rdquo; or &ldquo;using first-step analysis&rdquo; (Midsem&rsquo;25 Q2(c) asks for a short note on gambler&rsquo;s ruin). The $+1$ in the duration recursion is the round just played.</div>\n      <div class=\"ex\"><b>Paper:</b> Midsem&rsquo;25 Q2(c) &mdash; &ldquo;write a short note on gambler&rsquo;s ruin&rdquo; [2 marks].<br><b>Trick:</b> state the recursion, state the boundary conditions, then quote 3.1/3.3 as its solution. That is the whole answer.</div>\n      <details class=\"proof\"><summary>Proof</summary><div>Condition on the first round: with probability $p$ you move to $i+1$, with $q$ to $i-1$, and the game starts afresh from there. For $D$, that first round itself costs $1$. Boundaries: at $0$ or $N$ the game is over.</div></details>", "4.1": "\n      <h4>4.1 &nbsp;Diffusive Scaling — Driftless</h4>\n      <div class=\"eq\">steps $\\pm\\Delta x$ every $\\Delta t$, $\\ \\Delta x = \\sigma\\sqrt{\\Delta t}$</div>\n      <div class=\"eq\">$X_{\\lfloor t/\\Delta t\\rfloor} \\;\\xrightarrow[\\Delta t\\to0]{d}\\; \\sigma W(t)$</div>\n      <div class=\"use\"><b>Use when:</b> a limit of a symmetric walk is taken. The <b>only</b> scaling that survives is space $\\sim \\sqrt{\\text{time}}$ &mdash; that is what forces $\\operatorname{Var}(W_t)=t$.</div>\n      <div class=\"ex\"><b>Q:</b> &ldquo;why is $\\operatorname{Var}(W_t) = t$ and not $t^2$?&rdquo;<br><b>Trick:</b> $n = t/\\Delta t$ steps of size $\\Delta x$ give variance $t(\\Delta x)^2/\\Delta t$. That is finite <em>and</em> non-zero only if $\\Delta x \\propto \\sqrt{\\Delta t}$. Everything else degenerates.</div>\n      <details class=\"proof\"><summary>Proof</summary><div>After $n=t/\\Delta t$ steps: a sum of $n$ i.i.d. $\\pm\\Delta x$, mean $0$, variance $n\\Delta x^2=\\frac{t}{\\Delta t}\\sigma^2\\Delta t=\\sigma^2t$. CLT: $\\to N(0,\\sigma^2t)$. Disjoint time intervals use disjoint steps, so increments are independent: the limit is $\\sigma W$.</div></details>", "4.2": "\n      <h4>4.2 &nbsp;Scaling with Drift</h4>\n      <div class=\"eq\">$p = \\tfrac12\\Big(1 + \\dfrac{\\mu}{\\sigma}\\sqrt{\\Delta t}\\Big),\\quad \\text{steps } \\pm\\sigma\\sqrt{\\Delta t}$</div>\n      <div class=\"eq\">$\\Longrightarrow\\ X(t) \\to \\mu t + \\sigma W(t)$</div>\n      <div class=\"use\"><b>Use when:</b> the question hands you a walk with $p$ depending on $\\sqrt{\\Delta t}$ and says &ldquo;argue the limit is BM with drift $\\mu$&rdquo;.</div>\n      <div class=\"ex\"><b>Paper:</b> Midsem&rsquo;24 Q3(b) &mdash; steps $\\pm\\sqrt{\\Delta t}$, $p=\\tfrac12(1+\\mu\\sqrt{\\Delta t})$ (so $\\sigma=1$).<br><b>Two-line proof:</b> per-step mean $=\\sqrt{\\Delta t}(2p-1) = \\mu\\,\\Delta t$; per-step variance $=\\Delta t\\big(1-(2p-1)^2\\big) \\to \\Delta t$. Over $n=t/\\Delta t$ steps: mean $\\to\\mu t$, variance $\\to t$. CLT finishes it.</div>\n      <details class=\"proof\"><summary>Proof</summary><div>One step has mean $\\sigma\\sqrt{\\Delta t}(2p-1)=\\sigma\\sqrt{\\Delta t}\\cdot\\frac{\\mu}{\\sigma}\\sqrt{\\Delta t}=\\mu\\Delta t$ and variance $\\sigma^2\\Delta t-\\mu^2\\Delta t^2\\approx\\sigma^2\\Delta t$. Over $t/\\Delta t$ steps: mean $\\mu t$, variance $\\to\\sigma^2t$. CLT as in &sect;4.1: $\\mu t+\\sigma W(t)$.</div></details>", "5.1": "\n      <h4>5.1 &nbsp;Wiener Process Axioms</h4>\n      <div class=\"eq\">(i) $W(0) = 0$</div>\n      <div class=\"eq\">(ii) independent increments</div>\n      <div class=\"eq\">(iii) $W(t) - W(s) \\sim N(0,\\,t-s)$ for $s&lt;t$ &nbsp;(stationary)</div>\n      <div class=\"eq\">(iv) $t \\mapsto W(t)$ continuous a.s.</div>\n      <div class=\"use\"><b>Use when:</b> the four-item checklist for &ldquo;short note&rdquo; questions and for verifying a constructed process.</div>\n      <div class=\"ex\"><b>Paper:</b> Midsem&rsquo;25 Q2 / Endsem &mdash; &ldquo;short note on the Wiener process&rdquo;.<br><b>Trick:</b> write the four axioms, then add $\\operatorname{Cov}=\\min(s,t)$ and &ldquo;continuous but nowhere differentiable&rdquo; for the extra mark.</div>", "5.2": "\n      <h4>5.2 &nbsp;Is $X(t)$ a Wiener Process? — 4-point test</h4>\n      <div class=\"eq\">1. $X(0) = 0$ ?</div>\n      <div class=\"eq\">2. $X$ Gaussian (all f.d.d. normal) ?</div>\n      <div class=\"eq\">3. $E[X(t)] = 0$ ?</div>\n      <div class=\"eq\">4. $\\operatorname{Cov}(X(s),X(t)) = \\min(s,t)$ ?</div>\n      <div class=\"use\"><b>Use when:</b> T/F questions of the form &ldquo;$V(t) = \\ldots$ is also a Wiener process&rdquo;. Checking $\\min(s,t)$ is usually the deciding step; $X(0)=0$ is the usual trap.</div>\n      <div class=\"ex\"><b>Paper:</b> Midsem&rsquo;25 Q1(e) &mdash; $V(t)=\\tfrac1c W(c^2t)$, $c&gt;0$. $V(0)=0$ ✓; $\\operatorname{Cov} = \\tfrac1{c^2}\\min(c^2s,c^2t)=\\min(s,t)$ ✓ &rArr; <b>True</b>.</div>\n      <details class=\"proof\"><summary>Proof</summary><div>A Gaussian process is fixed by its mean and covariance. If $X$ is Gaussian with $X(0)=0$, mean $0$, covariance $\\min(s,t)$, it has the same finite-dimensional laws as BM. Independent increments follow: for $s\\le t$, $\\operatorname{Cov}(X_t-X_s,X_s)=s-s=0$, and uncorrelated jointly normal variables are independent. Add continuity and it is BM.</div></details>", "5.3": "\n      <h4>5.3 &nbsp;Is $X(t)$ a Gaussian Process? — weaker test</h4>\n      <div class=\"eq\">Yes $\\iff$ every finite $\\sum a_i X(t_i)$ is normal</div>\n      <div class=\"eq\">$f(t)\\,W(g(t))$ with $f,g$ deterministic $\\Rightarrow$ <b>always</b> Gaussian</div>\n      <div class=\"use\"><b>Use when:</b> the question asks Gaussian process, <em>not</em> Wiener. A deterministic scaling and a deterministic time change can never destroy jointly-normal structure &mdash; the answer is Yes without computation.</div>\n      <div class=\"ex\"><b>Paper:</b> CT-1&rsquo;26 Q3, all four sets &mdash; $e^{-t}W(e^{2t})$, $e^{-2t}W(e^{2t})$, $e^{-t}W(e^{t})$, $e^{-3t}W(e^{2t})$. Every answer is <b>Yes</b>. Not Wiener though: $X(0) = W(1) \\ne 0$.</div>\n      <details class=\"proof\"><summary>Proof</summary><div>Gaussian process = every finite combination is normal (definition). For $f(t)W(g(t))$: $\\sum a_if(t_i)W(g(t_i))$ is a fixed linear combination of BM values, which is normal (&sect;6.2).</div></details>", "5.4": "\n      <h4>5.4 &nbsp;The Four Invariances of BM</h4>\n      <div class=\"eq\">Scaling: $\\tfrac1c W(c^2 t)$</div>\n      <div class=\"eq\">Symmetry: $-W(t)$</div>\n      <div class=\"eq\">Time inversion: $t\\,W(1/t)$ for $t&gt;0$, $\\;0$ at $t=0$</div>\n      <div class=\"eq\">Time reversal on $[0,T]$: $W(T) - W(T-t)$</div>\n      <div class=\"use\"><b>Use when:</b> all four <em>are</em> standard BM. Anything else in a T/F question is probably not &mdash; run &sect;5.2.</div>\n      <div class=\"ex\"><b>Q:</b> &ldquo;Is $-W(t)$ a Wiener process? Is $tW(1/t)$?&rdquo; &mdash; both <b>Yes</b>.<br><b>Trick:</b> memorize these four outright. A T/F on anything <em>else</em> means you must run the &sect;5.2 checklist.</div>\n      <details class=\"proof\"><summary>Proof</summary><div>Each is a linear function of $W$, so Gaussian with mean $0$ and value $0$ at $t=0$. Check covariance $=s$ for $s\\le t$ (&sect;5.2):<br>Scaling: $\\frac1{c^2}\\min(c^2s,c^2t)=s$. Symmetry: unchanged. Inversion: $st\\min(\\tfrac1s,\\tfrac1t)=st\\cdot\\tfrac1t=s$. Reversal: $\\operatorname{Cov}=T-\\min(T,T-t)-\\min(T-s,T)+\\min(T-s,T-t)=T-(T-t)-(T-s)+(T-t)=s$.<br>(Inversion is continuous at $0$ because $W(u)/u\\to0$, law of large numbers.)</div></details>", "5.5": "\n      <h4>5.5 &nbsp;Ornstein&ndash;Uhlenbeck from BM</h4>\n      <div class=\"eq\">$X(t) = e^{-\\lambda t}W\\!\\big(e^{2\\lambda t}\\big)$</div>\n      <div class=\"eq\">$\\operatorname{Cov}(X(s),X(t)) = e^{-\\lambda|t-s|}$</div>\n      <div class=\"use\"><b>Use when:</b> covariance depends on $|t-s|$ only &rArr; <b>stationary</b> Gaussian process (OU), <em>not</em> Wiener. Wiener needs $\\min(s,t)$.</div>\n      <div class=\"ex\"><b>Trick:</b> $\\operatorname{Cov} = e^{-\\lambda(s+t)}\\min(e^{2\\lambda s},e^{2\\lambda t}) = e^{-\\lambda(s+t)}e^{2\\lambda \\min(s,t)} = e^{-\\lambda|t-s|}$.</div>\n      <details class=\"proof\"><summary>Proof</summary><div>For $s\\le t$: $\\operatorname{Cov}=e^{-\\lambda(s+t)}\\min(e^{2\\lambda s},e^{2\\lambda t})=e^{-\\lambda(s+t)}e^{2\\lambda s}=e^{-\\lambda(t-s)}$.</div></details>", "5.6": "\n      <h4>5.6 &nbsp;Lévy&rsquo;s Martingale Characterization</h4>\n      <div class=\"eq\">$X$ continuous, $X(0)=0$, $X_t$ and $X_t^2 - t$ both martingales $\\Longrightarrow X$ is standard BM</div>\n      <div class=\"use\"><b>Use when:</b> you must prove BM-ness without touching distributions. Two martingale checks replace the whole axiom list.</div>\n      <div class=\"ex\"><b>Q:</b> &ldquo;prove the scaled random-walk limit is Brownian motion&rdquo;.<br><b>Trick:</b> L&eacute;vy lets you skip distributions entirely &mdash; show continuity, $X_0 = 0$, and that $X_t$ and $X_t^2 - t$ are both martingales.</div>\n      <details class=\"proof\"><summary>Proof</summary><div>Sketch. $X^2-t$ a MG gives $(dX)^2=dt$, so Itô applies as for BM: $e^{uX_t-u^2t/2}$ has zero drift and is a MG. Hence $E\\big[e^{u(X_t-X_s)}\\mid\\mathcal F_s\\big]=e^{u^2(t-s)/2}$, the MGF of $N(0,t-s)$, and it does not depend on the past. So increments are $N(0,t-s)$ and independent of $\\mathcal F_s$: $X$ is BM.</div></details>", "5.7": "\n      <h4>5.7 &nbsp;Path Properties (short-note ammo)</h4>\n      <div class=\"eq\">Continuous everywhere, differentiable nowhere (a.s.)</div>\n      <div class=\"eq\">Quadratic variation on $[0,t]$ $= t$, i.e. $(dW)^2 = dt$</div>\n      <div class=\"eq\">Total variation $=\\infty$; Markov; strong Markov</div>\n      <div class=\"use\"><b>Use when:</b> &ldquo;write a short note&rdquo;. $(dW)^2 = dt$ is the single fact that powers all of It&ocirc;&rsquo;s formula.</div>\n      <div class=\"ex\"><b>Q:</b> Endsem-style &mdash; &ldquo;explain quadratic variation&rdquo;.<br><b>Trick:</b> say the sentence &ldquo;$(dW)^2 = dt$ is why It&ocirc;&rsquo;s formula carries the $\\tfrac12 f''$ term at all&rdquo;. That one link is what they are marking.</div>\n      <details class=\"proof\"><summary>Proof</summary><div>Quadratic variation: $E\\sum(\\Delta W)^2=\\sum\\Delta t=t$ and $\\operatorname{Var}\\sum(\\Delta W)^2=\\sum2\\Delta t^2\\le2t\\cdot\\text{mesh}\\to0$.<br>Total variation: $\\sum(\\Delta W)^2\\le\\max|\\Delta W|\\cdot\\sum|\\Delta W|$. Left $\\to t&gt;0$, $\\max|\\Delta W|\\to0$ (continuity), so $\\sum|\\Delta W|\\to\\infty$.<br>Not differentiable (idea): $\\frac{W_{t+h}-W_t}{h}\\sim N(0,\\tfrac1h)$, which blows up as $h\\to0$. Markov: future increments are independent of the past.</div></details>", "6.1": "\n      <h4>6.1 &nbsp;Covariance of BM</h4>\n      <div class=\"eq\">$\\operatorname{Cov}(W_s, W_t) = E[W_s W_t] = \\min(s,t)$</div>\n      <div class=\"use\"><b>Use when:</b> the single most-used identity in the paper. Derivation: for $s&lt;t$, $E[W_sW_t] = E[W_s(W_s + (W_t-W_s))] = s + 0$.</div>\n      <div class=\"ex\"><b>Paper:</b> Midsem&rsquo;24 Q1(d) &mdash; $\\operatorname{Var}(W_4)\\times\\operatorname{Cov}(W_1,W_4) = 4 \\times 1 = \\mathbf{4}$.</div>\n      <details class=\"proof\"><summary>Proof</summary><div>$s\\le t$: $E[W_sW_t]=E[W_s(W_s+(W_t-W_s))]=E[W_s^2]+E[W_s]E[W_t-W_s]=s$.</div></details>", "6.2": "\n      <h4>6.2 &nbsp;Linear Combination of BM Values</h4>\n      <div class=\"eq\">$\\sum_i a_i W_{t_i} \\sim N\\!\\Big(0,\\ \\sum_i\\sum_j a_i a_j \\min(t_i,t_j)\\Big)$</div>\n      <div class=\"eq\">two terms: $\\operatorname{Var}(aW_s + bW_t) = a^2 s + b^2 t + 2ab\\min(s,t)$</div>\n      <div class=\"use\"><b>Use when:</b> &ldquo;$4W_1 + 6W_3 \\sim\\ ?$&rdquo;. <b>Trick:</b> build the $\\min$ matrix, then it is one dot product. Mean is always $0$.</div>\n      <div class=\"ex\"><b>Paper:</b> CT-1&rsquo;26 Q4 &mdash; $4W_1+6W_3$: $16(1)+36(3)+2(24)(1) = 16+108+48 = \\mathbf{N(0,172)}$. Set-2 $3W_1{+}6W_3\\!: \\mathbf{153}$; Set-3 $4W_1{+}5W_3\\!: \\mathbf{131}$; Set-4 $4W_1{+}7W_3\\!: \\mathbf{219}$.<br>Midsem&rsquo;25 Q1(d) &mdash; $W_1 - W_2 + 3W_4$: $1+2+36-2(1)+6(1)-6(2) = \\mathbf{N(0,31)}$.</div>\n      <details class=\"proof\"><summary>Proof</summary><div>BM values are jointly normal, so a linear combination is normal with mean $0$. Variance by &sect;1.6 with $\\operatorname{Cov}(W_{t_i},W_{t_j})=\\min(t_i,t_j)$ (&sect;6.1).</div></details>", "6.3": "\n      <h4>6.3 &nbsp;Increment Splitting — the universal trick</h4>\n      <div class=\"eq\">$s&lt;t:\\quad W_t = W_s + \\underbrace{(W_t - W_s)}_{\\Delta \\,\\sim\\, N(0,\\,t-s),\\ \\perp\\ \\mathcal F_s}$</div>\n      <div class=\"use\"><b>Use when:</b> <em>any</em> mixed moment $E[g(W_s)h(W_t)]$. Substitute, expand, kill odd powers of $\\Delta$, use independence. This replaces every 2-D integral.</div>\n      <div class=\"ex\"><b>Q:</b> $E[W_2 W_3^3]$. Put $W_3 = W_2 + \\Delta$, expand: $E[W_2^4] + 3E[W_2^2]E[\\Delta^2] = 3(4) + 3(2)(1) = \\mathbf{18}$.<br><b>General:</b> $E[W_sW_t^3] = 3st$ for $s\\le t$. <b>Trick:</b> every odd power of $\\Delta$ dies.</div>", "6.4": "\n      <h4>6.4 &nbsp;Mixed Fourth Moment</h4>\n      <div class=\"eq\">$E[W_s^2 W_t^2] = 2s^2 + st = s(2s+t), \\qquad s \\le t$</div>\n      <div class=\"use\"><b>Use when:</b> exactly this question. <b>Order matters</b> &mdash; $s$ is the <em>smaller</em> index. Derivation: $E[W_s^2(W_s+\\Delta)^2] = E[W_s^4] + E[W_s^2]E[\\Delta^2] = 3s^2 + s(t-s)$.</div>\n      <div class=\"ex\"><b>Paper:</b> CT-1&rsquo;26 Q2 &mdash; $E(W_2^2W_3^2)=2(4)+6=\\mathbf{14}$; $E(W_1^2W_3^2)=2+3=\\mathbf{5}$; $E(W_2^2W_4^2)=8+8=\\mathbf{16}$; $E(W_1^2W_4^2)=2+4=\\mathbf{6}$.</div>\n      <details class=\"proof\"><summary>Proof</summary><div>$W_t=W_s+\\Delta$, $\\Delta\\sim N(0,t-s)$ independent of $W_s$. $E[W_s^2(W_s+\\Delta)^2]=E[W_s^4]+2E[W_s^3]E[\\Delta]+E[W_s^2]E[\\Delta^2]=3s^2+0+s(t-s)=2s^2+st$.</div></details>", "6.5": "\n      <h4>6.5 &nbsp;Covariance of Exponentials</h4>\n      <div class=\"eq\">$\\operatorname{Cov}\\big(e^{W_s}, e^{W_t}\\big) = e^{(s+t)/2}\\Big(e^{\\min(s,t)} - 1\\Big)$</div>\n      <div class=\"use\"><b>Use when:</b> $Y_t := e^{W_t}$, asked for $\\operatorname{Cov}(Y_s,Y_t)$. <b>Trick:</b> $W_s+W_t\\sim N(0,\\,s+t+2\\min(s,t))$, then $E[e^{W_s+W_t}] = e^{(s+t+2\\min)/2}$, minus $e^{s/2}e^{t/2}$.</div>\n      <div class=\"ex\"><b>Paper:</b> CT-1&rsquo;26 Q9 &mdash; $\\operatorname{Cov}(Y_1,Y_2)=e^{3/2}(e-1)$; $\\operatorname{Cov}(Y_2,Y_3)=e^{5/2}(e^2-1)$; $\\operatorname{Cov}(Y_2,Y_4)=e^{3}(e^2-1)\\approx\\mathbf{948.43}$; $\\operatorname{Cov}(Y_1,Y_4)=e^{5/2}(e-1)\\approx\\mathbf{20.93}$.</div>\n      <details class=\"proof\"><summary>Proof</summary><div>$s\\le t$: $W_s+W_t=2W_s+\\Delta\\sim N(0,4s+t-s)=N(0,3s+t)$, so $E[e^{W_s+W_t}]=e^{(3s+t)/2}$. And $E[e^{W_s}]E[e^{W_t}]=e^{(s+t)/2}$. Subtract: $e^{(s+t)/2}(e^{s}-1)$.</div></details>", "6.6": "\n      <h4>6.6 &nbsp;Correlation of BM</h4>\n      <div class=\"eq\">$\\operatorname{Corr}(W_s,W_t) = \\dfrac{\\min(s,t)}{\\sqrt{st}} = \\sqrt{\\dfrac{s}{t}}, \\quad s\\le t$</div>\n      <div class=\"use\"><b>Use when:</b> a correlation (not covariance) is requested, or when setting up a bivariate normal for conditioning.</div>\n      <div class=\"ex\"><b>Q:</b> $\\operatorname{Corr}(W_1,W_4) = \\sqrt{1/4} = \\mathbf{0.5}$.<br><b>Trick:</b> correlation is $\\sqrt{s/t}$ &mdash; it decays like a square root, so distant times are far less correlated than the covariance $\\min(s,t)$ suggests.</div>\n      <details class=\"proof\"><summary>Proof</summary><div>$\\frac{\\operatorname{Cov}}{\\sqrt{\\operatorname{Var}\\operatorname{Var}}}=\\frac{s}{\\sqrt{st}}=\\sqrt{\\frac st}$ for $s\\le t$.</div></details>", "7.1": "\n      <h4>7.1 &nbsp;Canonical Martingale 1 — Linear</h4>\n      <div class=\"eq\">$W(t)$ is a martingale; $\\;E[W_t\\mid\\mathcal F_s] = W_s$</div>\n      <div class=\"use\"><b>Use when:</b> the base case, and the engine behind every Optional Stopping argument (&sect;10.7).</div>\n      <div class=\"ex\"><b>Paper:</b> Endsem&rsquo;25 Q1(d) needs exactly one fact: $E[W_T] = 0$ at a stopping time.<br><b>Trick:</b> $W_t$ being a martingale is the <em>only</em> input to every OST question in &sect;10.7. Nothing else is required.</div>\n      <details class=\"proof\"><summary>Proof</summary><div>$E[W_t\\mid\\mathcal F_s]=W_s+E[W_t-W_s]=W_s$ (increment independent of $\\mathcal F_s$, mean $0$). Integrable: $E|W_t|&lt;\\infty$.</div></details>", "7.2": "\n      <h4>7.2 &nbsp;Canonical Martingale 2 — Quadratic</h4>\n      <div class=\"eq\">$W(t)^2 - t$ is a martingale; $\\;E[W_t^2\\mid\\mathcal F_s] = W_s^2 + (t-s)$</div>\n      <div class=\"use\"><b>Use when:</b> you need $E[\\tau]$ for an exit problem, or a T/F on $|W_t|^2 - t$.</div>\n      <div class=\"ex\"><b>Paper:</b> Midsem&rsquo;25 Q1(f) &mdash; &ldquo;$|W_t|^2 - t$ is <b>NOT</b> a martingale (T/F)&rdquo;. It <em>is</em> one, so the statement is <b>False</b>. Watch the negation.</div>\n      <details class=\"proof\"><summary>Proof</summary><div>$E[(W_s+\\Delta)^2\\mid\\mathcal F_s]=W_s^2+2W_sE\\Delta+E\\Delta^2=W_s^2+(t-s)$. Subtract $t$ from both sides: $W_s^2-s$.</div></details>", "7.3": "\n      <h4>7.3 &nbsp;Canonical Martingale 3 — Exponential</h4>\n      <div class=\"eq\">$M_\\lambda(t) = \\exp\\!\\Big(\\lambda W_t - \\tfrac{\\lambda^2}{2}t\\Big)$ is a martingale, every $\\lambda\\in\\mathbb R$</div>\n      <div class=\"use\"><b>Use when:</b> hitting-time Laplace transforms, Girsanov, and checking GBM martingality. $E[M_\\lambda(t)] = 1$ for all $t$.</div>\n      <div class=\"ex\"><b>Q:</b> find $E[e^{-\\lambda\\tau_a}]$ (&sect;12.7).<br><b>Trick:</b> choose the $\\lambda$ in the martingale that makes the exponent match the barrier, then apply OST. $E[M_\\lambda(t)] = 1$ finishes it.</div>\n      <details class=\"proof\"><summary>Proof</summary><div>$E[e^{\\lambda W_t}\\mid\\mathcal F_s]=e^{\\lambda W_s}E[e^{\\lambda\\Delta}]=e^{\\lambda W_s+\\lambda^2(t-s)/2}$ (&sect;1.2). Multiply by $e^{-\\lambda^2t/2}$: $M_\\lambda(s)$.</div></details>", "7.4": "\n      <h4>7.4 &nbsp;Martingale Test for $f(t,W_t)$ — It&ocirc; drift $=0$</h4>\n      <div class=\"eq\">$df = \\Big(\\partial_t f + \\tfrac12 \\partial_{ww} f\\Big)dt + \\partial_w f\\, dW$</div>\n      <div class=\"eq\">martingale $\\iff \\partial_t f + \\tfrac12\\partial_{ww} f = 0$</div>\n      <div class=\"eq\">special case $f = e^{\\lambda t}g(w)$: &nbsp; MG $\\iff \\lambda g + \\tfrac12 g'' = 0$</div>\n      <div class=\"use\"><b>Use when:</b> every &ldquo;Is $Y_t := \\ldots$ a martingale? (Yes/No)&rdquo;. For $g = \\sin$ or $\\cos$, $g'' = -g$, so the <b>only</b> working exponent is $\\lambda = \\tfrac12$.</div>\n      <div class=\"ex\"><b>Paper:</b> CT-1&rsquo;26 Q5 &mdash; $e^{t/2}\\sin W_t$ <b>Yes</b>; $e^{t}\\cos W_t$ <b>No</b>; $e^{t}\\sin W_t$ <b>No</b>; $e^{t/2}\\cos W_t$ <b>Yes</b>.<br><b>One-second rule:</b> exponent $\\tfrac12$ &rarr; Yes, anything else &rarr; No.</div>\n      <details class=\"proof\"><summary>Proof</summary><div>Itô Form 2 (&sect;I3.3) gives $df$; a process is a MG iff its $dt$ coefficient is $0$ (&sect;I5.1). Special case $f=e^{\\lambda t}g(w)$: $\\partial_tf=\\lambda e^{\\lambda t}g$, $\\partial_{ww}f=e^{\\lambda t}g''$, so the drift is $e^{\\lambda t}(\\lambda g+\\tfrac12g'')$.</div></details>", "7.5": "\n      <h4>7.5 &nbsp;It&ocirc; Integral — Always a Martingale, Always Normal</h4>\n      <div class=\"eq\">$\\int_0^T f(t)\\,dW_t \\sim N\\!\\Big(0,\\ \\int_0^T f(t)^2\\,dt\\Big)$</div>\n      <div class=\"eq\">It&ocirc; isometry: $E\\Big[\\big(\\int_0^T H_t dW_t\\big)^2\\Big] = E\\int_0^T H_t^2\\,dt$</div>\n      <div class=\"cond\">Normality needs $f$ <em>deterministic</em>; the martingale/isometry part holds for adapted $H$.</div>\n      <div class=\"use\"><b>Use when:</b> &ldquo;compute the distribution of $\\int_0^1 t^k dW_t$&rdquo;. Mean is $0$ free; variance is a one-line calculus integral.</div>\n      <div class=\"ex\"><b>Paper:</b> CT-1&rsquo;26 Q1 &mdash; $\\int_0^1 t^k dW_t \\sim N\\big(0,\\tfrac{1}{2k+1}\\big)$: $k{=}1\\!\\to\\!\\mathbf{N(0,1/3)}$, $k{=}2\\!\\to\\!\\mathbf{1/5}$, $k{=}3\\!\\to\\!\\mathbf{1/7}$, $k{=}4\\!\\to\\!\\mathbf{1/9}$. CT-2&rsquo;25 Q7: $\\int_0^1 t^2dW_t\\sim N(0,1/5)$.</div>\n      <details class=\"proof\"><summary>Proof</summary><div>Normal: &sect;I1.5. Isometry: &sect;I1.4. Martingale: &sect;I1.3 (open their proofs).</div></details>", "7.6": "\n      <h4>7.6 &nbsp;Conditional Expectations You Can Quote</h4>\n      <div class=\"eq\">$E[W_t\\mid\\mathcal F_s] = W_s$</div>\n      <div class=\"eq\">$E[W_t^2\\mid\\mathcal F_s] = W_s^2 + (t-s)$</div>\n      <div class=\"eq\">$E[e^{W_t}\\mid\\mathcal F_s] = e^{W_s + (t-s)/2}$</div>\n      <div class=\"eq\">$E[e^{\\lambda W_t}\\mid\\mathcal F_s] = e^{\\lambda W_s + \\lambda^2(t-s)/2}$</div>\n      <div class=\"use\"><b>Use when:</b> &ldquo;filtration expectation&rdquo; questions. All four come from &sect;6.3 + &sect;1.2 in one step.</div>\n      <div class=\"ex\"><b>Q:</b> find $E[W_3^2 \\mid \\mathcal F_1]$ &rarr; $\\mathbf{W_1^2 + 2}$.<br><b>Trick:</b> all four lines are &sect;6.3 (split the increment) followed by &sect;1.2 (exponential moment). Derive them, don&rsquo;t memorize them.</div>\n      <details class=\"proof\"><summary>Proof</summary><div>Split $W_t=W_s+\\Delta$ with $\\Delta\\sim N(0,t-s)$ independent of $\\mathcal F_s$: lines 1&ndash;2 are &sect;7.1&ndash;7.2. Lines 3&ndash;4: $E[e^{\\lambda W_t}\\mid\\mathcal F_s]=e^{\\lambda W_s}E[e^{\\lambda\\Delta}]=e^{\\lambda W_s+\\lambda^2(t-s)/2}$, with $\\lambda=1$ for line 3.</div></details>", "8.1": "\n      <h4>8.1 &nbsp;Density of $W(t)$</h4>\n      <div class=\"eq\">$f_{W(t)}(x) = \\dfrac{1}{\\sqrt{2\\pi t}}\\,e^{-x^2/(2t)}$</div>\n      <div class=\"eq\">$P(W(t) \\le x) = \\Phi\\!\\left(\\dfrac{x}{\\sqrt t}\\right)$</div>\n      <div class=\"use\"><b>Use when:</b> $W$ alone, no maximum, no barrier. <b>Always divide by $\\sqrt t$, never $t$.</b></div>\n      <div class=\"ex\"><b>Q:</b> $P(W(4) &gt; 3) = 1 - \\Phi(3/2) = \\mathbf{0.0668}$.<br><b>Trick:</b> $\\sqrt t = 2$, <b>not</b> $4$. Dividing by $t$ instead of $\\sqrt t$ is the single most common lost mark on this paper.</div>\n      <details class=\"proof\"><summary>Proof</summary><div>$W(t)\\sim N(0,t)$: that is the normal density with variance $t$. $P(W(t)\\le x)=P(\\sqrt tZ\\le x)=\\Phi(x/\\sqrt t)$.</div></details>", "8.2": "\n      <h4>8.2 &nbsp;Transition Density</h4>\n      <div class=\"eq\">$p(t;x,y) = \\dfrac{1}{\\sqrt{2\\pi t}}\\exp\\!\\Big(-\\dfrac{(y-x)^2}{2t}\\Big)$</div>\n      <div class=\"use\"><b>Use when:</b> BM started at $x\\neq0$. Everything else is unchanged &mdash; recentre and reuse every formula.</div>\n      <div class=\"ex\"><b>Q:</b> BM started at $5$; $P(W(1) &lt; 7) = \\Phi(2) = \\mathbf{0.9772}$.<br><b>Trick:</b> subtract the starting point first &mdash; then every other formula on this sheet applies unchanged.</div>\n      <details class=\"proof\"><summary>Proof</summary><div>Given $W(s)=x$: $W(s+t)=x+(W(s+t)-W(s))$, and the increment is $N(0,t)$, independent of the past. So $W(s+t)\\sim N(x,t)$.</div></details>", "8.3": "\n      <h4>8.3 &nbsp;Diffusion (Heat) Equation</h4>\n      <div class=\"eq\">$\\dfrac{\\partial p}{\\partial t} = \\dfrac12\\,\\dfrac{\\partial^2 p}{\\partial y^2}$</div>\n      <div class=\"use\"><b>Use when:</b> &ldquo;show the density satisfies the heat equation&rdquo;. Differentiate 8.1 twice; the $\\tfrac12$ is the same $\\tfrac12$ as in It&ocirc;&rsquo;s formula.</div>\n      <div class=\"ex\"><b>Q:</b> &ldquo;show the transition density satisfies the heat equation&rdquo;.<br><b>Trick:</b> $\\partial_{yy}$ brings down $\\big(\\tfrac{(y-x)^2}{t^2} - \\tfrac1t\\big)$; $\\partial_t$ brings down exactly half of it. One line each, then compare.</div>\n      <details class=\"proof\"><summary>Proof</summary><div>Put $u=y-x$, $p=(2\\pi t)^{-1/2}e^{-u^2/(2t)}$. Then $\\partial_tp=p\\big(-\\tfrac1{2t}+\\tfrac{u^2}{2t^2}\\big)$ and $\\partial_yp=-\\tfrac utp$, $\\partial_{yy}p=p\\big(\\tfrac{u^2}{t^2}-\\tfrac1t\\big)$. Compare: $\\partial_tp=\\tfrac12\\partial_{yy}p$.</div></details>", "8.4": "\n      <h4>8.4 &nbsp;Chapman&ndash;Kolmogorov</h4>\n      <div class=\"eq\">$p(t+s;x,z) = \\displaystyle\\int_{\\mathbb R} p(t;x,y)\\,p(s;y,z)\\,dy$</div>\n      <div class=\"use\"><b>Use when:</b> justifying the Markov property, or short-note questions on why independent increments force Gaussian transitions.</div>\n      <div class=\"ex\"><b>Q:</b> &ldquo;why <em>must</em> the increments be Gaussian?&rdquo;<br><b>Trick:</b> Chapman&ndash;Kolmogorov plus stationary independent increments plus continuity forces the normal family. That sentence is the short-note answer.</div>\n      <details class=\"proof\"><summary>Proof</summary><div>Go from $x$ to $z$ in time $t+s$ via the position $y$ at time $t$: condition on $W(t)=y$, then the next stretch is an independent increment. Total probability over $y$ gives the integral. (Both sides are the $N(x,t+s)$ density at $z$.)</div></details>", "9.1": "\n      <h4>9.1 &nbsp;Which Direction Are You Conditioning?</h4>\n      <div class=\"eq\">Forward (know past, want future) &rarr; 9.2</div>\n      <div class=\"eq\">Backward (know endpoint, want interior) &rarr; 9.4</div>\n      <div class=\"use\"><b>Use when:</b> <b>decide this first.</b> Forward is trivial (martingale, variance grows). Backward is the bridge (variance shrinks to $0$ at both ends).</div>\n      <div class=\"ex\"><b>Q:</b> &ldquo;Given $W(1)=2$, find $E[W(3)]$&rdquo; &rarr; $\\mathbf{2}$ (martingale). &ldquo;Given $W(3)=2$, find $E[W(1)]$&rdquo; &rarr; $\\tfrac13(2) = \\mathbf{2/3}$ (bridge).<br><b>Trick:</b> identify which time is <em>known</em> before you write a single symbol.</div>", "9.2": "\n      <h4>9.2 &nbsp;Forward Conditional</h4>\n      <div class=\"eq\">$W(t)\\mid \\mathcal F_s \\sim N\\big(W(s),\\ t-s\\big), \\qquad t&gt;s$</div>\n      <div class=\"use\"><b>Use when:</b> future given present. Independent increments, nothing more.</div>\n      <div class=\"ex\"><b>Q:</b> $W(5) \\mid W(2) = 3 \\sim \\mathbf{N(3,\\,3)}$.<br><b>Trick:</b> the mean stays exactly where you left it; the variance is the elapsed time and nothing else.</div>\n      <details class=\"proof\"><summary>Proof</summary><div>$W(t)=W(s)+(W(t)-W(s))$: $W(s)$ is known, the increment is $N(0,t-s)$ and independent of $\\mathcal F_s$.</div></details>", "9.3": "\n      <h4>9.3 &nbsp;Standard Brownian Bridge</h4>\n      <div class=\"eq\">$B(s) = W(s) - \\dfrac{s}{t}W(t), \\quad 0\\le s\\le t$</div>\n      <div class=\"eq\">$E[B(s)] = 0, \\qquad \\operatorname{Cov}(B(u),B(v)) = \\min(u,v) - \\dfrac{uv}{t}$</div>\n      <div class=\"eq\">$B \\perp W(t)$</div>\n      <div class=\"use\"><b>Use when:</b> the process is pinned at both ends. The $-uv/t$ term is exactly the information removed by knowing the endpoint.</div>\n      <div class=\"ex\"><b>Q:</b> $\\operatorname{Var}(B(t/2))$ on $[0,t]$ $= \\tfrac t2 - \\tfrac{(t/2)^2}{t} = \\mathbf{t/4}$.<br><b>Trick:</b> bridge variance is zero at both ends and peaks at the midpoint &mdash; a 2-second sanity check on any bridge answer.</div>\n      <details class=\"proof\"><summary>Proof</summary><div>Mean: linear in $W$. For $u,v\\le t$: $\\operatorname{Cov}(B_u,B_v)=\\min(u,v)-\\tfrac vt\\min(u,t)-\\tfrac ut\\min(v,t)+\\tfrac{uv}{t^2}t=\\min(u,v)-\\tfrac{uv}t$.<br>$\\operatorname{Cov}(B(s),W(t))=s-\\tfrac st\\,t=0$; jointly normal, so independent.</div></details>", "9.4": "\n      <h4>9.4 &nbsp;Backward Interpolation — the workhorse</h4>\n      <div class=\"eq\">$W(s)\\mid W(t){=}b \\;\\sim\\; N\\!\\left(\\dfrac{s}{t}\\,b,\\ \\dfrac{s(t-s)}{t}\\right), \\qquad s&lt;t$</div>\n      <div class=\"use\"><b>Use when:</b> &ldquo;given $W(t)=b$, find $P(W(s)&lt;x)$ / $E[W(s)]$ / $\\operatorname{Var}$&rdquo;. Mean is the straight line from $(0,0)$ to $(t,b)$; variance vanishes at both ends and peaks at the midpoint.</div>\n      <div class=\"ex\"><b>Trick:</b> derive it in 10 seconds from &sect;1.9 with $\\Sigma = \\begin{pmatrix} s & s\\\\ s& t\\end{pmatrix}$: mean $= \\frac st b$, var $= s - \\frac{s^2}{t}$.</div>\n      <details class=\"proof\"><summary>Proof</summary><div>$W(s)=B(s)+\\tfrac stW(t)$ with $B(s)\\perp W(t)$ (&sect;9.3). Given $W(t)=b$: mean $\\tfrac stb$, variance $\\operatorname{Var}B(s)=s-\\tfrac{s^2}t=\\tfrac{s(t-s)}t$.</div></details>", "9.5": "\n      <h4>9.5 &nbsp;Bridge Pinned $a \\to b$</h4>\n      <div class=\"eq\">$X(s) \\sim N\\!\\left(a + \\dfrac{s}{t}(b-a),\\ \\dfrac{s(t-s)}{t}\\right)$</div>\n      <div class=\"cond\">$X(0)=a$, $X(t)=b$.</div>\n      <div class=\"use\"><b>Use when:</b> the process does not start at $0$. Only the mean line shifts; the variance is untouched.</div>\n      <div class=\"ex\"><b>Q:</b> $W(0)=2$, $W(10)=8$; find the law of $W(4)$. Mean $= 2 + \\tfrac{4}{10}(6) = \\mathbf{4.4}$, Var $= \\tfrac{4(6)}{10} = \\mathbf{2.4}$.<br><b>Trick:</b> the mean is plain linear interpolation between the two pins.</div>\n      <details class=\"proof\"><summary>Proof</summary><div>Write $X(s)=a+W(s)$ conditioned on $W(t)=b-a$. By &sect;9.4: mean $a+\\tfrac st(b-a)$, variance $\\tfrac{s(t-s)}t$.</div></details>", "9.6": "\n      <h4>9.6 &nbsp;Scaled BM ($Y = \\sigma W$)</h4>\n      <div class=\"eq\">$Y(s)\\mid Y(t){=}b \\sim N\\!\\left(\\dfrac st b,\\ \\sigma^2\\dfrac{s(t-s)}{t}\\right)$</div>\n      <div class=\"use\"><b>Use when:</b> volatility $\\sigma\\ne1$. <b>Mean is unchanged</b> (ratios cancel); only variance picks up $\\sigma^2$.</div>\n      <div class=\"ex\"><b>Q:</b> $Y = 3W$ with $Y(10) = 12$; find the law of $Y(4)$. Mean $= \\tfrac{4}{10}(12) = \\mathbf{4.8}$, Var $= 9\\cdot\\tfrac{4(6)}{10} = \\mathbf{21.6}$.<br><b>Trick:</b> $\\sigma^2$ scales the variance and leaves the mean untouched.</div>\n      <details class=\"proof\"><summary>Proof</summary><div>$Y(t)=b\\iff W(t)=b/\\sigma$. Then $Y(s)=\\sigma W(s)$ has mean $\\sigma\\cdot\\tfrac st\\cdot\\tfrac b\\sigma=\\tfrac stb$ and variance $\\sigma^2\\tfrac{s(t-s)}t$ (&sect;9.4).</div></details>", "10.1": "\n      <h4>10.1 &nbsp;Definition</h4>\n      <div class=\"eq\">$X(t) = \\mu t + \\sigma W(t) \\sim N(\\mu t,\\ \\sigma^2 t)$</div>\n      <div class=\"eq\">$X(t) - X(s) \\sim N\\big(\\mu(t-s),\\ \\sigma^2(t-s)\\big)$, independent increments</div>\n      <div class=\"use\"><b>Use when:</b> &ldquo;drift coefficient / drift parameter $\\mu$ and variance parameter $\\sigma^2$&rdquo;. Careful: some questions give $\\sigma^2$, some give $\\sigma$.</div>\n      <div class=\"ex\"><b>Paper:</b> Midsem&rsquo;25 Q1(a) says &ldquo;drift coefficient $-1$&rdquo; &rarr; $\\mu{=}{-}1$, $\\sigma{=}1$. CT-1&rsquo;26 Q6 says &ldquo;volatility parameter $\\sigma = 0.3$&rdquo; &rarr; that is $\\sigma$, not $\\sigma^2$.<br><b>Trick:</b> check whether they handed you $\\sigma$ or $\\sigma^2$ &mdash; the exit formulas all want $\\sigma^2$.</div>\n      <details class=\"proof\"><summary>Proof</summary><div>$\\sigma W(t)\\sim N(0,\\sigma^2t)$, shift by $\\mu t$. Increments: $X(t)-X(s)=\\mu(t-s)+\\sigma(W_t-W_s)$, inheriting independence from $W$.</div></details>", "10.2": "\n      <h4>10.2 &nbsp;Which Exit Formula? — decision</h4>\n      <div class=\"eq\">$\\mu = 0$, two barriers &nbsp;&rarr;&nbsp; 10.4 (distance ratio)</div>\n      <div class=\"eq\">$\\mu \\ne 0$, two barriers &nbsp;&rarr;&nbsp; 10.3 (exponentials)</div>\n      <div class=\"eq\">$\\mu &lt; 0$, <em>one</em> upper barrier, no lower &nbsp;&rarr;&nbsp; 10.5</div>\n      <div class=\"eq\">$\\mu \\ge 0$, one upper barrier &nbsp;&rarr;&nbsp; probability $= 1$</div>\n      <div class=\"use\"><b>Use when:</b> run this fork before writing anything. Most lost marks are from using 10.3 when $\\mu=0$ (it is $0/0$).</div>\n      <div class=\"ex\"><b>Paper:</b> Midsem&rsquo;24 Q1(c) gives $\\mu = 0$, so 10.4 applies and 10.3 does not.<br><b>Trick:</b> at $\\mu = 0$ the exponential formula is literally $0/0$. Check the drift <em>before</em> choosing a formula, every time.</div>", "10.3": "\n      <h4>10.3 &nbsp;Two-Barrier Exit — Drift $\\ne 0$</h4>\n      <div class=\"eq\">$P(\\text{hit } A \\text{ before } -B \\mid X(0){=}0) = \\dfrac{1 - e^{-2\\mu B/\\sigma^2}}{1 - e^{-2\\mu(A+B)/\\sigma^2}}$</div>\n      <div class=\"eq\">from $X(0)=x$, barriers $A$ (up) and $-B$ (down): replace $B \\to x+B$</div>\n      <div class=\"cond\">$A,B&gt;0$, $\\mu\\ne0$.</div>\n      <div class=\"use\"><b>Use when:</b> continuous-time gambler&rsquo;s ruin. It is &sect;3.3 with $(q/p)^i \\to e^{-2\\mu x/\\sigma^2}$.</div>\n      <div class=\"ex\"><b>Q:</b> $\\mu = 0.1$, $\\sigma = 1$, barriers $+2$ and $-3$ from $0$.<br>$P = \\dfrac{1-e^{-0.6}}{1-e^{-1}} = \\dfrac{0.4512}{0.6321} = \\mathbf{0.714}$.<br><b>Trick:</b> set $r = 2\\mu/\\sigma^2$ once, then it reads $\\frac{1-e^{-rB}}{1-e^{-r(A+B)}}$.</div>\n      <details class=\"proof\"><summary>Proof</summary><div>With $\\theta=-2\\mu/\\sigma^2$, $e^{\\theta X_t}$ has Itô drift $e^{\\theta X}(\\theta\\mu+\\tfrac12\\theta^2\\sigma^2)=0$: a MG, bounded between the barriers. OST (&sect;M6.7) with $p=P(\\text{hit }A\\text{ first})$:<br>$1=p\\,e^{\\theta A}+(1-p)e^{-\\theta B}\\Rightarrow p=\\frac{1-e^{-\\theta B}}{e^{\\theta A}-e^{-\\theta B}}$. Multiply top and bottom by $e^{\\theta B}$: $\\frac{e^{\\theta B}-1}{e^{\\theta(A+B)}-1}=\\frac{1-e^{-2\\mu B/\\sigma^2}}{1-e^{-2\\mu(A+B)/\\sigma^2}}$. Starting at $x$: shift everything by $x$.</div></details>", "10.4": "\n      <h4>10.4 &nbsp;Two-Barrier Exit — Zero Drift</h4>\n      <div class=\"eq\">$P(\\text{hit } A \\text{ before } -B \\mid X(0){=}0) = \\dfrac{B}{A+B}$</div>\n      <div class=\"eq\">$P(\\text{hit } -B \\text{ first}) = \\dfrac{A}{A+B}$</div>\n      <div class=\"use\"><b>Use when:</b> $\\mu = 0$. <b>Trick:</b> the probability of hitting a barrier is proportional to the distance to the <em>opposite</em> barrier. $\\sigma$ cancels and never appears.</div>\n      <div class=\"ex\"><b>Paper:</b> Midsem&rsquo;24 Q1(c) &mdash; $B(t)$, $\\mu{=}0$, $\\sigma^2{=}1$, &ldquo;reaches $-2$ before $1$&rdquo;. Up-barrier $A{=}1$, down-barrier $B{=}2$. $P(\\text{down first}) = \\frac{A}{A+B} = \\frac{1}{3}$ &rArr; $\\mathbf{1/3}$.</div>\n      <details class=\"proof\"><summary>Proof</summary><div>$X=\\sigma W$ is a MG trapped between the barriers. OST: $0=pA-(1-p)B$, so $p=\\frac{B}{A+B}$.</div></details>", "10.5": "\n      <h4>10.5 &nbsp;Negative Drift — Maximum Ever Reached</h4>\n      <div class=\"eq\">$P\\Big(\\max_{t\\ge0} X(t) \\ge a\\Big) = e^{2\\mu a/\\sigma^2} = e^{-2|\\mu|a/\\sigma^2}, \\qquad \\mu&lt;0,\\ a&gt;0$</div>\n      <div class=\"eq\">$\\displaystyle\\max_{t\\ge0}X(t) \\sim \\mathrm{Exp}\\!\\left(\\frac{2|\\mu|}{\\sigma^2}\\right)$</div>\n      <div class=\"use\"><b>Use when:</b> only an upper target, drift pulls down, infinite horizon. If $\\mu\\ge0$ the probability is $1$ (the process reaches every level a.s.).</div>\n      <div class=\"ex\"><b>Trick:</b> memoryless &rArr; the all-time max is Exponential. That is what makes the optimization in 10.6 a one-line calculus problem.</div>\n      <details class=\"proof\"><summary>Proof</summary><div>Take &sect;10.3 with upper barrier $a$ and lower barrier $-B$, then $B\\to\\infty$. With $c=2|\\mu|/\\sigma^2&gt;0$ ($\\mu&lt;0$): $p=\\frac{e^{cB}-1}{e^{c(a+B)}-1}\\to e^{-ca}$. So $P(\\max\\ge a)=e^{-ca}$, which is the tail of $\\mathrm{Exp}(c)$.</div></details>", "10.6": "\n      <h4>10.6 &nbsp;Optimal Exercise Threshold (drift $-\\mu$)</h4>\n      <div class=\"eq\">maximize $\\ g(x) = (x-K)\\,e^{-2\\mu x/\\sigma^2}$ over $x \\ge K$</div>\n      <div class=\"eq\">$x^{*} = K + \\dfrac{\\sigma^2}{2\\mu}, \\qquad g(x^{*}) = \\dfrac{\\sigma^2}{2\\mu}\\,e^{-2\\mu K/\\sigma^2 - 1}$</div>\n      <div class=\"cond\">$\\sigma = 1$ case: $x^{*} = K + \\frac{1}{2\\mu}$.</div>\n      <div class=\"use\"><b>Use when:</b> &ldquo;option to buy at fixed price $K$; price follows BM with drift coefficient $-\\mu$; when, if ever, should we exercise?&rdquo;<br><b>Set-up:</b> payoff $x-K$, probability of ever reaching $x$ is $e^{-2\\mu x/\\sigma^2}$ (10.5). Differentiate, done.</div>\n      <div class=\"ex\"><b>Paper:</b> Midsem&rsquo;25 Q1(a) &mdash; $K=100$, drift $-1$ (so $\\mu{=}1$, $\\sigma{=}1$), start $0$. $x^{*} = 100 + \\tfrac12 = \\mathbf{100.5}$. Exercise the first time the price hits $100.5$.</div>\n      <details class=\"proof\"><summary>Proof</summary><div>Waiting until $X$ first reaches $x$ pays $x-K$ and happens with probability $e^{-cx}$, $c=2\\mu/\\sigma^2$ (&sect;10.5). So maximise $g(x)=(x-K)e^{-cx}$: $g'(x)=e^{-cx}\\big(1-c(x-K)\\big)=0\\Rightarrow x^{*}=K+\\tfrac1c=K+\\tfrac{\\sigma^2}{2\\mu}$ ($g'$ changes from $+$ to $-$: a max). $g(x^{*})=\\tfrac1ce^{-cK-1}$.</div></details>", "10.7": "\n      <h4>10.7 &nbsp;Hitting a Sloping Line — via OST</h4>\n      <div class=\"eq\">$T = \\min\\{t: W_t = a + bt\\}$</div>\n      <div class=\"eq\">$E[W_T] = 0 \\Rightarrow a + b\\,E[T] = 0 \\Rightarrow E[T] = -\\dfrac{a}{b}$</div>\n      <div class=\"cond\">Needs $ab &lt; 0$ (line slopes back toward $0$), else $E[T]=\\infty$.</div>\n      <div class=\"use\"><b>Use when:</b> the barrier <em>moves</em>. Apply OST to the martingale $W_t$ at the stopping time, substitute the barrier equation, solve for $E[T]$.</div>\n      <div class=\"ex\"><b>Paper:</b> Endsem&rsquo;25 Q1(d) &mdash; $T = \\min\\{t: W_t = 2 - 3t\\}$. $0 = 2 - 3E[T] \\Rightarrow E[T] = \\mathbf{2/3}$.</div>\n      <details class=\"proof\"><summary>Proof</summary><div>Same as &sect;M6.5: $E[W_T]=0$ and $W_T=a+bT$ on the line, so $0=a+bE[T]$.</div></details>", "10.8": "\n      <h4>10.8 &nbsp;Expected Exit Time (two barriers)</h4>\n      <div class=\"eq\">$\\mu = 0:\\quad E[T] = \\dfrac{A\\,B}{\\sigma^2}$ &nbsp; (from $X(0)=0$)</div>\n      <div class=\"eq\">$\\mu \\ne 0:\\quad E[T] = \\dfrac{A\\,p - B(1-p)}{\\mu}$, &nbsp; $p$ from 10.3</div>\n      <div class=\"use\"><b>Use when:</b> duration asked as well as probability. $\\mu{=}0$ case uses the martingale $X_t^2 - \\sigma^2 t$; $\\mu\\ne0$ uses $X_t - \\mu t$ (Wald).</div>\n      <div class=\"ex\"><b>Check:</b> $\\mu=0$, $\\sigma=1$, $A=B=n$ gives $E[T]=n^2$ &mdash; the discrete $i(N-i)$ of &sect;3.2 with $i=n,N=2n$.</div>\n      <details class=\"proof\"><summary>Proof</summary><div>$\\mu=0$: $X^2-\\sigma^2t$ is a MG, so $\\sigma^2E[T]=E[X_T^2]=A^2p+B^2(1-p)=AB$ with $p=\\frac B{A+B}$.<br>$\\mu\\ne0$: $X_t-\\mu t$ is a MG, so $E[X_T]=\\mu E[T]$, and $E[X_T]=Ap-B(1-p)$.</div></details>", "11.1": "\n      <h4>11.1 &nbsp;⚠ Two Conventions for &ldquo;drift parameter $\\mu$&rdquo;</h4>\n      <div class=\"eq\"><b>(R) Ross / log-drift:</b> $\\;Y(t) = Y(0)\\,e^{\\mu t + \\sigma W_t}$</div>\n      <div class=\"eq\"><b>(S) SDE:</b> $\\;dY = \\mu Y\\,dt + \\sigma Y\\,dW \\;\\Rightarrow\\; Y(t) = Y(0)e^{(\\mu - \\sigma^2/2)t + \\sigma W_t}$</div>\n      <div class=\"bad\"><b>The paper accepts BOTH.</b> CT-1&rsquo;26 Q6 official answer key literally reads &ldquo;$5e^{0.55}$ <b>or</b> $5e^{0.1}$&rdquo;. <b>Write both, label them</b> &mdash; that is the full-marks answer.</div>\n      <div class=\"use\"><b>Tell them apart:</b> &ldquo;logarithm of the price is BM with drift $\\mu$&rdquo; &rArr; (R). &ldquo;$dS = \\mu S\\,dt + \\ldots$&rdquo; or &ldquo;expected return $\\mu$&rdquo; &rArr; (S).</div>\n      <div class=\"ex\"><b>Paper:</b> CT-1&rsquo;26 Q6, all four sets, and Midsem&rsquo;25 Q1(b).<br><b>Trick:</b> write both, labelled: &ldquo;log-drift convention: $\\ldots$ ; SDE convention: $\\ldots$&rdquo;. Two boxed expressions. The key accepts either, and labelling proves you know why there are two.</div>\n      <details class=\"proof\"><summary>Proof</summary><div>(S): apply Itô to the candidate $Y_0e^{(\\mu-\\sigma^2/2)t+\\sigma W_t}$: drift $(\\mu-\\tfrac{\\sigma^2}2)+\\tfrac{\\sigma^2}2=\\mu$, diffusion $\\sigma$. It solves $dY=\\mu Y\\,dt+\\sigma Y\\,dW$ (&sect;I4.6).</div></details>", "11.2": "\n      <h4>11.2 &nbsp;Mean of GBM</h4>\n      <div class=\"eq\">(R) $\\;E[Y(t)] = Y(0)\\,e^{(\\mu + \\sigma^2/2)\\,t}$</div>\n      <div class=\"eq\">(S) $\\;E[Y(t)] = Y(0)\\,e^{\\mu t}$</div>\n      <div class=\"use\"><b>Use when:</b> the single most repeated GBM question. Both are &sect;1.2 applied to $\\mu t + \\sigma W_t \\sim N(\\mu t, \\sigma^2 t)$.</div>\n      <div class=\"ex\"><b>Paper:</b> CT-1&rsquo;26 Q6 &mdash; $\\mu{=}.01,\\sigma{=}.3,Y_0{=}5$: $5e^{(.01+.045)10} = \\mathbf{5e^{0.55}}$ or $\\mathbf{5e^{0.1}}$. Set-2 $\\mu{=}.02,\\sigma{=}.4,Y_0{=}10$: $\\mathbf{10e^{1}}$ or $\\mathbf{10e^{0.2}}$. Set-3: $\\mathbf{5e^{0.5}}$ / $5e^{0.3}$. Set-4: $\\mathbf{5e^{0.3}}$ / $5e^{0.1}$.<br>Midsem&rsquo;25 Q1(b) &mdash; $\\mu{=}.01,\\sigma{=}.2,Y_0{=}10,t{=}100$: $\\mathbf{10e^{3}}$ or $10e^{1}$.</div>\n      <details class=\"proof\"><summary>Proof</summary><div>(R): $E[e^{\\mu t+\\sigma W_t}]=e^{\\mu t+\\sigma^2t/2}$ (&sect;1.2). (S): the exponent is $(\\mu-\\sigma^2/2)t+\\sigma W_t$, so the $\\sigma^2/2$ cancels: $e^{\\mu t}$.</div></details>", "11.3": "\n      <h4>11.3 &nbsp;Filtration Expectation of GBM</h4>\n      <div class=\"eq\">(R) $\\;E[Y(t)\\mid\\mathcal F_s] = Y(s)\\,e^{(\\mu+\\sigma^2/2)(t-s)}$</div>\n      <div class=\"eq\">(S) $\\;E[Y(t)\\mid\\mathcal F_s] = Y(s)\\,e^{\\mu(t-s)}$</div>\n      <div class=\"use\"><b>Use when:</b> conditional version. <b>Trick:</b> same formula with $Y(0)\\to Y(s)$ and $t \\to t-s$ &mdash; GBM is multiplicatively memoryless.</div>\n      <div class=\"ex\"><b>Q:</b> $E[Y(10)\\mid\\mathcal F_4]$ with (R), $\\mu{=}0.01$, $\\sigma{=}0.2$ &rarr; $Y(4)\\,e^{(0.01+0.02)(6)} = \\mathbf{Y(4)e^{0.18}}$.<br><b>Trick:</b> same formula as 11.2 with $Y(0)\\to Y(s)$ and $t \\to t-s$.</div>\n      <details class=\"proof\"><summary>Proof</summary><div>$Y(t)=Y(s)\\,e^{\\mu(t-s)+\\sigma(W_t-W_s)}$. Take out $Y(s)$; the increment is independent: $E[e^{\\sigma\\Delta}]=e^{\\sigma^2(t-s)/2}$. (S) is the same with $\\mu-\\sigma^2/2$ in place of $\\mu$.</div></details>", "11.4": "\n      <h4>11.4 &nbsp;GBM Martingale Condition</h4>\n      <div class=\"eq\">(R) MG $\\iff \\mu = -\\dfrac{\\sigma^2}{2}$ &nbsp;&nbsp; (S) MG $\\iff \\mu = 0$</div>\n      <div class=\"use\"><b>Use when:</b> &ldquo;is the discounted/undiscounted price a martingale?&rdquo;. Same $-\\sigma^2/2$ as the geometric random walk (&sect;2.3).</div>\n      <div class=\"ex\"><b>Q:</b> &ldquo;Is $e^{W_t - t/2}$ a martingale?&rdquo; &rarr; <b>Yes</b>: it is (R) with $\\mu = -\\tfrac12 = -\\sigma^2/2$, $\\sigma = 1$.<br><b>Trick:</b> it is precisely the exponential martingale of &sect;7.3 at $\\lambda = 1$. Same object, two names.</div>\n      <details class=\"proof\"><summary>Proof</summary><div>From &sect;11.3: $E[Y(t)\\mid\\mathcal F_s]=Y(s)$ iff the exponent is $0$: $\\mu+\\sigma^2/2=0$ (R), $\\mu=0$ (S).</div></details>", "11.5": "\n      <h4>11.5 &nbsp;Higher Moments &amp; Variance</h4>\n      <div class=\"eq\">(R) $\\;E[Y(t)^n] = Y(0)^n\\,e^{(n\\mu + n^2\\sigma^2/2)t}$</div>\n      <div class=\"eq\">$\\operatorname{Var}(Y(t)) = E[Y(t)]^2\\big(e^{\\sigma^2 t} - 1\\big)$</div>\n      <div class=\"use\"><b>Use when:</b> variance or second moment requested. Note $\\operatorname{Var}$ is convention-free once written in terms of $E[Y(t)]$.</div>\n      <div class=\"ex\"><b>Q:</b> (R) with $\\mu{=}0$, $\\sigma{=}1$, $Y_0{=}1$, $t{=}1$: $E[Y] = e^{1/2}$, so $\\operatorname{Var} = e(e-1) = \\mathbf{4.67}$.<br><b>Trick:</b> get $E[Y(t)]$ first, square it, multiply by $(e^{\\sigma^2 t}-1)$. Convention-free once written that way.</div>\n      <details class=\"proof\"><summary>Proof</summary><div>$Y^n=Y_0^ne^{n\\mu t+n\\sigma W_t}$, and $E[e^{n\\sigma W_t}]=e^{n^2\\sigma^2t/2}$. Variance: $E[Y^2]-(EY)^2=Y_0^2e^{2\\mu t+2\\sigma^2t}-Y_0^2e^{2\\mu t+\\sigma^2t}=(EY)^2(e^{\\sigma^2t}-1)$.</div></details>", "11.6": "\n      <h4>11.6 &nbsp;Median vs Mean</h4>\n      <div class=\"eq\">(R) median $= Y(0)e^{\\mu t}$, &nbsp; mean $= Y(0)e^{(\\mu+\\sigma^2/2)t}$</div>\n      <div class=\"use\"><b>Use when:</b> conceptual questions on why a GBM can have growing mean but drift to $0$ almost surely: for $\\mu &lt; 0 &lt; \\mu + \\sigma^2/2$, median $\\to 0$ while mean $\\to\\infty$.</div>\n      <div class=\"ex\"><b>Q:</b> (R) with $\\mu = -0.1$, $\\sigma = 0.6$: does the stock grow? The mean grows ($\\mu + \\sigma^2/2 = 0.08 &gt; 0$) while the median $\\to 0$.<br><b>Trick:</b> that gap <em>is</em> the log-normal. Expect a conceptual mark on it.</div>\n      <details class=\"proof\"><summary>Proof</summary><div>$\\ln Y(t)\\sim N(\\ln Y_0+\\mu t,\\sigma^2t)$ is symmetric, so its median is its mean. $e^x$ is increasing, so the median of $Y$ is $e^{\\text{median of }\\ln Y}=Y_0e^{\\mu t}$. The mean has the extra $e^{\\sigma^2t/2}$ (&sect;11.2).</div></details>", "11.7": "\n      <h4>11.7 &nbsp;Distribution &amp; Probabilities</h4>\n      <div class=\"eq\">$\\ln\\dfrac{Y(t)}{Y(0)} \\sim N\\big(\\mu t,\\ \\sigma^2 t\\big)$ &nbsp;(R)</div>\n      <div class=\"eq\">$P(Y(t) &gt; K) = 1 - \\Phi\\!\\left(\\dfrac{\\ln(K/Y_0) - \\mu t}{\\sigma\\sqrt t}\\right)$</div>\n      <div class=\"use\"><b>Use when:</b> &ldquo;probability the price exceeds $K$&rdquo;. Take logs first, <em>then</em> standardize. This is the $d_2$ of Black&ndash;Scholes.</div>\n      <div class=\"ex\"><b>Q:</b> $S_0 = 100$, (R) $\\mu = 0.05$, $\\sigma = 0.2$, $t = 1$. $P(S_1 &gt; 110) = 1 - \\Phi\\!\\big(\\tfrac{\\ln 1.1 - 0.05}{0.2}\\big) = 1-\\Phi(0.227) = \\mathbf{0.410}$.<br><b>Trick:</b> take logs <em>first</em>, then standardize. That expression is Black&ndash;Scholes&rsquo; $d_2$.</div>\n      <details class=\"proof\"><summary>Proof</summary><div>$\\ln\\frac{Y(t)}{Y_0}=\\mu t+\\sigma W_t\\sim N(\\mu t,\\sigma^2t)$. $P(Y&gt;K)=P\\big(\\mu t+\\sigma\\sqrt tZ&gt;\\ln\\tfrac K{Y_0}\\big)=P\\Big(Z&gt;\\frac{\\ln(K/Y_0)-\\mu t}{\\sigma\\sqrt t}\\Big)$.</div></details>", "12.1": "\n      <h4>12.1 &nbsp;Hitting Time &amp; the Key Equivalence</h4>\n      <div class=\"eq\">$\\tau_a = \\inf\\{t \\ge 0 : W(t) = a\\}$</div>\n      <div class=\"eq\">$\\{\\tau_a \\le t\\} = \\{M(t) \\ge a\\}$ &nbsp; for $a &gt; 0$</div>\n      <div class=\"use\"><b>Use when:</b> this equivalence is the bridge between &sect;12 and &sect;13 &mdash; every maximum question is a hitting-time question and vice versa. For $a&lt;0$ use $\\tau_a \\overset{d}{=} \\tau_{|a|}$ by symmetry.</div>\n      <div class=\"ex\"><b>Q:</b> $P(M(4) \\ge 3)$ and $P(\\tau_3 \\le 4)$ are the same number.<br><b>Trick:</b> whenever you see one, silently rewrite it as the other and use whichever formula you actually remember. They are interchangeable by definition.</div>\n      <details class=\"proof\"><summary>Proof</summary><div>If $M(t)\\ge a&gt;0=W(0)$, the continuous path climbs from $0$ to $\\ge a$ by time $t$, so by the intermediate value theorem it equals $a$ at some time $\\le t$: $\\tau_a\\le t$. Conversely $W(\\tau_a)=a$ gives $M(t)\\ge a$.</div></details>", "12.2": "\n      <h4>12.2 &nbsp;Reflection Principle</h4>\n      <div class=\"eq\">$P\\big(W(t)\\le a-y,\\ M(t) &gt; a\\big) = P\\big(W(t) \\ge a+y\\big) = 1-\\Phi\\!\\left(\\dfrac{a+y}{\\sqrt t}\\right)$</div>\n      <div class=\"cond\"><b>Requires $a&gt;0$ and $y\\ge0$.</b></div>\n      <div class=\"use\"><b>Use when:</b> the engine for all of &sect;13&ndash;14. Reflect the path after $\\tau_a$ across the line $W=a$; the strong Markov property makes the reflected path an equally likely BM.<br><b>Why $a&gt;0$:</b> it forces $\\tau_a\\le t$ automatically, so that condition can be dropped.</div>\n      <div class=\"ex\"><b>Q:</b> $P(W(4) \\le 1,\\ M(4) &gt; 2)$: read off $a = 2$, $y = 2-1 = 1$ &rarr; $1 - \\Phi(3/2) = \\mathbf{0.0668}$.<br><b>Trick:</b> $a$ is the barrier, $y$ is barrier minus the $W$-bound. If $y &lt; 0$ you are in the third branch of &sect;14.1 instead.</div>\n      <details class=\"proof\"><summary>Proof</summary><div>Let $\\tau_a$ be the first time $W$ hits $a$. On $\\{M(t)&gt;a\\}$ we have $\\tau_a&lt;t$.<br>\nReflect the path after $\\tau_a$: $\\widetilde W(s)=W(s)$ for $s\\le\\tau_a$, $\\widetilde W(s)=2a-W(s)$ after. By symmetry and the fresh start at $\\tau_a$, $\\widetilde W$ is again a BM.<br>\nReflection swaps $\\{W(t)\\le a-y\\}$ with $\\{\\widetilde W(t)\\ge a+y\\}$, and every path ending at $\\ge a+y$ has already passed $a$. So $P(W(t)\\le a-y,\\,M(t)&gt;a)=P(W(t)\\ge a+y)=1-\\Phi\\big(\\tfrac{a+y}{\\sqrt t}\\big)$.</div></details>", "12.3": "\n      <h4>12.3 &nbsp;cdf of $\\tau_a$</h4>\n      <div class=\"eq\">$P(\\tau_a \\le t) = 2\\Big(1-\\Phi\\!\\Big(\\dfrac{a}{\\sqrt t}\\Big)\\Big) = 2\\,\\Phi\\!\\Big(-\\dfrac{a}{\\sqrt t}\\Big)$</div>\n      <div class=\"cond\">$a&gt;0$; for general $a$ replace $a$ by $|a|$.</div>\n      <div class=\"use\"><b>Use when:</b> &ldquo;find the cdf of $\\tau_a$&rdquo;. <b>Trick:</b> set $y=0$ in 12.2 and double for the symmetric half $\\{W(t)&gt;a\\}$.</div>\n      <div class=\"ex\"><b>Paper:</b> Midsem&rsquo;25 Q5(a) &mdash; &ldquo;find the cdf and pdf of $\\tau_a$&rdquo;. Quote 12.3 then differentiate for 12.4.</div>\n      <details class=\"proof\"><summary>Proof</summary><div>$\\{\\tau_a\\le t\\}=\\{M(t)\\ge a\\}$. Split on where $W(t)$ ends:<br>\n$P(M(t)\\ge a)=P(W(t)\\ge a)+P\\big(W(t)&lt;a,\\,M(t)\\ge a\\big)$; the first term needs no $M$ (ending above $a$ means you crossed it).<br>\nReflection (&sect;12.2 with $y=0$): the second term $=P(W(t)&gt;a)$. Total $=2P(W(t)\\ge a)=2\\big(1-\\Phi(a/\\sqrt t)\\big)$.</div></details>", "12.4": "\n      <h4>12.4 &nbsp;pdf of $\\tau_a$ (Lévy distribution)</h4>\n      <div class=\"eq\">$f_{\\tau_a}(t) = \\dfrac{|a|}{\\sqrt{2\\pi}\\;t^{3/2}}\\,e^{-a^2/(2t)}, \\qquad t&gt;0$</div>\n      <div class=\"use\"><b>Use when:</b> follows from 12.3 by $\\frac{d}{dt}$. <b>Trick:</b> differentiate $2\\Phi(-a t^{-1/2})$ using the chain rule; the $t^{-3/2}$ comes from $\\frac{d}{dt}t^{-1/2}$.</div>\n      <div class=\"ex\"><b>Paper:</b> Midsem&rsquo;25 Q5(a), second half. $f_{\\tau_2}(4) = \\dfrac{2}{\\sqrt{2\\pi}\\,8}e^{-1/2} = \\mathbf{0.0605}$.<br><b>Trick:</b> differentiate 12.3 &mdash; never try to integrate the joint density for this.</div>\n      <details class=\"proof\"><summary>Proof</summary><div>Differentiate the cdf $2\\Phi(-a\\,t^{-1/2})$: $\\frac{d}{dt}=2\\varphi(a/\\sqrt t)\\cdot\\tfrac a2t^{-3/2}=\\frac{a}{\\sqrt{2\\pi}\\,t^{3/2}}e^{-a^2/(2t)}$.</div></details>", "12.5": "\n      <h4>12.5 &nbsp;Certain Hitting, Infinite Mean</h4>\n      <div class=\"eq\">$P(\\tau_a &lt; \\infty) = \\lim_{t\\to\\infty} 2\\Phi\\!\\Big(-\\dfrac{a}{\\sqrt t}\\Big) = 2\\Phi(0) = 1$</div>\n      <div class=\"eq\">$E[\\tau_a] = \\infty$ &nbsp; (the $t^{-3/2}$ tail is not integrable)</div>\n      <div class=\"use\"><b>Use when:</b> &ldquo;$P(T_a &lt; \\infty) = ?$&rdquo; &rarr; <b>1</b>. BM hits every level with certainty, but takes infinitely long on average. Both facts are asked.</div>\n      <div class=\"ex\"><b>Paper:</b> Midsem&rsquo;24 Q1(f) &mdash; &ldquo;$T_a$ = time to hit $a$; find $P(T_a&lt;\\infty)$&rdquo; &rArr; $\\mathbf{1}$.</div>\n      <details class=\"proof\"><summary>Proof</summary><div>$P(\\tau_a&lt;\\infty)=\\lim_tP(\\tau_a\\le t)=2\\Phi(0)=1$. Mean: $E\\tau_a=\\int_0^\\infty P(\\tau_a&gt;t)\\,dt$, and $P(\\tau_a&gt;t)=2\\Phi(a/\\sqrt t)-1\\approx\\frac{2a}{\\sqrt{2\\pi t}}$ for large $t$. $\\int^\\infty t^{-1/2}dt=\\infty$.</div></details>", "12.6": "\n      <h4>12.6 &nbsp;Scaling of $\\tau_a$</h4>\n      <div class=\"eq\">$\\tau_a \\overset{d}{=} a^2\\,\\tau_1$</div>\n      <div class=\"use\"><b>Use when:</b> comparing hitting times of different levels, or sanity-checking: doubling the barrier quadruples the typical time.</div>\n      <div class=\"ex\"><b>Q:</b> &ldquo;$E[\\tau_1] = \\infty$ &mdash; what about $\\tau_{10}$?&rdquo; Also $\\infty$, and typically $100\\times$ longer.<br><b>Trick:</b> $\\tau_a \\overset{d}{=} a^2\\tau_1$ is the same $\\sqrt t$ scaling as &sect;4.1, seen from the other side.</div>\n      <details class=\"proof\"><summary>Proof</summary><div>$\\widetilde W(u)=\\tfrac1aW(a^2u)$ is a BM (scaling, &sect;5.4). $W$ hits $a$ at time $a^2u$ exactly when $\\widetilde W$ hits $1$ at time $u$. So $\\tau_a=a^2\\widetilde\\tau_1$, and $\\widetilde\\tau_1\\overset d=\\tau_1$.</div></details>", "12.7": "\n      <h4>12.7 &nbsp;Laplace Transform of $\\tau_a$</h4>\n      <div class=\"eq\">$E\\big[e^{-\\lambda \\tau_a}\\big] = e^{-a\\sqrt{2\\lambda}}, \\qquad \\lambda&gt;0,\\ a&gt;0$</div>\n      <div class=\"use\"><b>Use when:</b> a discounted payoff at a hitting time. Derived by OST on the exponential martingale (&sect;7.3) with $\\lambda_{\\text{mg}}=\\sqrt{2\\lambda}$.</div>\n      <div class=\"ex\"><b>Q:</b> value of Rs 1 paid the instant the price first hits $a$, discounted at rate $\\lambda$ &rarr; $\\mathbf{e^{-a\\sqrt{2\\lambda}}}$.<br><b>Trick:</b> apply OST to $\\exp(\\theta W_t - \\tfrac{\\theta^2}{2}t)$ with $\\theta = \\sqrt{2\\lambda}$, so the $t$-term becomes exactly the discount factor.</div>\n      <details class=\"proof\"><summary>Proof</summary><div>$M_t=e^{\\theta W_t-\\theta^2t/2}$ ($\\theta&gt;0$) is a MG (&sect;7.3) and stays $\\le e^{\\theta a}$ up to $\\tau_a$. OST: $1=E\\big[e^{\\theta a-\\theta^2\\tau_a/2}\\big]$, so $E[e^{-(\\theta^2/2)\\tau_a}]=e^{-\\theta a}$. Put $\\lambda=\\theta^2/2$, $\\theta=\\sqrt{2\\lambda}$.</div></details>", "13.1": "\n      <h4>13.1 &nbsp;Definition &amp; Hard Constraints</h4>\n      <div class=\"eq\">$M(t) = \\max_{0\\le u \\le t} W(u)$</div>\n      <div class=\"eq\">$M(t) \\ge W(t)$ &nbsp;and&nbsp; $M(t) \\ge 0$ &nbsp;<b>always</b></div>\n      <div class=\"use\"><b>Use when:</b> check these two first. Any event forcing $W(t) &gt; M(t)$, or $M(t) &lt; 0$, has probability <b>zero</b> &mdash; write &ldquo;$0$&rdquo; and move on.</div>\n      <div class=\"ex\"><b>Q:</b> $P(2 &lt; W(4) &lt; 3,\\ 0 &lt; M(4) &lt; 1) = \\mathbf{0}$.<br><b>Trick:</b> it demands $W &gt; M$, which is impossible. Spot it, write $0$, move on &mdash; this is a deliberate trap and free marks.</div>\n      <details class=\"proof\"><summary>Proof</summary><div>The max over $[0,t]$ is at least the value at $u=t$, which is $W(t)$, and at $u=0$, which is $0$.</div></details>", "13.2": "\n      <h4>13.2 &nbsp;Distribution of $M(t)$ — half-normal</h4>\n      <div class=\"eq\">$M(t) \\overset{d}{=} |W(t)|$</div>\n      <div class=\"eq\">$P(M(t) \\ge a) = 2\\Big(1 - \\Phi\\!\\Big(\\dfrac{a}{\\sqrt t}\\Big)\\Big), \\qquad a\\ge0$</div>\n      <div class=\"eq\">$P(M(t) \\le a) = 2\\Phi\\!\\Big(\\dfrac{a}{\\sqrt t}\\Big) - 1$</div>\n      <div class=\"use\"><b>Use when:</b> the question involves $M$ but <b>not</b> $W$. One line, no reflection needed.</div>\n      <div class=\"ex\"><b>Interval:</b> $P(c &lt; M(t) \\le d) = 2\\big[\\Phi(d/\\sqrt t) - \\Phi(c/\\sqrt t)\\big]$. For $t{=}4$, $(c,d)=(2,3)$: $2[\\Phi(1.5)-\\Phi(1)] = 2[.9332-.8413] = \\mathbf{0.1837}$.</div>\n      <details class=\"proof\"><summary>Proof</summary><div>$P(M\\ge a)=P(W\\ge a)+P(W&lt;a,M\\ge a)=2P(W(t)\\ge a)$ (reflection, see &sect;12.3 proof) $=P(|W(t)|\\ge a)$. The second line is $1-$ the first.</div></details>", "13.3": "\n      <h4>13.3 &nbsp;Density of $M(t)$</h4>\n      <div class=\"eq\">$f_{M(t)}(a) = \\dfrac{2}{\\sqrt{2\\pi t}}\\,e^{-a^2/(2t)}, \\qquad a\\ge0$</div>\n      <div class=\"use\"><b>Use when:</b> an expectation over $M$ is needed. It is twice the $N(0,t)$ density on the positive half-line.</div>\n      <div class=\"ex\"><b>Q:</b> &ldquo;compute $E[M(4)]$ by integrating the density&rdquo;.<br><b>Trick:</b> don&rsquo;t &mdash; quote &sect;13.4. Reach for the density only when the functional is non-standard (e.g. $E[e^{M(t)}]$).</div>\n      <details class=\"proof\"><summary>Proof</summary><div>Differentiate $P(M\\le a)=2\\Phi(a/\\sqrt t)-1$: $\\frac{2}{\\sqrt t}\\varphi(a/\\sqrt t)=\\frac{2}{\\sqrt{2\\pi t}}e^{-a^2/(2t)}$.</div></details>", "13.4": "\n      <h4>13.4 &nbsp;Moments of $M(t)$</h4>\n      <div class=\"eq\">$E[M(t)] = \\sqrt{\\dfrac{2t}{\\pi}}, \\qquad E[M(t)^2] = t$</div>\n      <div class=\"eq\">$\\operatorname{Var}(M(t)) = t\\Big(1 - \\dfrac{2}{\\pi}\\Big)$</div>\n      <div class=\"use\"><b>Use when:</b> &ldquo;expected maximum&rdquo;. Note $E[M(t)^2]=E[W(t)^2]=t$ because $M\\overset{d}{=}|W|$.</div>\n      <div class=\"ex\"><b>Q:</b> &ldquo;expected highest level reached in 4 units of time&rdquo; $= \\sqrt{8/\\pi} = \\mathbf{1.596}$.<br><b>Trick:</b> $E[M(t)] = \\sqrt{2t/\\pi} \\approx 0.8\\sqrt t$ &mdash; useful for checking an answer&rsquo;s order of magnitude.</div>\n      <details class=\"proof\"><summary>Proof</summary><div>$M(t)\\overset d=|W(t)|=\\sqrt t|Z|$. $E|Z|=\\sqrt{2/\\pi}$ (&sect;E2.4), so $E M=\\sqrt{2t/\\pi}$; $EM^2=E W^2=t$; $\\operatorname{Var}=t-\\tfrac{2t}\\pi$.</div></details>", "13.5": "\n      <h4>13.5 &nbsp;Running Minimum</h4>\n      <div class=\"eq\">$m(t) = \\min_{0\\le u\\le t}W(u) \\overset{d}{=} -M(t)$</div>\n      <div class=\"eq\">$P(m(t) \\le -a) = 2\\Big(1-\\Phi\\!\\Big(\\dfrac{a}{\\sqrt t}\\Big)\\Big), \\quad a \\ge 0$</div>\n      <div class=\"use\"><b>Use when:</b> a minimum or a lower barrier appears. Flip signs everywhere ($W\\to-W$) and reuse &sect;13&ndash;14 verbatim.</div>\n      <div class=\"ex\"><b>Q:</b> $P\\big(\\min_{u\\le4} W(u) \\le -3\\big) = 2(1-\\Phi(1.5)) = \\mathbf{0.1336}$.<br><b>Trick:</b> reflect the entire problem ($W \\to -W$) and reuse &sect;13.2 with $+3$. Never derive a separate minimum formula.</div>\n      <details class=\"proof\"><summary>Proof</summary><div>$\\min W=-\\max(-W)$ and $-W$ is a BM (symmetry). So $m(t)\\overset d=-M(t)$ and $P(m\\le-a)=P(M\\ge a)$.</div></details>", "14.1": "\n      <h4>14.1 &nbsp;⚠ The Guard Everyone Gets Wrong</h4>\n      <div class=\"eq\">$G(b,c) := P\\big(W(t)\\le b,\\ M(t)\\ge c\\big)$</div>\n      <div class=\"eq\">$\\boxed{\\;G(b,c)=\\begin{cases} \\Phi\\!\\big(\\tfrac{b}{\\sqrt t}\\big) & c \\le 0\\\\[6pt] 1 - \\Phi\\!\\big(\\tfrac{2c-b}{\\sqrt t}\\big) & 0 &lt; c,\\ b \\le c\\\\[6pt] 1 + \\Phi\\!\\big(\\tfrac{b}{\\sqrt t}\\big) - 2\\Phi\\!\\big(\\tfrac{c}{\\sqrt t}\\big) & 0 &lt; c &lt; b\\end{cases}}$</div>\n      <div class=\"bad\"><b>The $b&gt;c$ case is NOT zero.</b> The reflection formula $1-\\Phi(\\frac{2c-b}{\\sqrt t})$ is only valid while $b\\le c$. If $b &gt; c$, split at $c$: once $W(t)&gt;c$ the constraint $M(t)\\ge c$ is automatic, so<br>$G = \\underbrace{[1-\\Phi(\\tfrac{c}{\\sqrt t})]}_{W\\le c} + \\underbrace{[\\Phi(\\tfrac{b}{\\sqrt t})-\\Phi(\\tfrac{c}{\\sqrt t})]}_{c&lt;W\\le b}$.</div>\n      <div class=\"use\"><b>Sanity checks:</b> at $b=c$ both branches give $1-\\Phi(c/\\sqrt t)$ ✓. As $b\\to\\infty$: $G\\to 2(1-\\Phi(c/\\sqrt t)) = P(M\\ge c)$ ✓ (&sect;13.2).</div>\n      <div class=\"ex\"><b>Q:</b> this guard alone decides Midsem&rsquo;25 Q5(b) &mdash; see 14.4.<br><b>Trick:</b> before evaluating any $G$, <b>write down the comparison of $b$ with $c$</b> on your script. $b \\le c$ &rarr; reflection formula; $b &gt; c$ &rarr; the three-term form.</div>\n      <details class=\"proof\"><summary>Proof</summary><div><b>$c\\le0$:</b> $M\\ge0\\ge c$ always, so $G=P(W\\le b)$.<br><b>$0&lt;c$, $b\\le c$:</b> reflection at level $c$ (&sect;12.2, $y=c-b$): $P(W\\le b,M\\ge c)=P(W\\ge2c-b)$.<br><b>$0&lt;c&lt;b$:</b> split $\\{W\\le c\\}$ and $\\{c&lt;W\\le b\\}$. The first is the previous case with $b=c$: $1-\\Phi(\\tfrac c{\\sqrt t})$. On the second $W&gt;c$ already forces $M\\ge c$: $\\Phi(\\tfrac b{\\sqrt t})-\\Phi(\\tfrac c{\\sqrt t})$. Add.</div></details>", "14.2": "\n      <h4>14.2 &nbsp;Joint Density</h4>\n      <div class=\"eq\">$f_{W,M}(w,m) = \\dfrac{2(2m-w)}{t\\sqrt{2\\pi t}}\\;\\exp\\!\\left(-\\dfrac{(2m-w)^2}{2t}\\right)$</div>\n      <div class=\"cond\">Support: $m \\ge \\max(0, w)$. Zero elsewhere.</div>\n      <div class=\"use\"><b>Use when:</b> the region is not a rectangle, or an expectation like $E[M(t)W(t)]$ is asked. For rectangles use 14.3 &mdash; it is far faster.</div>\n      <div class=\"ex\"><b>Q:</b> $E[M(4)W(4)]$, or any non-rectangular region.<br><b>Trick:</b> the substitution $u = 2m - w$ turns every integral of this density into a standard normal one &mdash; that substitution is exactly how &sect;14.1 is derived.</div>\n      <details class=\"proof\"><summary>Proof</summary><div>For $m&gt;0$, $w\\le m$: $H(w,m):=P(W\\le w,M\\ge m)=1-\\Phi(u)$, $u=\\frac{2m-w}{\\sqrt t}$. Density $=-\\partial_m\\partial_wH$. $\\partial_wH=\\varphi(u)/\\sqrt t$; then $\\partial_m$ gives $\\varphi'(u)\\tfrac2t=-u\\varphi(u)\\tfrac2t$. So $f=\\frac{2u}{t}\\varphi(u)=\\frac{2(2m-w)}{t\\sqrt{2\\pi t}}e^{-(2m-w)^2/(2t)}$.</div></details>", "14.3": "\n      <h4>14.3 &nbsp;⭐ Master Recipe — any rectangle, any position</h4>\n      <div class=\"eq\">$P\\big(a &lt; W(t) \\le b,\\ c &lt; M(t)\\le d\\big)$</div>\n      <div class=\"eq\">$=\\big[G(b,c) - G(a,c)\\big] - \\big[G(b,d) - G(a,d)\\big]$</div>\n      <div class=\"cond\">$G$ from 14.1. Strict vs non-strict inequalities are irrelevant (continuous laws).</div>\n      <div class=\"use\"><b>Use when:</b> <b>always.</b> This one line replaces the whole &ldquo;is $I$ above / below / overlapping $J$&rdquo; case tree &mdash; the branching lives inside $G$, so you never classify anything.<br><b>Four evaluations of $G$, one subtraction. That is the entire problem.</b></div>\n      <div class=\"ex\"><b>Trick:</b> tabulate $G(b,c), G(a,c), G(b,d), G(a,d)$ in a $2\\times2$ box, then (top-left − bottom-left) − (top-right − bottom-right).</div>\n      <details class=\"proof\"><summary>Proof</summary><div>$P(a&lt;W\\le b,\\ M\\ge c)=G(b,c)-G(a,c)$. Then $\\{c&lt;M\\le d\\}=\\{M&gt;c\\}\\setminus\\{M&gt;d\\}$, so subtract the same thing at level $d$. ($M$ is continuous, so $\\ge$ vs $&gt;$ makes no difference.)</div></details>", "14.4": "\n      <h4>14.4 &nbsp;⭐ Worked: Midsem&rsquo;25 Q5(b)</h4>\n      <div class=\"eq\">$P\\big(1 &lt; W(4) &lt; 3,\\ 2 &lt; M(4) &lt; 3\\big)$</div>\n      <div class=\"eq\">$t=4,\\ \\sqrt t = 2;\\quad a{=}1,\\ b{=}3,\\ c{=}2,\\ d{=}3$</div>\n      <div class=\"ex\">\n      $G(3,2)$: $0&lt;c{=}2&lt;b{=}3$ &rarr; branch 3: $1+\\Phi(1.5)-2\\Phi(1) = 1+.9332-1.6827 = .2505$<br>\n      $G(1,2)$: $b{=}1\\le c{=}2$ &rarr; branch 2: $1-\\Phi(\\tfrac{4-1}{2}) = 1-\\Phi(1.5) = .0668$<br>\n      $G(3,3)$: $b{=}3\\le c{=}3$ &rarr; branch 2: $1-\\Phi(\\tfrac{6-3}{2}) = 1-\\Phi(1.5) = .0668$<br>\n      $G(1,3)$: $b{=}1\\le c{=}3$ &rarr; branch 2: $1-\\Phi(\\tfrac{6-1}{2}) = 1-\\Phi(2.5) = .0062$<br>\n      <br>\n      $\\big[.2505-.0668\\big] - \\big[.0668-.0062\\big] = .1837 - .0606 = \\mathbf{0.1231}$\n      </div>\n      <div class=\"use\"><b>Note:</b> $G(3,2)$ hits the $b&gt;c$ branch. Using the naive reflection formula there gives $1-\\Phi(\\tfrac{4-3}{2})=1-\\Phi(.5)=.3085$ and a wrong final answer of $0.1811$. <b>This single guard is the whole question.</b></div>", "14.5": "\n      <h4>14.5 &nbsp;Shortcut — $I$ entirely below $J$</h4>\n      <div class=\"eq\">$b \\le c,\\ c&gt;0:$</div>\n      <div class=\"eq\">$P = \\Phi\\!\\Big(\\tfrac{2d-b}{\\sqrt t}\\Big) - \\Phi\\!\\Big(\\tfrac{2c-b}{\\sqrt t}\\Big) + \\Phi\\!\\Big(\\tfrac{2c-a}{\\sqrt t}\\Big) - \\Phi\\!\\Big(\\tfrac{2d-a}{\\sqrt t}\\Big)$</div>\n      <div class=\"use\"><b>Use when:</b> $I=(a,b]$ lies entirely below $J=(c,d]$. This is 14.3 with all four $G$&rsquo;s on branch 2 &mdash; write it directly if you spot $b\\le c$.</div>\n      <div class=\"ex\"><b>Q:</b> $P(1 &lt; W(4) \\le 2,\\ 2 &lt; M(4) \\le 3)$: here $b{=}2 \\le c{=}2$ ✓, so<br>$\\Phi(2)-\\Phi(1)+\\Phi(1.5)-\\Phi(2.5) = \\mathbf{0.0753}$.<br><b>Trick:</b> this is exactly the lower half of Midsem&rsquo;25 Q5(b); the upper half $(2,3)$ needs the $b&gt;c$ branch.</div>\n      <details class=\"proof\"><summary>Proof</summary><div>$b\\le c&lt;d$, so all four $G$ terms in &sect;14.3 are in case 2 of &sect;14.1. Substitute $G(x,y)=1-\\Phi(\\frac{2y-x}{\\sqrt t})$; the 1&rsquo;s cancel and the four $\\Phi$ terms remain.</div></details>", "14.6": "\n      <h4>14.6 &nbsp;Shortcut — $I$ entirely above $J$</h4>\n      <div class=\"eq\">$a \\ge d \\;\\Longrightarrow\\; P\\big(W(t)\\in I,\\ M(t)\\in J\\big) = 0$</div>\n      <div class=\"use\"><b>Use when:</b> would need $W(t) &gt; M(t)$ &mdash; impossible (&sect;13.1). Also $d\\le 0 \\Rightarrow 0$, since $M \\ge 0$. Spot these before computing anything.</div>\n      <div class=\"ex\"><b>Q:</b> $P(3 &lt; W(4) &lt; 4,\\ 1 &lt; M(4) &lt; 2) = \\mathbf{0}$, since $a{=}3 \\ge d{=}2$.<br><b>Trick:</b> test $a \\ge d$ and $d \\le 0$ first &mdash; two comparisons that can retire the whole question instantly.</div>\n      <details class=\"proof\"><summary>Proof</summary><div>$W\\in I$ means $W&gt;a\\ge d$; but $M\\ge W&gt;d$, so $M\\in J=(c,d]$ is impossible. Empty event.</div></details>", "14.7": "\n      <h4>14.7 &nbsp;Useful Special Cases of $G$</h4>\n      <div class=\"eq\">$P(W(t)\\le b,\\ M(t)\\ge c)$ with $b=c$: $\\;1-\\Phi\\!\\big(\\tfrac{c}{\\sqrt t}\\big)$</div>\n      <div class=\"eq\">$P(M(t)\\ge c) = G(\\infty,c) = 2\\big(1-\\Phi(\\tfrac{c}{\\sqrt t})\\big)$</div>\n      <div class=\"eq\">$P(W(t)\\le b,\\ M(t)&lt; c) = \\Phi\\!\\big(\\tfrac{b}{\\sqrt t}\\big) - G(b,c)$</div>\n      <div class=\"use\"><b>Use when:</b> the question gives a half-line instead of an interval &mdash; set the missing endpoint to $\\pm\\infty$ and use $\\Phi(\\pm\\infty) = 1$ or $0$.</div>\n      <div class=\"ex\"><b>Q:</b> $P(W(4) \\le 1,\\ M(4) &lt; 2) = \\Phi(0.5) - G(1,2) = 0.6915 - 0.0668 = \\mathbf{0.6247}$.<br><b>Trick:</b> &ldquo;the max stayed below $c$&rdquo; is the marginal of $W$ minus $G$. Half-lines are just $\\pm\\infty$ endpoints.</div>\n      <details class=\"proof\"><summary>Proof</summary><div>$b=c$ in case 2: $1-\\Phi(\\frac{2c-c}{\\sqrt t})$. $G(\\infty,c)$: case 3 with $b\\to\\infty$, $1+1-2\\Phi$. Last: $P(W\\le b)=P(W\\le b,M\\ge c)+P(W\\le b,M&lt;c)$.</div></details>", "14.8": "\n      <h4>14.8 &nbsp;Barrier-Option Translation</h4>\n      <div class=\"eq\">knock-out / knock-in payoffs $=$ rectangles in $(W,M)$</div>\n      <div class=\"eq\">$P(\\text{never hit } c,\\ W(t)\\in I) = P(W(t)\\in I) - G(\\cdot,c)$ terms</div>\n      <div class=\"use\"><b>Use when:</b> a question is dressed as a barrier option. Translate &ldquo;price never exceeded $c$&rdquo; into $\\{M(t) &lt; c\\}$ and run 14.3.</div>\n      <div class=\"ex\"><b>Q:</b> up-and-out call, barrier $3$, $S = e^{W}$ &rarr; $P\\big(M(4) &lt; \\ln 3,\\ W(4) \\in I\\big)$.<br><b>Trick:</b> &ldquo;the price never touched the barrier&rdquo; $\\iff \\{M(t) &lt; c\\}$. Translate, then run &sect;14.3 verbatim.</div>", "I1.1": "\n      <h4>I1.1 &nbsp;Definition — left endpoint</h4>\n      <div class=\"eq\">$\\displaystyle\\int_a^b f(t)\\,dW_t = \\lim_{\\text{mesh}\\to0}\\sum_{i=1}^{n} f(t_{i-1})\\big(W(t_i)-W(t_{i-1})\\big)$</div>\n      <div class=\"use\"><b>Use when:</b> the <b>left</b> endpoint $t_{i-1}$ is the whole definition. It makes the integrand <em>predictable</em> &mdash; you commit the stake before seeing the increment (&sect;M7.2). A midpoint rule would give a different answer (Stratonovich).</div>\n      <div class=\"ex\"><b>Trick:</b> for ordinary Riemann integrals the evaluation point does not matter in the limit. Here it does, because $\\Delta W \\sim \\sqrt{\\Delta t}$ is too big for the difference to vanish. Say that sentence in any &ldquo;define the It&ocirc; integral&rdquo; question.</div>", "I1.2": "\n      <h4>I1.2 &nbsp;Pathwise Formula (deterministic $f$)</h4>\n      <div class=\"eq\">$\\displaystyle\\int_a^b f(t)\\,dW_t = f(b)W(b) - f(a)W(a) - \\int_a^b W(t)f'(t)\\,dt$</div>\n      <div class=\"cond\">$f$ non-random with continuous derivative.</div>\n      <div class=\"use\"><b>Use when:</b> integration by parts, and note <b>no $dW$ remains on the right</b> &mdash; it is ordinary calculus path by path. Only works because $f$ is deterministic.</div>\n      <div class=\"ex\"><b>Q:</b> $\\displaystyle\\int_0^1 t\\,dW_t = W(1) - \\int_0^1 W(t)\\,dt$.<br><b>Trick:</b> $f\\equiv1$ gives $\\int_a^b dW = W(b)-W(a)$ &mdash; the sum telescopes.</div>\n      <details class=\"proof\"><summary>Proof</summary><div>Product rule with $f$ deterministic (no correction, $df\\,dW=0$): $d(fW)=f'(t)W_t\\,dt+f(t)\\,dW_t$. Integrate from $a$ to $b$ and rearrange.</div></details>", "I1.3": "\n      <h4>I1.3 &nbsp;Mean Zero &amp; Martingale</h4>\n      <div class=\"eq\">$E\\Big[\\displaystyle\\int_0^t H_s\\,dW_s\\Big] = 0$</div>\n      <div class=\"eq\">$\\Big(\\displaystyle\\int_0^t H_s\\,dW_s\\Big)_{t\\ge0}$ is a martingale &nbsp;($H$ adapted, square-integrable)</div>\n      <div class=\"use\"><b>Use when:</b> <b>every</b> expectation computation in Part III starts by killing the $dW$ term. Each summand has $E[f(t_{i-1})\\Delta W_i] = f(t_{i-1})\\cdot 0 = 0$ by independence.</div>\n      <div class=\"ex\"><b>Trick:</b> &ldquo;an It&ocirc; integral is a martingale&rdquo; is the continuous-time version of &ldquo;you cannot beat a fair game&rdquo; (&sect;M7.4). Predictable integrand against martingale increments &mdash; identical statement.</div>\n      <details class=\"proof\"><summary>Proof</summary><div>Simple $H$: $E[h_k\\Delta W_k\\mid\\mathcal F_{t_k}]=h_kE[\\Delta W_k]=0$, so each piece has conditional mean $0$; summing gives mean $0$. For $s&lt;t$, the pieces after $s$ have zero conditional mean given $\\mathcal F_s$: MG. General $H$: limits of simple ones.</div></details>", "I1.4": "\n      <h4>I1.4 &nbsp;⭐ It&ocirc; Isometry</h4>\n      <div class=\"eq\">$E\\Big[\\Big(\\displaystyle\\int_0^T H_s\\,dW_s\\Big)^{2}\\Big] = \\displaystyle\\int_0^T E\\big[H_s^{2}\\big]\\,ds$</div>\n      <div class=\"use\"><b>Use when:</b> any variance of a stochastic integral. It turns a <em>stochastic</em> second moment into an <b>ordinary</b> integral. Why it works: increments over disjoint intervals are independent, so all cross terms vanish and variances just add.</div>\n      <div class=\"ex\"><b>Q:</b> Endsem&rsquo;25 Q1(a) &mdash; $\\operatorname{Var}\\big(\\int_0^1 t^4dW_t\\big) = \\int_0^1 t^{8}dt = \\mathbf{1/9}$.<br><b>Trick:</b> square the integrand, integrate $dt$, done. No stochastic work at all.</div>\n      <details class=\"proof\"><summary>Proof</summary><div>Simple integrand: $H=h_k$ on $(t_k,t_{k+1}]$, $h_k$ known at $t_k$. Then $I=\\sum_k h_k\\,\\Delta W_k$.<br>\n$E[I^2]=\\sum_k E[h_k^2\\Delta W_k^2]+2\\sum_{j&lt;k}E[h_jh_k\\Delta W_j\\,\\Delta W_k]$.<br>\nCross terms: condition on $\\mathcal F_{t_k}$; $\\Delta W_k$ is independent of it with mean $0$, so each is $0$.<br>\nDiagonal: $E[h_k^2]\\,E[\\Delta W_k^2]=E[h_k^2](t_{k+1}-t_k)$. The sum is $\\int_0^T E[H_s^2]\\,ds$. General $H$: limit of simple ones.</div></details>", "I1.5": "\n      <h4>I1.5 &nbsp;⭐ Distribution (deterministic integrand)</h4>\n      <div class=\"eq\">$\\displaystyle\\int_0^T f(t)\\,dW_t \\;\\sim\\; N\\Big(0,\\ \\int_0^T f(t)^{2}\\,dt\\Big)$</div>\n      <div class=\"cond\"><b>$f$ must be non-random.</b> Sums of independent normals are normal, and $L^2$ limits of normals are normal.</div>\n      <div class=\"use\"><b>Use when:</b> &ldquo;compute the distribution of $\\int_0^1 t^k dW_t$&rdquo;. Mean $0$ is free; variance is a one-line calculus integral. Same card as &sect;7.5.</div>\n      <div class=\"ex\"><b>Paper:</b> $\\int_0^1 t^k dW_t \\sim N\\big(0,\\tfrac{1}{2k+1}\\big)$.<br>CT-1&rsquo;26: $k{=}1\\to\\mathbf{N(0,\\tfrac13)}$, $k{=}2\\to\\mathbf{\\tfrac15}$, $k{=}3\\to\\mathbf{\\tfrac17}$, $k{=}4\\to\\mathbf{\\tfrac19}$. CT-2&rsquo;25 Q7: $\\mathbf{N(0,\\tfrac15)}$. Endsem&rsquo;25 Q1(a): $\\mathbf{\\tfrac19}$.</div>\n      <details class=\"proof\"><summary>Proof</summary><div>Riemann sums $\\sum f(t_i)\\Delta W_i$ are sums of independent $N(0,f(t_i)^2\\Delta t)$, so $N\\big(0,\\sum f^2\\Delta t\\big)$. As the mesh $\\to0$ the variance $\\to\\int_0^Tf^2$, and an $L^2$ limit of normals is normal.</div></details>", "I1.6": "\n      <h4>I1.6 &nbsp;⚠ Random Integrand &rArr; NOT Normal</h4>\n      <div class=\"eq\">$\\displaystyle\\int_0^t W_s\\,dW_s$ is <b>not</b> $N\\big(0,\\int_0^t W_s^2\\,ds\\big)$</div>\n      <div class=\"bad\">That &ldquo;variance&rdquo; would itself be a random variable. Normality in I1.5 needs a <b>deterministic</b> integrand. The isometry (I1.4) survives, normality does not.</div>\n      <div class=\"use\"><b>Use when:</b> check before writing &ldquo;$\\sim N$&rdquo;: <b>does the integrand contain $W$ or $X$?</b> If yes, you may still take mean and variance, but not the distribution.</div>\n      <div class=\"ex\"><b>Q:</b> what <em>is</em> it? By I1.7, $\\int_0^t W\\,dW = \\tfrac12W_t^2 - \\tfrac t2$, so it is a shifted and scaled $\\chi^2_1$ &mdash; visibly skewed, not normal.</div>\n      <details class=\"proof\"><summary>Proof</summary><div>$\\int_0^tW\\,dW=\\tfrac12(W_t^2-t)\\ge-\\tfrac t2$ always. A normal variable takes arbitrarily negative values with positive probability. So it is not normal.</div></details>", "I1.7": "\n      <h4>I1.7 &nbsp;⭐ The Correction Term</h4>\n      <div class=\"eq\">$\\displaystyle\\int_0^t W_s\\,dW_s = \\tfrac12 W_t^{2} - \\tfrac12 t$</div>\n      <div class=\"use\"><b>Use when:</b> the signature result of the subject. Ordinary calculus would give $\\tfrac12W_t^2$; the extra $-\\tfrac t2$ is there because squared increments do <b>not</b> vanish.</div>\n      <div class=\"ex\"><b>Derivation:</b> $a(b-a) = \\tfrac12(b^2-a^2) - \\tfrac12(b-a)^2$; sum, telescope, and use $\\sum(\\Delta W)^2 \\to t$.<br><b>Check:</b> $E[\\tfrac12W_t^2 - \\tfrac t2] = \\tfrac t2 - \\tfrac t2 = 0$ ✓ &mdash; as an It&ocirc; integral must have. Without the $-\\tfrac t2$ the mean would be $\\tfrac t2 \\ne 0$.</div>\n      <details class=\"proof\"><summary>Proof</summary><div>Itô on $f(w)=w^2$: $d(W_t^2)=2W_t\\,dW_t+\\tfrac12\\cdot2\\,(dW_t)^2=2W_t\\,dW_t+dt$.<br>\nIntegrate from $0$ to $t$: $W_t^2=2\\int_0^tW_s\\,dW_s+t$, so $\\int_0^tW_s\\,dW_s=\\tfrac12W_t^2-\\tfrac12t$.<br>\n(From sums: $W_k(W_{k+1}-W_k)=\\tfrac12(W_{k+1}^2-W_k^2)-\\tfrac12(\\Delta W_k)^2$, and $\\sum(\\Delta W_k)^2\\to t$, &sect;I1.8.)</div></details>", "I1.8": "\n      <h4>I1.8 &nbsp;Quadratic Variation</h4>\n      <div class=\"eq\">$\\displaystyle\\sum_{j}(\\Delta W_j)^2 \\;\\longrightarrow\\; t$ &nbsp; (in $L^2$, by the LLN)</div>\n      <div class=\"eq\">$[W]_t = t$, &nbsp; i.e. $(dW)^2 = dt$</div>\n      <div class=\"use\"><b>Use when:</b> the <b>one fact</b> that generates all of It&ocirc; calculus. Proof: $\\Delta W_j \\overset{d}{=}\\sqrt{t/n}\\,Z_j$, so the sum is $t\\cdot\\tfrac1n\\sum Z_j^2 \\to t\\,E[Z^2] = t$.</div>\n      <div class=\"ex\"><b>Numeric feel:</b> $t{=}1$, $n{=}4$, $Z = 1.2,-0.5,0.8,-1.1$: $\\tfrac{1.44+0.25+0.64+1.21}{4} = 0.885 \\approx 1$ ✓<br><b>Trick:</b> BM has infinite total variation but finite quadratic variation. That is exactly why the second-order Taylor term survives.</div>\n      <details class=\"proof\"><summary>Proof</summary><div>$E\\sum(\\Delta W_j)^2=\\sum\\Delta t_j=t$ and $\\operatorname{Var}\\sum(\\Delta W_j)^2=\\sum2\\Delta t_j^2\\le2t\\cdot\\text{mesh}\\to0$. A variable with mean $t$ and variance $\\to0$ converges to $t$.</div></details>", "I2.1": "\n      <h4>I2.1 &nbsp;It&ocirc; Process</h4>\n      <div class=\"eq\">$X_t = X_0 + \\displaystyle\\int_0^t a_s\\,ds + \\int_0^t b_s\\,dW_s$</div>\n      <div class=\"eq\">shorthand: &nbsp; $dX_t = a_t\\,dt + b_t\\,dW_t$</div>\n      <div class=\"cond\">$a$ (drift) and $b$ (diffusion) adapted; the differential is <em>notation for the integral equation</em>, nothing more.</div>\n      <div class=\"use\"><b>Use when:</b> identify $a$ and $b$ first, always. Every subsequent formula is stated in terms of them.</div>\n      <div class=\"ex\"><b>Q:</b> $X = W$: $a{=}0$, $b{=}1$. &nbsp; $X_t = \\int_0^t s\\,dW_s$: $a{=}0$, $b_s{=}s$.<br>From I1.7: $W_t^2 = \\int_0^t 1\\,ds + \\int_0^t 2W_s\\,dW_s$, so $d(W_t^2) = dt + 2W_t\\,dW_t$ &mdash; $a{=}1$, $b{=}2W_t$.</div>", "I2.2": "\n      <h4>I2.2 &nbsp;⭐ Multiplication Table</h4>\n      <div class=\"eq\">$dt\\cdot dt = 0$</div>\n      <div class=\"eq\">$dt\\cdot dW = 0$</div>\n      <div class=\"eq\">$dW\\cdot dW = dt$</div>\n      <div class=\"use\"><b>Use when:</b> <b>memorize these three lines and It&ocirc;&rsquo;s formula is just Taylor.</b> Reason: $\\Delta W$ has size $\\sqrt{\\Delta t}$, so $(\\Delta W)^2 \\sim \\Delta t$ survives while $(\\Delta t)^2$ and $\\Delta t^{3/2}$ die.</div>\n      <div class=\"ex\"><b>Orders:</b> $\\sum(\\Delta t)^2 = t^2/n \\to 0$; &nbsp; $\\sum\\Delta t\\,\\Delta W \\approx \\tfrac tn W_t \\to 0$; &nbsp; $\\sum(\\Delta W)^2 \\to t$ ✓<br><b>Trick:</b> third order and beyond all vanish, which is why the Taylor expansion stops at two terms.</div>\n      <details class=\"proof\"><summary>Proof</summary><div>Sum over a partition of $[0,t]$. $\\sum(\\Delta W)^2\\to t$ (&sect;I1.8), so $dW\\,dW=dt$. $E\\big(\\sum\\Delta t\\,\\Delta W\\big)^2=\\sum\\Delta t^3\\le\\text{mesh}^2t\\to0$, so $dt\\,dW=0$. $\\sum\\Delta t^2\\le\\text{mesh}\\cdot t\\to0$, so $dt\\,dt=0$.</div></details>", "I2.3": "\n      <h4>I2.3 &nbsp;Square of an It&ocirc; Process</h4>\n      <div class=\"eq\">$(dX_t)^2 = (a\\,dt + b\\,dW)^2 = b_t^{2}\\,dt$</div>\n      <div class=\"use\"><b>Use when:</b> the plug-in for the correction term. <b>The drift never enters $(dX)^2$</b> &mdash; only the diffusion coefficient does, and it gets squared.</div>\n      <div class=\"ex\"><b>Q:</b> $dX = dt + 2\\sqrt{X}\\,dW \\Rightarrow (dX)^2 = 4X\\,dt$. &nbsp; $dN = rN\\,dt + \\alpha N\\,dW \\Rightarrow (dN)^2 = \\alpha^2N^2\\,dt$.</div>\n      <details class=\"proof\"><summary>Proof</summary><div>$(a\\,dt+b\\,dW)^2=a^2dt^2+2ab\\,dt\\,dW+b^2dW^2=0+0+b^2dt$ by the table.</div></details>", "I3.1": "\n      <h4>I3.1 &nbsp;⭐ The Recipe</h4>\n      <div class=\"eq\">1. Taylor-expand to <b>second</b> order</div>\n      <div class=\"eq\">2. apply $dt\\,dt = 0$, &nbsp; $dt\\,dW = 0$, &nbsp; $dW\\,dW = dt$</div>\n      <div class=\"eq\">3. collect $dt$ terms (drift) and $dW$ terms (diffusion)</div>\n      <div class=\"use\"><b>Use when:</b> <b>every</b> It&ocirc; question. You do not need to memorize the three forms below &mdash; they are all this recipe. Memorize the recipe and the table.</div>\n      <div class=\"ex\"><b>Paper:</b> Midsem&rsquo;25 Q2(b) asks you to <em>derive</em> It&ocirc;&rsquo;s formula [2 marks].<br><b>Trick:</b> state the Taylor expansion, state the table with the $\\sqrt{\\Delta t}$ reason, note that third order vanishes. That is the derivation.</div>\n      <details class=\"proof\"><summary>Proof</summary><div>Taylor: $f(t+dt,X+dX)-f(t,X)=\\partial_tf\\,dt+\\partial_xf\\,dX+\\tfrac12\\partial_{xx}f\\,(dX)^2+\\partial_{tx}f\\,dt\\,dX+\\tfrac12\\partial_{tt}f\\,dt^2+\\dots$<br>\nSizes: $dW\\approx\\sqrt{dt}$, so $(dW)^2$ has mean $dt$ and variance $2dt^2$: it behaves like $dt$ (not random to first order). $dt\\,dW\\approx dt^{3/2}$ and $dt^2$ are smaller than $dt$ and vanish.<br>\nKeep only $dt$ and $dW$ terms: $df=\\partial_tf\\,dt+\\partial_xf\\,dX+\\tfrac12\\partial_{xx}f\\,(dX)^2$. The $\\tfrac12 f''$ term is the correction ordinary calculus lacks.</div></details>", "I3.2": "\n      <h4>I3.2 &nbsp;Form 1 — $f(X_t)$</h4>\n      <div class=\"eq\">$df(X_t) = f'(X_t)\\,dX_t + \\tfrac12 f''(X_t)\\,b_t^{2}\\,dt$</div>\n      <div class=\"eq\">for $X = W$: &nbsp; $df(W_t) = f'(W_t)\\,dW_t + \\tfrac12 f''(W_t)\\,dt$</div>\n      <div class=\"use\"><b>Use when:</b> no explicit $t$ in $f$. The second line is the one you use most &mdash; the whole dictionary in &sect;I4 comes from it.</div>\n      <div class=\"ex\"><b>Q:</b> $f(x) = x^3$, $X = W$: $d(W_t^3) = 3W_t^2\\,dW_t + 3W_t\\,dt$.<br><b>Trick:</b> compare with ordinary calculus, which would give only $3W^2dW$. The extra $\\tfrac12 f''$ term <em>is</em> It&ocirc; calculus.</div>\n      <details class=\"proof\"><summary>Proof</summary><div>Taylor in $x$ only (&sect;I3.1): $df=f'\\,dX+\\tfrac12f''(dX)^2$, and $(dX)^2=b^2dt$ (&sect;I2.3). For $X=W$: $b=1$.</div></details>", "I3.3": "\n      <h4>I3.3 &nbsp;⭐ Form 2 — $f(t, X_t)$</h4>\n      <div class=\"eq\">$df = \\partial_t f\\,dt + \\partial_x f\\,dX_t + \\tfrac12\\,\\partial_{xx} f\\,b_t^{2}\\,dt$</div>\n      <div class=\"eq\">for $X = W$: &nbsp; $df = \\Big(\\partial_t f + \\tfrac12\\partial_{xx}f\\Big)dt + \\partial_x f\\,dW_t$</div>\n      <div class=\"use\"><b>Use when:</b> <b>the exam&rsquo;s standard form</b> &mdash; almost every question has an $e^{\\lambda t}$ out front. Note there is <b>no</b> $\\partial_{tt}$ term: only $x$ carries the $dW$.</div>\n      <div class=\"ex\"><b>Q:</b> $f = x^2 - t$, $X = W$: $dY = -dt + 2W\\,dW + dt = 2W_t\\,dW_t$. Zero drift ✓ consistent with $W_t^2 - t$ being a martingale (&sect;7.2).</div>\n      <details class=\"proof\"><summary>Proof</summary><div>&sect;I3.1 with $(dX)^2=b^2dt$; the cross terms $dt\\,dX$ vanish. For $X=W$: $b=1$, $dX=dW$.</div></details>", "I3.4": "\n      <h4>I3.4 &nbsp;Form 3 — Product Rule</h4>\n      <div class=\"eq\">$d(X_tY_t) = X_t\\,dY_t + Y_t\\,dX_t + dX_t\\,dY_t$</div>\n      <div class=\"eq\">$dX\\,dY = b_1b_2\\,dt$ &nbsp; (the It&ocirc; correction)</div>\n      <div class=\"use\"><b>Use when:</b> the product of two processes. It is the ordinary product rule <b>plus one cross term</b>. If either factor is deterministic, $b_1b_2 = 0$ and the correction disappears.</div>\n      <div class=\"ex\"><b>Q:</b> $d(tW_t) = W_t\\,dt + t\\,dW_t$ &mdash; no correction, since $t$ is deterministic.<br><b>Q:</b> $X{=}Y{=}W$: $d(W^2) = 2W\\,dW + dt$ ✓ recovers I2.1.</div>\n      <details class=\"proof\"><summary>Proof</summary><div>Two-variable form (&sect;I3.6) with $f=xy$: $f_x=y$, $f_y=x$, $f_{xy}=1$, $f_{xx}=f_{yy}=0$. So $d(XY)=Y\\,dX+X\\,dY+dX\\,dY$.</div></details>", "I3.5": "\n      <h4>I3.5 &nbsp;Form 4 — Quotient Rule</h4>\n      <div class=\"eq\">$d\\Big(\\dfrac{X_t}{Y_t}\\Big) = \\dfrac{dX_t}{Y_t} - \\dfrac{X_t}{Y_t^{2}}\\,dY_t + \\dfrac{X_t}{Y_t^{3}}(dY_t)^{2} - \\dfrac{1}{Y_t^{2}}\\,dX_t\\,dY_t$</div>\n      <div class=\"use\"><b>Use when:</b> rarely asked directly. Usually faster to write $X/Y = X\\cdot Y^{-1}$ and use I3.2 on $Y^{-1}$ then the product rule.</div>\n      <div class=\"ex\"><b>Q:</b> $X{=}1$, $Y = e^{W_t}$: $d(e^{-W_t}) = \\tfrac12e^{-W_t}\\,dt - e^{-W_t}\\,dW_t$.<br><b>Check:</b> that is GBM (&sect;I4) with $\\mu{=}0$, $\\sigma{=}-1$ ✓</div>\n      <details class=\"proof\"><summary>Proof</summary><div>&sect;I3.6 with $f=x/y$: $f_x=\\tfrac1y$, $f_y=-\\tfrac x{y^2}$, $f_{xx}=0$, $f_{xy}=-\\tfrac1{y^2}$, $f_{yy}=\\tfrac{2x}{y^3}$. The second-order part $\\tfrac12[2f_{xy}dXdY+f_{yy}(dY)^2]=-\\tfrac{dXdY}{y^2}+\\tfrac{x(dY)^2}{y^3}$.</div></details>", "I3.6": "\n      <h4>I3.6 &nbsp;General 2-Variable Form</h4>\n      <div class=\"eq\">$df(X,Y) = \\partial_x f\\,dX + \\partial_y f\\,dY + \\tfrac12\\big[\\partial_{xx}f\\,(dX)^2 + 2\\partial_{xy}f\\,dX\\,dY + \\partial_{yy}f\\,(dY)^2\\big]$</div>\n      <div class=\"use\"><b>Use when:</b> I3.4 and I3.5 are this with $f = xy$ and $f = x/y$. Derive them rather than memorizing, if you prefer &mdash; it is two derivatives each.</div>\n      <div class=\"ex\"><b>Check:</b> $f = xy$ gives $\\partial_{xy}f = 1$, $\\partial_{xx}f = \\partial_{yy}f = 0$, so the bracket is $2\\,dX\\,dY$, halved to $dX\\,dY$ ✓ exactly I3.4.</div>\n      <details class=\"proof\"><summary>Proof</summary><div>Second-order Taylor in two variables, then drop every term smaller than $dt$ using the table (&sect;I2.2), exactly as in &sect;I3.1.</div></details>", "I4.1": "\n      <h4>I4.1 &nbsp;Powers of $W$</h4>\n      <div class=\"eq\">$d(W_t^{2}) = 2W_t\\,dW_t + dt$</div>\n      <div class=\"eq\">$d(W_t^{3}) = 3W_t^{2}\\,dW_t + 3W_t\\,dt$</div>\n      <div class=\"eq\">$d(W_t^{n}) = nW_t^{n-1}dW_t + \\tfrac12 n(n-1)W_t^{n-2}\\,dt$</div>\n      <div class=\"use\"><b>Use when:</b> $f = x^n$ in I3.2. The $dt$ coefficient is $\\tfrac12 f''$, nothing more.</div>\n      <div class=\"ex\"><b>Bonus &mdash; moment recursion:</b> taking $E$ kills the $dW$ term (I1.3), leaving<br>$E[W_t^n] = \\tfrac12 n(n-1)\\int_0^t E[W_s^{n-2}]\\,ds$.<br>$n{=}2 \\to t$; $n{=}4 \\to 6\\int_0^t s\\,ds = \\mathbf{3t^2}$; $n{=}6 \\to 15\\int_0^t 3s^2ds = \\mathbf{15t^3}$ ✓ (&sect;1.4)</div>\n      <details class=\"proof\"><summary>Proof</summary><div>Form 1 with $f=w^n$: $f'=nw^{n-1}$, $f''=n(n-1)w^{n-2}$, so the drift is $\\tfrac12n(n-1)W^{n-2}$.</div></details>", "I4.2": "\n      <h4>I4.2 &nbsp;Exponential of $W$</h4>\n      <div class=\"eq\">$d(e^{W_t}) = e^{W_t}\\,dW_t + \\tfrac12 e^{W_t}\\,dt$</div>\n      <div class=\"eq\">$d\\big(e^{\\sigma W_t}\\big) = \\sigma e^{\\sigma W_t}\\,dW_t + \\tfrac{\\sigma^2}{2}e^{\\sigma W_t}\\,dt$</div>\n      <div class=\"eq\">$d\\big(e^{\\sigma W_t - \\sigma^2t/2}\\big) = \\sigma\\,e^{\\sigma W_t - \\sigma^2t/2}\\,dW_t$ &nbsp;<b>(zero drift)</b></div>\n      <div class=\"use\"><b>Use when:</b> the third line is the exponential martingale of &sect;7.3 &mdash; the $-\\sigma^2t/2$ exists precisely to cancel the It&ocirc; correction.</div>\n      <div class=\"ex\"><b>Paper:</b> Midsem&rsquo;24 Q4(b) &mdash; $X_t = e^{W_t - t/2}$. $\\partial_t f = -\\tfrac12 f$, $\\partial_x f = \\partial_{xx}f = f$, so<br>$dX = -\\tfrac12X\\,dt + X\\,dW + \\tfrac12X\\,dt = \\mathbf{X_t\\,dW_t}$. Zero drift &rArr; <b>Yes, a martingale</b>.</div>\n      <details class=\"proof\"><summary>Proof</summary><div>$f=e^{\\sigma w}$: $f'=\\sigma f$, $f''=\\sigma^2f$. Form 1: $df=\\sigma f\\,dW+\\tfrac{\\sigma^2}2f\\,dt$.</div></details>", "I4.3": "\n      <h4>I4.3 &nbsp;⭐ Sine and Cosine</h4>\n      <div class=\"eq\">$d\\big(\\sin W_t\\big) = \\cos W_t\\,dW_t - \\tfrac12\\sin W_t\\,dt$</div>\n      <div class=\"eq\">$d\\big(\\cos W_t\\big) = -\\sin W_t\\,dW_t - \\tfrac12\\cos W_t\\,dt$</div>\n      <div class=\"use\"><b>Use when:</b> both have $f'' = -f$, so the drift is always $-\\tfrac12 f$. <b>That single fact answers every trig question on this paper.</b></div>\n      <div class=\"ex\"><b>Paper:</b> CT-2&rsquo;25 Q8 &mdash; &ldquo;compute $d\\sin(W_t)$&rdquo; &rarr; $\\mathbf{\\cos W_t\\,dW_t - \\tfrac12\\sin W_t\\,dt}$ ✓<br>Midsem&rsquo;24 Q1(e) &mdash; &ldquo;compute $d\\cos(W_t)$&rdquo; &rarr; $\\mathbf{-\\sin W_t\\,dW_t - \\tfrac12\\cos W_t\\,dt}$ ✓</div>\n      <details class=\"proof\"><summary>Proof</summary><div>$\\sin$: $f'=\\cos$, $f''=-\\sin$. $\\cos$: $f'=-\\sin$, $f''=-\\cos$. Plug into $df=f'\\,dW+\\tfrac12f''dt$.</div></details>", "I4.4": "\n      <h4>I4.4 &nbsp;⭐ The $e^{\\lambda t}$ Family</h4>\n      <div class=\"eq\">$d\\big(e^{\\lambda t}g(W_t)\\big) = e^{\\lambda t}\\Big(\\lambda g + \\tfrac12 g''\\Big)dt \\;+\\; e^{\\lambda t}g'(W_t)\\,dW_t$</div>\n      <div class=\"eq\">martingale $\\iff \\lambda g + \\tfrac12 g'' = 0$</div>\n      <div class=\"use\"><b>Use when:</b> the exam&rsquo;s favourite shape. For $g = \\sin$ or $\\cos$ ($g'' = -g$) the condition is $\\lambda = \\tfrac12$ &mdash; <b>exponent $\\tfrac12$ &rArr; martingale, anything else &rArr; not</b>.</div>\n      <div class=\"ex\"><b>Paper:</b> CT-1&rsquo;26 Q5 &mdash; $e^{t/2}\\sin W_t$ <b>Yes</b>; $e^{t}\\cos W_t$ <b>No</b>; $e^{t}\\sin W_t$ <b>No</b>; $e^{t/2}\\cos W_t$ <b>Yes</b>.<br>Midsem&rsquo;25 Q4(b)(i) &mdash; $X_t = e^{t/2}\\cos W_t$: $dX_t = \\mathbf{-e^{t/2}\\sin W_t\\,dW_t}$, drift zero.</div>\n      <details class=\"proof\"><summary>Proof</summary><div>Form 2 with $f=e^{\\lambda t}g(w)$: $\\partial_tf=\\lambda e^{\\lambda t}g$, $\\partial_wf=e^{\\lambda t}g'$, $\\partial_{ww}f=e^{\\lambda t}g''$. MG iff the $dt$ coefficient is $0$ (&sect;I5.1).</div></details>", "I4.5": "\n      <h4>I4.5 &nbsp;Deterministic Factors (no correction)</h4>\n      <div class=\"eq\">$d\\big(g(t)X_t\\big) = g'(t)X_t\\,dt + g(t)\\,dX_t$</div>\n      <div class=\"eq\">$d(tW_t) = W_t\\,dt + t\\,dW_t$</div>\n      <div class=\"eq\">$d(e^{t}W_t) = e^{t}W_t\\,dt + e^{t}\\,dW_t$</div>\n      <div class=\"use\"><b>Use when:</b> $\\partial_{xx}f = 0$, so <b>no It&ocirc; correction</b> &mdash; it is the ordinary product rule. This is also the integrating factor of &sect;I7.1.</div>\n      <div class=\"ex\"><b>Paper:</b> Endsem&rsquo;25 Q1(b) &mdash; &ldquo;compute $d(e^{t}W_t)$&rdquo; &rarr; $\\mathbf{e^{t}W_t\\,dt + e^{t}\\,dW_t}$ ✓<br>Endsem&rsquo;25 Q1(c) &mdash; &ldquo;is $Y_t = e^{t}W_t$ a martingale?&rdquo; Drift $= e^tW_t \\ne 0$ &rArr; <b>No</b>.</div>\n      <details class=\"proof\"><summary>Proof</summary><div>Product rule with $g$ deterministic: the correction $dg\\,dX=g'(t)\\,dt\\cdot dX=0$.</div></details>", "I4.6": "\n      <h4>I4.6 &nbsp;Geometric Brownian Motion (both directions)</h4>\n      <div class=\"eq\">$Z_t = e^{\\mu t + \\sigma W_t} \\;\\Rightarrow\\; dZ_t = \\Big(\\mu + \\tfrac{\\sigma^2}{2}\\Big)Z_t\\,dt + \\sigma Z_t\\,dW_t$</div>\n      <div class=\"eq\">$dN_t = rN_t\\,dt + \\alpha N_t\\,dW_t \\;\\Rightarrow\\; N_t = N_0\\exp\\Big\\{\\Big(r - \\tfrac{\\alpha^2}{2}\\Big)t + \\alpha W_t\\Big\\}$</div>\n      <div class=\"use\"><b>Use when:</b> this $\\tfrac{\\sigma^2}{2}$ shuttling back and forth <b>is</b> the two-convention problem of &sect;11.1. Read the direction the question gives you before writing anything.</div>\n      <div class=\"ex\"><b>Derivation of line 2:</b> It&ocirc; with $f = \\log x$: $d(\\log N) = \\tfrac{dN}{N} - \\tfrac{(dN)^2}{2N^2} = \\big(r - \\tfrac{\\alpha^2}{2}\\big)dt + \\alpha\\,dW$, then exponentiate.<br><b>Check:</b> $E[N_t] = N_0e^{(r-\\alpha^2/2)t}\\,e^{\\alpha^2t/2} = \\mathbf{N_0e^{rt}}$ ✓ and median $= N_0e^{(r-\\alpha^2/2)t}$.</div>\n      <details class=\"proof\"><summary>Proof</summary><div>$f=e^{\\mu t+\\sigma w}$: $\\partial_t=\\mu f$, $\\partial_w=\\sigma f$, $\\partial_{ww}=\\sigma^2f$, so $dZ=(\\mu+\\tfrac{\\sigma^2}2)Z\\,dt+\\sigma Z\\,dW$. Reverse: match $\\sigma=\\alpha$ and $\\mu+\\tfrac{\\alpha^2}2=r$, i.e. $\\mu=r-\\tfrac{\\alpha^2}2$; multiply by $N_0$.</div></details>", "I4.7": "\n      <h4>I4.7 &nbsp;Mixed Products</h4>\n      <div class=\"eq\">$d\\big(W_te^{W_t}\\big) = \\big(1+\\tfrac12W_t\\big)e^{W_t}\\,dt + (1+W_t)e^{W_t}\\,dW_t$</div>\n      <div class=\"eq\">$d\\big(e^{-W_t}\\big) = \\tfrac12 e^{-W_t}\\,dt - e^{-W_t}\\,dW_t$</div>\n      <div class=\"use\"><b>Use when:</b> two routes agree &mdash; the product rule I3.4, or I3.2 with $f(x) = xe^x$ ($f' = (1{+}x)e^x$, $f'' = (2{+}x)e^x$). <b>Use one and check with the other.</b></div>\n      <div class=\"ex\"><b>Trick:</b> for $f(x) = xe^x$ the drift is $\\tfrac12 f'' = \\tfrac12(2+W)e^{W} = (1 + \\tfrac12 W)e^{W}$ ✓ Same answer, fewer steps.</div>\n      <details class=\"proof\"><summary>Proof</summary><div>$f=we^w$: $f'=(1+w)e^w$, $f''=(2+w)e^w$, drift $\\tfrac12(2+w)e^w=(1+\\tfrac w2)e^w$. $f=e^{-w}$: $f'=-e^{-w}$, $f''=e^{-w}$.</div></details>", "I5.1": "\n      <h4>I5.1 &nbsp;⭐ Zero Drift = Martingale</h4>\n      <div class=\"eq\">$dX_t = a_t\\,dt + b_t\\,dW_t$ is a martingale $\\iff a_t \\equiv 0$</div>\n      <div class=\"cond\">Strictly, zero drift gives a <em>local</em> martingale; a true martingale also needs integrability, which holds in every exam case.</div>\n      <div class=\"use\"><b>Use when:</b> every &ldquo;Is $Y_t$ a martingale? (Yes/No)&rdquo;. <b>Apply It&ocirc;, read the $dt$ coefficient, done.</b> You never need the definition.</div>\n      <div class=\"ex\"><b>Paper:</b> Endsem&rsquo;25 Q1(c) &mdash; $Y = e^tW_t$, drift $e^tW_t \\ne 0$ &rArr; <b>No</b>.<br>Midsem&rsquo;24 Q4(b) &mdash; $X = e^{W_t-t/2}$, drift $0$ &rArr; <b>Yes</b>.<br>Midsem&rsquo;25 Q1(f) &mdash; $|W_t|^2 - t$ has drift $0$, so &ldquo;is NOT a MG&rdquo; is <b>False</b>.</div>\n      <details class=\"proof\"><summary>Proof</summary><div>Integral form: $X_t=X_0+\\int_0^ta_s\\,ds+\\int_0^tb_s\\,dW_s$.<br>\n(&lArr;) If $a\\equiv0$: $X_t-X_s=\\int_s^tb\\,dW$, and an Itô integral has conditional mean $0$ (&sect;I1.3). So $E(X_t\\mid\\mathcal F_s)=X_s$.<br>\n(&rArr;) If $X$ is a MG then $\\int_0^ta_s\\,ds=X_t-X_0-\\int_0^tb\\,dW$ is a MG with smooth (finite-variation) paths. A continuous MG with such paths is constant, so $\\int_0^ta_s\\,ds=0$ for all $t$, i.e. $a\\equiv0$.</div></details>", "I5.2": "\n      <h4>I5.2 &nbsp;⭐ Mean of a Solution — the drift ODE</h4>\n      <div class=\"eq\">take $E$ of the integral form; the $dW$ term dies (I1.3):</div>\n      <div class=\"eq\">$m(t) := E[X_t] = x_0 + \\displaystyle\\int_0^t E\\big[a(s,X_s)\\big]\\,ds$</div>\n      <div class=\"eq\">$a$ linear in $x$ $\\Rightarrow$ &nbsp; $m'(t) = a\\big(t, m(t)\\big)$, &nbsp; $m(0) = x_0$</div>\n      <div class=\"use\"><b>Use when:</b> &ldquo;find $E(X_t)$&rdquo; for <b>any</b> SDE with linear drift &mdash; <b>you never need the solution</b>. Differentiate the integral form and solve an ordinary ODE.</div>\n      <div class=\"ex\"><b>Q:</b> $a = 1$ &rarr; $m' = 1$, $m = \\mathbf t$. &nbsp; $a = -x$ &rarr; $m' = -m$, $m = \\mathbf{x_0e^{-t}}$. &nbsp; $a = \\kappa(b-x)$ &rarr; $m = \\mathbf{b + e^{-\\kappa t}(x_0-b)}$.<br><b>Trick:</b> the diffusion coefficient $b$ never appears in the mean.</div>\n      <details class=\"proof\"><summary>Proof</summary><div>Integral form $X_t=x_0+\\int_0^ta\\,ds+\\int_0^tb\\,dW$. Take $E$: the Itô integral has mean $0$ (&sect;I1.3); swap $E$ and $\\int ds$. If $a(t,x)=\\alpha(t)+\\beta(t)x$, then $E[a(s,X_s)]=a(s,m(s))$; differentiate in $t$.</div></details>", "I5.3": "\n      <h4>I5.3 &nbsp;⭐ Variance of a Solution — It&ocirc; on $X^2$</h4>\n      <div class=\"eq\">$d(X_t^{2}) = 2X_t\\,dX_t + (dX_t)^{2} = \\big(2X_ta_t + b_t^{2}\\big)dt + 2X_tb_t\\,dW_t$</div>\n      <div class=\"eq\">$\\dfrac{d}{dt}E[X_t^{2}] = E\\big[2X_ta_t + b_t^{2}\\big]$, &nbsp; then $\\operatorname{Var} = E[X_t^2] - m(t)^2$</div>\n      <div class=\"use\"><b>Use when:</b> &ldquo;also find $\\operatorname{Var}(X_t)$&rdquo; &mdash; the part they circle. Same method as I5.2, applied to $X^2$ instead of $X$.</div>\n      <div class=\"ex\"><b>Paper:</b> Midsem&rsquo;25 Q4(a) &mdash; $a{=}1$, $b{=}2\\sqrt X$, $x_0{=}0$.<br>$\\tfrac{d}{dt}E[X^2] = E[2X + 4X] = 6E[X] = 6t$, so $E[X_t^2] = 3t^2$ and $\\operatorname{Var} = 3t^2 - t^2 = \\mathbf{2t^2}$ ✓<br><b>Cross-check:</b> $X_t = W_t^2$, so $\\operatorname{Var} = E[W^4]-t^2 = 3t^2-t^2 = 2t^2$ ✓</div>\n      <details class=\"proof\"><summary>Proof</summary><div>Itô Form 1 on $f(x)=x^2$ ($f'=2x$, $f''=2$): $d(X^2)=2X\\,dX+(dX)^2=(2Xa+b^2)dt+2Xb\\,dW$. Take $E$; the $dW$ part has mean $0$.</div></details>", "I5.4": "\n      <h4>I5.4 &nbsp;⭐ $E[f(W_t)]$ via a Martingale</h4>\n      <div class=\"eq\">find $\\lambda$ with $\\lambda f + \\tfrac12 f'' = 0$; then $e^{\\lambda t}f(W_t)$ is a MG (I4.4)</div>\n      <div class=\"eq\">$\\Rightarrow e^{\\lambda t}E[f(W_t)] = f(0) \\Rightarrow E[f(W_t)] = f(0)\\,e^{-\\lambda t}$</div>\n      <div class=\"use\"><b>Use when:</b> &ldquo;using part (i) or otherwise, evaluate $E[f(W_t)]$&rdquo;. <b>Constant mean of a martingale turns a hard expectation into one division.</b></div>\n      <div class=\"ex\"><b>Paper:</b> Midsem&rsquo;25 Q4(b)(ii) &mdash; $E[\\cos W_t]$. Part (i) gave $e^{t/2}\\cos W_t$ a MG, so<br>$e^{t/2}E[\\cos W_t] = \\cos 0 = 1 \\Rightarrow E[\\cos W_t] = \\mathbf{e^{-t/2}}$ ✓<br><b>Check:</b> $E[e^{iW_t}] = e^{-t/2}$, and taking real parts gives the same ✓ &nbsp; Also $E[\\sin W_t] = 0$ by symmetry.</div>\n      <details class=\"proof\"><summary>Proof</summary><div>$e^{\\lambda t}f(W_t)$ is a MG, so its mean is constant: $E[e^{\\lambda t}f(W_t)]=e^{0}f(W_0)=f(0)$.</div></details>", "I5.5": "\n      <h4>I5.5 &nbsp;Reading the Distribution Off an SDE</h4>\n      <div class=\"eq\">$b$ <b>deterministic</b> (no $X$ in the noise) $\\Rightarrow X_t$ is <b>Gaussian</b></div>\n      <div class=\"eq\">$b$ contains $X$ $\\Rightarrow$ generally <b>not</b> Gaussian</div>\n      <div class=\"use\"><b>Use when:</b> decide this before claiming a distribution. Gaussian: BM with drift, OU, Vasicek. Not Gaussian: GBM (log-normal), CIR, $dX = dt + 2\\sqrt X\\,dW$.</div>\n      <div class=\"ex\"><b>Trick:</b> it is I1.5 restated &mdash; only a deterministic integrand keeps the It&ocirc; integral normal. Look at the $dW$ coefficient and nothing else.</div>\n      <details class=\"proof\"><summary>Proof</summary><div>Linear drift and deterministic $b$: the integrating factor (&sect;I7.1) gives $X_t=$ (deterministic) $+\\int_0^t(\\text{deterministic})\\,dW$, which is normal (&sect;I1.5). With $X$ in the noise this fails: GBM, $dN=rN\\,dt+\\alpha N\\,dW$, is log-normal and always $&gt;0$.</div></details>", "I6.1": "\n      <h4>I6.1 &nbsp;It&ocirc; Process vs SDE</h4>\n      <div class=\"eq\">It&ocirc; process: $dX_t = a_t\\,dt + b_t\\,dW_t$ &mdash; $a_t,b_t$ <b>given</b>, nothing to solve</div>\n      <div class=\"eq\">SDE: $dX_t = a(t,X_t)\\,dt + b(t,X_t)\\,dW_t$, $X_0 = x_0$ &mdash; coefficients built from the <b>unknown</b></div>\n      <div class=\"use\"><b>Use when:</b> tell them apart before starting. $dX = W_t\\,dt + dW_t$ is an It&ocirc; process (just integrate); $dX = X_t^2\\,dt + dW_t$ is a genuine equation.</div>\n      <div class=\"ex\"><b>Trick:</b> most SDEs have <b>no closed form</b>. The exam only asks the handful in &sect;I7 &mdash; or hands you the answer and asks you to verify it (I6.3).</div>", "I6.2": "\n      <h4>I6.2 &nbsp;What &ldquo;Solves&rdquo; Means</h4>\n      <div class=\"eq\">$X_t = x_0 + \\displaystyle\\int_0^t a(s,X_s)\\,ds + \\int_0^t b(s,X_s)\\,dW_s \\quad \\forall t \\in [0,T]$</div>\n      <div class=\"use\"><b>Use when:</b> a solution is an <em>It&ocirc; process satisfying the integral identity</em>. The differential form is shorthand and can never be manipulated as a fraction.</div>\n      <div class=\"ex\"><b>Q:</b> $dX = dt + 2\\,dW$, $x_0 = 3$: RHS $= 3 + t + 2W_t$, so $X_t = 3 + t + 2W_t$ ✓<br><b>Q:</b> $dX = X\\,dt$, $x_0 = 1$: RHS $= 1 + \\int_0^t X_s\\,ds$; candidate $e^t$ gives $1 + (e^t-1) = e^t$ ✓</div>", "I6.3": "\n      <h4>I6.3 &nbsp;⭐ Verification Recipe — &ldquo;show $X_t = \\ldots$ is a solution&rdquo;</h4>\n      <div class=\"eq\">1. apply It&ocirc; to the proposed $X_t$</div>\n      <div class=\"eq\">2. rewrite the result in terms of $X_t$ itself</div>\n      <div class=\"eq\">3. compare with the given SDE, and check $X_0 = x_0$</div>\n      <div class=\"use\"><b>Use when:</b> the exam asks this far more often than it asks you to <em>derive</em> a solution. <b>It is a one-way check &mdash; never try to solve.</b></div>\n      <div class=\"ex\"><b>Paper:</b> Midsem&rsquo;25 Q4(a) &mdash; show $X_t = W_t^2$ solves $dX_t = dt + 2\\sqrt{X_t}\\,dW_t$, $X_0 = 0$.<br>It&ocirc;: $d(W_t^2) = dt + 2W_t\\,dW_t$. Since $\\sqrt{X_t} = |W_t|$, this matches ✓ and $X_0 = W_0^2 = 0$ ✓<br>Then $E[X_t] = E[W_t^2] = \\mathbf t$ and $\\operatorname{Var}(X_t) = 3t^2 - t^2 = \\mathbf{2t^2}$ (I5.3).</div>\n      <details class=\"proof\"><summary>Proof</summary><div>If Itô turns the candidate into $dX_t=a(t,X_t)\\,dt+b(t,X_t)\\,dW_t$ and $X_0=x_0$, integrating gives exactly the integral equation of &sect;I6.2. That is the definition of a solution.</div></details>", "I6.4": "\n      <h4>I6.4 &nbsp;Express the Solution as a Stochastic Integral</h4>\n      <div class=\"eq\">$dY_t = h_t\\,dt + k_t\\,dW_t \\iff Y_t = Y_0 + \\displaystyle\\int_0^t h_s\\,ds + \\int_0^t k_s\\,dW_s$</div>\n      <div class=\"use\"><b>Use when:</b> the question says &ldquo;express $X_t$ as a stochastic integral with respect to $W_t$&rdquo;. Translate differentials into integrals &mdash; that <em>is</em> the answer, no evaluation needed.</div>\n      <div class=\"ex\"><b>Paper:</b> Midsem&rsquo;24 Q4(a) &mdash; $dX_t = -X_t\\,dt + dW_t$, $X_0 = x_0$.<br>Answer: $X_t = \\mathbf{x_0e^{-t} + \\int_0^t e^{-(t-s)}\\,dW_s}$ (derived in I7.3).</div>", "I7.1": "\n      <h4>I7.1 &nbsp;⭐ Integrating Factor (linear, additive noise)</h4>\n      <div class=\"eq\">$dX_t = \\big(\\alpha(t) - rX_t\\big)dt + \\sigma(t)\\,dW_t$</div>\n      <div class=\"eq\">multiply by $e^{rt}$: &nbsp; $d\\big(e^{rt}X_t\\big) = re^{rt}X_t\\,dt + e^{rt}dX_t$ &nbsp;(no correction, I4.5)</div>\n      <div class=\"eq\">$\\Rightarrow d\\big(e^{rt}X_t\\big) = e^{rt}\\alpha(t)\\,dt + e^{rt}\\sigma(t)\\,dW_t$ &nbsp;<b>the $X$ cancels</b></div>\n      <div class=\"use\"><b>Use when:</b> the <b>only</b> general solving technique on this syllabus. Integrate, then divide by $e^{rt}$. Works exactly when the noise does <em>not</em> contain $X$.</div>\n      <div class=\"ex\"><b>Trick:</b> the whole method is &ldquo;choose $f$ so that It&ocirc;&rsquo;s formula deletes the unknown&rdquo;. If the noise contains $X$ (as in CIR) nothing cancels &mdash; see I7.5.</div>", "I7.2": "\n      <h4>I7.2 &nbsp;Brownian Motion with Drift</h4>\n      <div class=\"eq\">$dX_t = \\mu\\,dt + \\sigma\\,dW_t$, &nbsp; $X_0 = x_0$</div>\n      <div class=\"eq\">$X_t = x_0 + \\mu t + \\sigma W_t \\sim N\\big(x_0 + \\mu t,\\ \\sigma^2 t\\big)$</div>\n      <div class=\"use\"><b>Use when:</b> the trivial case &mdash; both coefficients constant, so you just integrate. All of &sect;10 is built on it.</div>\n      <div class=\"ex\"><b>Trick:</b> constant coefficients $\\Rightarrow$ no It&ocirc; correction anywhere, because there is no $f$ to differentiate twice.</div>\n      <details class=\"proof\"><summary>Proof</summary><div>Integrate both sides: $X_t=x_0+\\mu t+\\sigma W_t$. $\\sigma W_t\\sim N(0,\\sigma^2t)$.</div></details>", "I7.3": "\n      <h4>I7.3 &nbsp;⭐ Ornstein&ndash;Uhlenbeck</h4>\n      <div class=\"eq\">$dX_t = -rX_t\\,dt + \\sigma\\,dW_t$, &nbsp; $X_0 = x_0$, &nbsp; $r,\\sigma&gt;0$</div>\n      <div class=\"eq\">$X_t = x_0e^{-rt} + \\sigma\\displaystyle\\int_0^t e^{-r(t-s)}\\,dW_s$</div>\n      <div class=\"eq\">$X_t \\sim N\\Big(x_0e^{-rt},\\ \\dfrac{\\sigma^2}{2r}\\big(1-e^{-2rt}\\big)\\Big)$</div>\n      <div class=\"use\"><b>Use when:</b> drift proportional to $-X$ = <b>mean reversion</b>: the pull toward $0$ strengthens with distance. Gaussian, because the noise is deterministic (I5.5).</div>\n      <div class=\"ex\"><b>Paper:</b> Midsem&rsquo;24 Q4(a) &mdash; $dX_t = -X_t\\,dt + dW_t$ ($r{=}\\sigma{=}1$). &ldquo;Whether it is an OU process? Explain.&rdquo; <b>Yes.</b><br>$E[X_t] = \\mathbf{x_0e^{-t}}$, &nbsp; $\\operatorname{Var}(X_t) = \\mathbf{\\tfrac12(1-e^{-2t})}$.<br><b>Variance derivation:</b> isometry gives $\\int_0^t e^{-2r(t-s)}ds = \\tfrac{1-e^{-2rt}}{2r}$ ✓</div>\n      <details class=\"proof\"><summary>Proof</summary><div>&sect;I7.1 with $\\alpha=0$: $d(e^{rt}X_t)=\\sigma e^{rt}dW_t$, so $e^{rt}X_t=x_0+\\sigma\\int_0^te^{rs}dW_s$; divide by $e^{rt}$.<br>Normal: deterministic integrand (&sect;I1.5). Mean $x_0e^{-rt}$ (&sect;I1.3). Variance by isometry: $\\sigma^2\\int_0^te^{-2r(t-s)}ds=\\frac{\\sigma^2}{2r}(1-e^{-2rt})$.</div></details>", "I7.4": "\n      <h4>I7.4 &nbsp;OU: Stationary Limit</h4>\n      <div class=\"eq\">$X_t \\;\\xrightarrow{d}\\; N\\Big(0,\\ \\dfrac{\\sigma^2}{2r}\\Big)$ as $t\\to\\infty$</div>\n      <div class=\"use\"><b>Use when:</b> the contrast with BM is the marked point &mdash; <b>OU variance stays bounded; BM variance grows like $t$.</b> The starting value is forgotten exponentially fast.</div>\n      <div class=\"ex\"><b>Cross-ref:</b> &sect;5.5 &mdash; $e^{-\\lambda t}W(e^{2\\lambda t})$ is the <em>same</em> process, built by time-changing BM. Its covariance $e^{-\\lambda|t-s|}$ depends only on $|t-s|$: stationary, and therefore <b>not</b> a Wiener process.</div>\n      <details class=\"proof\"><summary>Proof</summary><div>Let $t\\to\\infty$ in &sect;I7.3: mean $x_0e^{-rt}\\to0$, variance $\\to\\frac{\\sigma^2}{2r}$. Normal laws with converging mean and variance converge to the limiting normal.</div></details>", "I7.5": "\n      <h4>I7.5 &nbsp;Vasicek &amp; CIR (interest rates)</h4>\n      <div class=\"eq\"><b>Vasicek:</b> $dr_t = \\kappa(b-r_t)dt + \\sigma\\,dW_t$</div>\n      <div class=\"eq\">$r_t \\sim N\\Big(b + e^{-\\kappa t}(r_0-b),\\ \\dfrac{\\sigma^2}{2\\kappa}\\big(1-e^{-2\\kappa t}\\big)\\Big)$</div>\n      <div class=\"eq\"><b>CIR:</b> $dr_t = \\kappa(b-r_t)dt + \\sigma\\sqrt{r_t}\\,dW_t$ &mdash; no closed form</div>\n      <div class=\"eq\">same mean $E[r_t] = b + e^{-\\kappa t}(r_0-b)$, but <b>not</b> normal</div>\n      <div class=\"use\"><b>Use when:</b> Vasicek is OU shifted &mdash; substitute $X_t = r_t - b$ and it becomes I7.3 exactly. CIR keeps $r_t \\ge 0$ (noise vanishes at $0$), strictly positive if $2\\kappa b \\ge \\sigma^2$ (Feller).</div>\n      <div class=\"ex\"><b>Trick:</b> Vasicek&rsquo;s flaw is that Gaussian rates can go negative, $P(r_t &lt; 0) &gt; 0$. That is precisely the motivation for CIR &mdash; a standard short-note answer.<br><b>Note:</b> the two share a mean because they share a drift (I5.2: the diffusion never enters the mean).</div>\n      <details class=\"proof\"><summary>Proof</summary><div>Vasicek: $X=r-b$ satisfies $dX=-\\kappa X\\,dt+\\sigma\\,dW$, an OU process (&sect;I7.3); add $b$ back.<br>CIR mean: the drift is linear, so $m'=\\kappa(b-m)$ (&sect;I5.2), giving $m(t)=b+e^{-\\kappa t}(r_0-b)$. Not normal: the noise $\\sigma\\sqrt{r_t}$ depends on $r_t$ (&sect;I5.5), and $r_t\\ge0$, which a normal variable cannot be.</div></details>", "I7.6": "\n      <h4>I7.6 &nbsp;Geometric Brownian Motion</h4>\n      <div class=\"eq\">$dN_t = rN_t\\,dt + \\alpha N_t\\,dW_t$, &nbsp; $N_0 &gt; 0$</div>\n      <div class=\"eq\">$N_t = N_0\\exp\\Big\\{\\Big(r-\\tfrac{\\alpha^2}{2}\\Big)t + \\alpha W_t\\Big\\}$ &nbsp;&mdash;&nbsp; <b>log-normal</b></div>\n      <div class=\"eq\">$E[N_t] = N_0e^{rt}$, &nbsp; median $= N_0e^{(r-\\alpha^2/2)t}$</div>\n      <div class=\"use\"><b>Use when:</b> multiplicative noise. Solve by It&ocirc; on $\\log N$ &mdash; the <b>only</b> place taking a logarithm is the right move, and it works because $N_t &gt; 0$.</div>\n      <div class=\"ex\"><b>Cross-ref:</b> all numerical GBM questions are &sect;11.2, including the two-convention trap of &sect;11.1. This card is where that $\\tfrac{\\alpha^2}{2}$ comes from.<br><b>Rigour note:</b> $\\log N_t$ presumes $N_t &gt; 0$; close the gap by verifying the candidate with I6.3 instead.</div>\n      <details class=\"proof\"><summary>Proof</summary><div>Solution: &sect;I4.6 reversed (or Itô on $\\ln N$, &sect;D5.3). Mean: $E[e^{\\alpha W_t}]=e^{\\alpha^2t/2}$ cancels the $-\\alpha^2/2$, giving $N_0e^{rt}$. Median: the exponent is normal with median $(r-\\tfrac{\\alpha^2}2)t$, and $e^x$ is increasing.</div></details>", "I7.7": "\n      <h4>I7.7 &nbsp;Summary Table</h4>\n      <div class=\"eq\">$\\mu\\,dt + \\sigma\\,dW$ &nbsp;&rarr;&nbsp; Normal, variance $\\sigma^2t$</div>\n      <div class=\"eq\">$-rX\\,dt + \\sigma\\,dW$ &nbsp;&rarr;&nbsp; Normal, variance bounded (OU)</div>\n      <div class=\"eq\">$rN\\,dt + \\alpha N\\,dW$ &nbsp;&rarr;&nbsp; log-Normal (GBM)</div>\n      <div class=\"eq\">$\\kappa(b-r)dt + \\sigma\\sqrt r\\,dW$ &nbsp;&rarr;&nbsp; no closed form (CIR)</div>\n      <div class=\"use\"><b>Use when:</b> identify which row you are in before doing anything. <b>Noise depends on $X$?</b> If no &rarr; Gaussian, integrating factor works. If yes &rarr; either GBM (log) or nothing.</div>\n      <div class=\"ex\"><b>Trick:</b> the mean always comes from the drift alone (I5.2), so you can answer &ldquo;find $E[X_t]$&rdquo; for <em>every</em> row &mdash; even CIR, where no solution exists.</div>", "I8.1": "\n      <h4>I8.1 &nbsp;⭐ Midsem&rsquo;25 Q4(a)</h4>\n      <div class=\"eq\">$dX_t = dt + 2\\sqrt{X_t}\\,dW_t$, &nbsp; $X_0 = 0$. Show $X_t = W_t^2$; find $E(X_t)$, $\\operatorname{Var}(X_t)$.</div>\n      <div class=\"ex\">\n      <b>Show:</b> It&ocirc; with $f(x) = x^2$ on $X{=}W$ gives $d(W_t^2) = 2W_t\\,dW_t + dt$.<br>\n      With $X_t = W_t^2$ we have $\\sqrt{X_t} = |W_t|$, so $2\\sqrt{X_t}\\,dW_t = 2W_t\\,dW_t$ ✓, and $X_0 = 0$ ✓<br><br>\n      <b>Mean (I5.2):</b> $a \\equiv 1$, so $m' = 1$, $m(0)=0$ &rArr; $E[X_t] = \\mathbf t$. &nbsp;(Check: $E[W_t^2] = t$ ✓)<br><br>\n      <b>Variance (I5.3):</b> $\\tfrac{d}{dt}E[X^2] = E[2X_ta_t + b_t^2] = E[2X_t + 4X_t] = 6t$<br>\n      $\\Rightarrow E[X_t^2] = 3t^2 \\Rightarrow \\operatorname{Var}(X_t) = 3t^2 - t^2 = \\mathbf{2t^2}$<br>\n      <b>Check:</b> $\\operatorname{Var}(W_t^2) = E[W^4] - t^2 = 3t^2 - t^2 = 2t^2$ ✓\n      </div>\n      <div class=\"use\"><b>Note:</b> $\\operatorname{Var}$ is the part they circled on the paper. Do it both ways &mdash; the cross-check costs one line.</div>", "I8.2": "\n      <h4>I8.2 &nbsp;⭐ Midsem&rsquo;25 Q4(b)</h4>\n      <div class=\"eq\">$X_t := e^{t/2}\\cos(W_t)$. (i) find $dX_t$. (ii) evaluate $E[\\cos W_t]$.</div>\n      <div class=\"ex\">\n      <b>(i)</b> $f(t,x) = e^{t/2}\\cos x$: &nbsp; $\\partial_t f = \\tfrac12 f$, &nbsp; $\\partial_x f = -e^{t/2}\\sin x$, &nbsp; $\\partial_{xx}f = -f$.<br>\n      $dX_t = \\tfrac12 X_t\\,dt - e^{t/2}\\sin W_t\\,dW_t - \\tfrac12 X_t\\,dt = \\mathbf{-e^{t/2}\\sin(W_t)\\,dW_t}$<br><br>\n      <b>(ii)</b> Drift is zero &rArr; $X_t$ is a martingale &rArr; $E[X_t] = X_0 = e^0\\cos 0 = 1$.<br>\n      $e^{t/2}E[\\cos W_t] = 1 \\;\\Rightarrow\\; E[\\cos W_t] = \\mathbf{e^{-t/2}}$<br>\n      <b>Check:</b> $E[e^{iW_t}] = e^{-t/2}$; real part gives the same ✓ &nbsp;($E[\\sin W_t] = 0$ by symmetry.)\n      </div>\n      <div class=\"use\"><b>Trick:</b> &ldquo;using part (i) or otherwise&rdquo; is the examiner telling you the martingale <em>is</em> the method. This is I5.4.</div>", "I8.3": "\n      <h4>I8.3 &nbsp;⭐ Midsem&rsquo;24 Q4(a)</h4>\n      <div class=\"eq\">$dX_t = -X_t\\,dt + dW_t$, $X_0 = x_0$. Express as a stochastic integral. OU? Find $E(X_t)$, $\\operatorname{Var}(X_t)$.</div>\n      <div class=\"ex\">\n      <b>Solve (I7.1):</b> $d(e^{t}X_t) = e^{t}X_t\\,dt + e^{t}dX_t = e^{t}dW_t$ &mdash; the $X$ cancels.<br>\n      Integrate: $e^{t}X_t = x_0 + \\int_0^t e^{s}dW_s$, so<br>\n      $X_t = \\mathbf{x_0e^{-t} + \\displaystyle\\int_0^t e^{-(t-s)}\\,dW_s}$<br><br>\n      <b>OU?</b> <b>Yes</b> &mdash; drift $-rX$ with $r=1$, constant noise $\\sigma=1$: mean-reverting to $0$.<br><br>\n      <b>Mean:</b> the It&ocirc; integral has mean $0$, so $E[X_t] = \\mathbf{x_0e^{-t}}$.<br>\n      <b>Variance (isometry):</b> $\\displaystyle\\int_0^t e^{-2(t-s)}ds = \\mathbf{\\tfrac12\\big(1-e^{-2t}\\big)}$<br>\n      <b>Distribution:</b> noise is deterministic &rArr; $X_t \\sim N\\big(x_0e^{-t},\\ \\tfrac12(1-e^{-2t})\\big)$.\n      </div>\n      <div class=\"use\"><b>Trick:</b> as $t\\to\\infty$, $X_t \\Rightarrow N(0,\\tfrac12)$ &mdash; add that line, it is the point of OU.</div>", "I8.4": "\n      <h4>I8.4 &nbsp;Midsem&rsquo;24 Q4(b) &amp; Endsem&rsquo;25 Q1(b,c)</h4>\n      <div class=\"eq\">$X_t = e^{W_t - t/2}$: MG? &nbsp;|&nbsp; $d(e^{t}W_t) = ?$ &nbsp;|&nbsp; is $e^{t}W_t$ a MG?</div>\n      <div class=\"ex\">\n      <b>$X_t = e^{W_t-t/2}$:</b> $f = e^{x-t/2}$, so $\\partial_t f = -\\tfrac12 f$, $\\partial_x f = \\partial_{xx}f = f$.<br>\n      $dX = -\\tfrac12X\\,dt + X\\,dW + \\tfrac12X\\,dt = \\mathbf{X_t\\,dW_t}$ &rArr; zero drift &rArr; <b>Yes, a martingale</b>.<br><br>\n      <b>$d(e^{t}W_t)$:</b> $\\partial_{xx}f = 0$, so no correction (I4.5):<br>\n      $d(e^{t}W_t) = \\mathbf{e^{t}W_t\\,dt + e^{t}\\,dW_t}$<br><br>\n      <b>Is $e^{t}W_t$ a MG?</b> Drift $= e^{t}W_t \\ne 0$ &rArr; <b>No</b>.\n      </div>\n      <div class=\"use\"><b>Trick:</b> both questions are one It&ocirc; application each. Compute the differential, then read the $dt$ coefficient &mdash; that single number answers the Yes/No.</div>", "I8.5": "\n      <h4>I8.5 &nbsp;The Short Differentials (1-mark answers)</h4>\n      <div class=\"eq\">$d\\sin W_t = \\cos W_t\\,dW_t - \\tfrac12\\sin W_t\\,dt$ &nbsp;<span style=\"font-size:.8em\">[CT-2&rsquo;25 Q8]</span></div>\n      <div class=\"eq\">$d\\cos W_t = -\\sin W_t\\,dW_t - \\tfrac12\\cos W_t\\,dt$ &nbsp;<span style=\"font-size:.8em\">[Midsem&rsquo;24 Q1(e)]</span></div>\n      <div class=\"eq\">$\\operatorname{Var}\\big(\\int_0^1 t^4dW_t\\big) = \\tfrac19$ &nbsp;<span style=\"font-size:.8em\">[Endsem&rsquo;25 Q1(a)]</span></div>\n      <div class=\"eq\">$\\int_0^1 t^2dW_t \\sim N(0,\\tfrac15)$ &nbsp;<span style=\"font-size:.8em\">[CT-2&rsquo;25 Q7]</span></div>\n      <div class=\"use\"><b>Use when:</b> these appear as single-mark table entries. Write the answer, show nothing. Each takes ten seconds with I4.3 and I1.5.</div>\n      <div class=\"ex\"><b>Trick:</b> for any $f(W_t)$ the drift is always $\\tfrac12 f''(W_t)$ and the diffusion always $f'(W_t)$. <b>Two derivatives and you are done</b> &mdash; that covers every entry above.</div>", "A1.1": "\n      <h4>A1.1 &nbsp;Arbitrage &mdash; definition</h4>\n      <div class=\"eq\">A scheme with cost $\\le 0$ at $t=0$ and payoff $\\ge 0$ in <em>every</em> outcome, $&gt;0$ in some.</div>\n      <div class=\"note\">Risk-free profit. &ldquo;Every outcome&rdquo; means with probability 1 &mdash; not on average. No probability appears in the definition.</div>\n      <div class=\"use\"><b>Use when:</b> any question saying &ldquo;sure win&rdquo;, &ldquo;risk-free profit&rdquo;, &ldquo;for which price is there no arbitrage&rdquo;. The answer is always: build a portfolio whose time-1 value is the <em>same number</em> in all states, then compare with its cost.</div>\n      <div class=\"ex\"><b>Trick:</b> if a certain gain is positive for one scale $x$, it is unbounded &mdash; scale $x$ up. That is why an arbitrage price cannot survive in a market.</div>", "A1.2": "\n      <h4>A1.2 &nbsp;⭐ No-arbitrage price by hedging (one period, two states)</h4>\n      <div class=\"eq\">Buy $x$ stock, $y$ options. Force $V_1$ equal in both states $\\Rightarrow$ $y$ in terms of $x$, then</div>\n      <div class=\"eq\">$\\text{gain} = V_1 - (1+r)\\,\\text{cost} = 0 \\;\\Longrightarrow\\; c^{*}$</div>\n      <div class=\"use\"><b>Use when:</b> two possible time-1 prices and an option quoted at $c$. Works with <em>no probabilities at all</em> &mdash; only the two possible values matter.</div>\n      <div class=\"ex\"><b>Paper:</b> $S_0=100 \\to \\{200,50\\}$, $K=150$, $r=0$. $200x+50y = 50x \\Rightarrow y=-3x$; $V_1 = 50x$, cost $=100x-3xc$, gain $= x(3c-50)$. So $c^{*} = \\mathbf{50/3}$; $c=20$ (buy stock, write 3 calls) or $c=15$ (short stock, buy 3 calls) both print money.</div>\n      <details class=\"proof\"><summary>Proof</summary><div>The equalised portfolio pays the same amount in both states, so it is riskless. A riskless position with a non-zero net gain can be scaled up without limit: an arbitrage. So the only price without arbitrage is the one making that gain $0$.</div></details>", "A1.3": "\n      <h4>A1.3 &nbsp;⭐ Risk-neutral measure</h4>\n      <div class=\"eq\">$S_0 = \\dfrac{E^{*}[S_1]}{1+r}, \\qquad p^{*} = \\dfrac{(1+r)S_0 - S_d}{S_u - S_d} = \\dfrac{1+r-d}{u-d}$</div>\n      <div class=\"note\">Equivalently: every wager has zero expected return under $p^{*}$; the discounted price is a martingale (&sect;M3.4).</div>\n      <div class=\"use\"><b>Use when:</b> any binomial pricing question. Need $d &lt; 1+r &lt; u$, else $p^{*}\\notin(0,1)$ and the market itself is an arbitrage.</div>\n      <div class=\"ex\"><b>Paper:</b> $100\\to\\{130,80\\}$, $r=0.05$: $105 = 80 + 50p^{*} \\Rightarrow p^{*} = \\mathbf{0.5}$ &mdash; unrelated to the real $p=0.4$.</div>\n      <details class=\"proof\"><summary>Proof</summary><div>Solve $S_0(1+r)=p^{*}S_u+(1-p^{*})S_d$ for $p^{*}$. With $S_u=uS_0$, $S_d=dS_0$, divide by $S_0$: $1+r=p^{*}u+(1-p^{*})d$. It is in $(0,1)$ iff $d&lt;1+r&lt;u$ (&sect;M3.4).</div></details>", "A1.4": "\n      <h4>A1.4 &nbsp;⭐ Pricing formula for any claim</h4>\n      <div class=\"eq\">$C_0 = \\dfrac{E^{*}\\big[g(S_1)\\big]}{1+r}, \\qquad \\text{call: } g(s) = (s-K)^+$</div>\n      <div class=\"use\"><b>Use when:</b> &ldquo;price the call / put / claim&rdquo;. Two steps, never more: (1) $p^{*}$ from the <em>stock</em>, (2) expectation of the <em>payoff</em>. Never use the real-world $p$.</div>\n      <div class=\"ex\"><b>Paper:</b> $100\\to\\{130,80\\}$, $K=110$, $r=0.05$, $p^{*}=0.5$: $C_0 = \\frac{20(0.5)+0(0.5)}{1.05} = \\mathbf{9.52}$.</div>\n      <details class=\"proof\"><summary>Proof</summary><div>The replicating portfolio (&sect;A1.5) costs $\\frac{p^{*}C_u+(1-p^{*})C_d}{1+r}=\\frac{E^{*}[g(S_1)]}{1+r}$ (&sect;M4.4). Any other option price: buy the cheaper of {option, portfolio}, sell the dearer; the payoffs cancel and you keep the gap.</div></details>", "A1.5": "\n      <h4>A1.5 &nbsp;⭐ Replicating portfolio $(\\Delta, B)$</h4>\n      <div class=\"eq\">$\\Delta = \\dfrac{C_u - C_d}{S_u - S_d}, \\qquad B = \\dfrac{C_u - \\Delta S_u}{1+r}, \\qquad C_0 = \\Delta S_0 + B$</div>\n      <div class=\"note\">$\\Delta$ = shares held, $B$ = cash lent ($B&lt;0$ means borrowing). $C_0$ from replication always equals $C_0$ from &sect;A1.4 &mdash; two routes, one price.</div>\n      <div class=\"use\"><b>Use when:</b> &ldquo;construct the hedge&rdquo;, &ldquo;show the claim is attainable&rdquo;, or when you must <em>exhibit</em> the arbitrage rather than merely assert it.</div>\n      <div class=\"ex\"><b>Paper:</b> $\\Delta = \\frac{20-0}{130-80} = 0.4$, $B = \\frac{20-52}{1.05} = -30.48$; $C_0 = 40-30.48 = \\mathbf{9.52}$. Down state check: $0.4(80)-32 = 0$ ✓.</div>\n      <details class=\"proof\"><summary>Proof</summary><div>Solve two equations (up and down state) in two unknowns:<br>\n$\\Delta S_u+B(1+r)=C_u,\\quad \\Delta S_d+B(1+r)=C_d$.<br>\nSubtract: $\\Delta(S_u-S_d)=C_u-C_d$. Back-substitute for $B$. Its cost today is $\\Delta S_0+B$; any other option price lets you buy the cheaper of {option, portfolio} and sell the other: arbitrage.</div></details>", "A1.6": "\n      <h4>A1.6 &nbsp;⚠ The actuarial trap</h4>\n      <div class=\"eq\">$\\dfrac{E_p[\\text{payoff}]}{1+r} \\ne C_0$ &nbsp; when $p$ is the <em>real-world</em> probability.</div>\n      <div class=\"bad\"><b>Why it fails:</b> the payoff is <em>replicable</em>. Two portfolios with identical cash flows in every state must have identical prices, whatever anyone believes about likelihoods. Insurance risk is diversifiable but not hedgeable, which is why the actuarial method is right there and wrong here.</div>\n      <div class=\"ex\"><b>Paper:</b> $p=0.4$ gives $\\frac{20(0.4)}{1.05} = 7.62$, but $C_0 = 9.52$. At $7.62$: buy the call, short $0.4$ shares $(+40)$, lend $30.48$ &rArr; net $+1.90$ today, payoffs cancel at $T$. <b>Certain $1.90$ per contract.</b></div>\n      <details class=\"proof\"><summary>Proof</summary><div>The price must equal the replication cost (&sect;A1.5), which uses $p^{*}$ only. $E_p[\\text{payoff}]/(1+r)$ equals it only when $p=p^{*}$; otherwise the gap is an arbitrage, as in the example.</div></details>", "A2.1": "\n      <h4>A2.1 &nbsp;Payoff dictionary</h4>\n      <div class=\"eq\">Forward (long): $S_T - K$ &nbsp;&mdash;&nbsp; an <b>obligation</b>, can be negative</div>\n      <div class=\"eq\">European call: $(S_T-K)^+$ &nbsp;|&nbsp; European put: $(K-S_T)^+$</div>\n      <div class=\"note\">A <em>derivative security</em> / <em>contingent claim</em> is any $X = g(S_T)$ paid at $T$. $x^+ := \\max(x,0)$.</div>\n      <div class=\"use\"><b>Use when:</b> writing down the payoff before anything else. Getting $(\\cdot)^+$ vs no $(\\cdot)^+$ right is the whole difference between an option and a forward.</div>\n      <div class=\"ex\"><b>Trick:</b> $K=150$, $S_T\\in\\{200,50\\}$ &rarr; forward $+50/-100$, call $50/0$, put $0/100$. The call is the forward with the loss leg deleted &mdash; which is exactly what the premium buys. Futures = forwards, but exchange-traded and marked to market daily.</div>", "A2.2": "\n      <h4>A2.2 &nbsp;American vs European</h4>\n      <div class=\"eq\">$\\text{price} = \\sup_{\\tau \\le T} E^{*}\\big[(1+r)^{-\\tau}(S_\\tau - K)^+\\big]$, &nbsp; $\\tau$ a <b>stopping time</b></div>\n      <div class=\"note\">$(S_\\tau-K)^+$ is the <em>payoff</em>, not the price. $\\{\\tau \\le t\\}\\in\\mathcal F_t$ is compulsory: &ldquo;the time $S$ peaks&rdquo; is not admissible, since at $t$ you cannot know the future (&sect;M5).</div>\n      <div class=\"use\"><b>Use when:</b> &ldquo;short note: American vs European&rdquo;. No dividends and $r\\ge0$ &rArr; early exercise of a <b>call</b> is never optimal, so the two prices are <b>equal</b>; for a <b>put</b> early exercise can be optimal and the American is strictly dearer.</div>\n      <div class=\"ex\"><b>Trick:</b> one-line reason for the call: exercising early throws away the interest on $K$ and the remaining insurance value. See &sect;M7.6.</div>\n      <details class=\"proof\"><summary>Proof</summary><div>The price formula: the holder may pick any stopping time, so the fair price is the best discounted risk-neutral value over them. Call = European: proof in &sect;M7.6.</div></details>", "A2.3": "\n      <h4>A2.3 &nbsp;⭐ Arbitrage theorem (dichotomy)</h4>\n      <div class=\"eq\">Wagers $i=1..n$, outcomes $j=1..m$, return $r_i(j)$ per unit staked. <b>Exactly one</b> holds:</div>\n      <div class=\"eq\">(i) $\\exists\\,\\underline p$ probability vector with $\\displaystyle\\sum_{j} p_j r_i(j) = 0 \\;\\forall i$</div>\n      <div class=\"eq\">(ii) $\\exists\\,\\underline x$ with $\\displaystyle\\sum_{i} x_i r_i(j) &gt; 0 \\;\\forall j$</div>\n      <div class=\"note\"><b>No arbitrage $\\iff$ a risk-neutral measure exists.</b> Return $=$ payoff $-$ cost; $x_i&lt;0$ allowed (short).</div>\n      <div class=\"use\"><b>Use when:</b> asked to state or prove it. The &ldquo;not both&rdquo; half is three lines: $\\sum_j p_j R(j) = \\sum_i x_i\\sum_j p_j r_i(j) = 0$, yet $R(j)&gt;0 \\,\\forall j$ and $\\sum_j p_j = 1$ force it $&gt;0$. Contradiction.</div>\n      <div class=\"ex\"><b>Trick:</b> the &ldquo;at least one&rdquo; half is a separating-hyperplane (Farkas) argument &mdash; say so and move on; it is never asked in full.</div>\n      <details class=\"proof\"><summary>Proof</summary><div><b>Not both.</b> Suppose $p$ and $x$ both exist. Total return $R(j)=\\sum_ix_ir_i(j)&gt;0$ for every $j$. Then $\\sum_jp_jR(j)=\\sum_ix_i\\sum_jp_jr_i(j)=0$; but a $p$-average of positive numbers is positive. Contradiction.<br>\n<b>At least one.</b> If no $p$ makes all expected returns $0$, the point $0$ lies outside the convex hull of the return vectors; a separating hyperplane gives the stakes $x$ with $\\sum_ix_ir_i(j)&gt;0$ for all $j$ (same geometry as &sect;D3.3, Step 3).</div></details>", "A2.4": "\n      <h4>A2.4 &nbsp;Three states &rArr; incomplete market</h4>\n      <div class=\"eq\">$m$ states, $n$ assets with $n &lt; m$: $\\;p^{*}$ is not unique $\\Rightarrow$ an <b>interval</b> of arbitrage-free prices.</div>\n      <div class=\"use\"><b>Use when:</b> the tree has three or more branches from one node. Do not hunt for a single price &mdash; there is none, and the claim cannot be replicated.</div>\n      <div class=\"ex\"><b>Paper:</b> $100 \\to \\{300,200,50\\}$, $K=150$, $r=0$. Stock: $250p_1 + 150p_2 = 50 \\Rightarrow p_2 = \\frac{1-5p_1}{3}$; option: $c = 150p_1 + 50p_2 = \\frac{200p_1+50}{3}$. Positivity gives $p_1\\in(0,\\tfrac15)$, hence $c \\in \\big(\\mathbf{\\tfrac{50}{3}},\\,\\mathbf{30}\\big)$.</div>\n      <details class=\"proof\"><summary>Proof</summary><div>EMM: the stock equation plus $\\sum p=1$ is 2 equations in 3 unknowns, a line of solutions. Replication: 3 states give 3 equations in 2 unknowns $(\\Delta,b)$, generally no solution, so the claim is not attainable. $E^{*}[X]$ then moves along the line, and the open range allowed by all $p_k&gt;0$ is the interval of prices (&sect;D3.4).</div></details>", "D1.1": "\n      <h4>D1.1 &nbsp;Present value</h4>\n      <div class=\"eq\">$v$ paid at time $t$ is worth $\\;v(1+r)^{-t}$ (discrete) $\\;=\\;ve^{-rt}$ (continuous) today</div>\n      <div class=\"use\"><b>Use when:</b> every price on this sheet is a <em>discounted</em> expected payoff. Binomial models use $(1+r)^{-T}$; Black&ndash;Scholes uses $e^{-rT}$. Never mix them in one answer.</div>\n      <div class=\"ex\"><b>Trick:</b> the two agree when $1+r_n=e^{r\\delta_n}$ per step, which is exactly the choice in &sect;D5.1.</div>\n      <details class=\"proof\"><summary>Proof</summary><div>One rupee in the bank grows to $(1+r)^t$ (or $e^{rt}$). So to have $v$ at time $t$ you need $v(1+r)^{-t}$ now. Any other price for a sure $v$ at $t$: borrow or lend the difference: arbitrage.</div></details>", "D1.2": "\n      <h4>D1.2 &nbsp;⭐ Forward price</h4>\n      <div class=\"eq\">long forward pays $S_T-K$ (an obligation, &sect;A2.1)</div>\n      <div class=\"eq\">fair delivery price: $\\;K=S_0(1+r)^T\\;$ (discrete) $\\;=\\;S_0e^{rT}\\;$ (continuous)</div>\n      <div class=\"eq\">value today of a forward with price $K$: $\\;S_0-K(1+r)^{-T}$</div>\n      <div class=\"use\"><b>Use when:</b> &ldquo;find $K$ so the contract costs nothing&rdquo;. No probabilities and no $p^{*}$ needed: only the stock and the bond.</div>\n      <div class=\"ex\"><b>Paper-style:</b> $S_0=100$, $r=5\\%$, $T=1$: $K=\\mathbf{105}$. If someone quotes $K=110$: borrow $100$, buy the stock, sell forward at $110$; at $T$ deliver, repay $105$, keep $\\mathbf 5$ for sure.</div>\n      <details class=\"proof\"><summary>Proof</summary><div>Buy 1 share with borrowed money ($S_0$) and sell a forward at $K$. At $T$: deliver the share for $K$ and repay $S_0(1+r)^T$. Profit $K-S_0(1+r)^T$ is certain, so it must be $0$ (if negative, run the trade backwards: short the share, lend, buy forward).<br>Same via $E^{*}$: the contract costs $0$, so $E^{*}[S_T-K]=0$, i.e. $K=E^{*}[S_T]=S_0(1+r)^T$.</div></details>", "D2.1": "\n      <h4>D2.1 &nbsp;Market &amp; trading strategy</h4>\n      <div class=\"eq\">$S_n=(S_n^0,S_n^1,\\dots,S_n^d)$, &nbsp; bond $S_n^0=(1+r)^n$</div>\n      <div class=\"eq\">strategy $\\Phi_n=(\\Phi_n^0,\\dots,\\Phi_n^d)$ = number of units of each asset held</div>\n      <div class=\"eq\">$V_n(\\Phi)=\\Phi_n\\cdot S_n=\\displaystyle\\sum_{i=0}^d\\Phi_n^iS_n^i$</div>\n      <div class=\"use\"><b>Use when:</b> setting up any multi-asset question. Write the value as a dot product first; everything else (self-financing, arbitrage) is a statement about $V_n$.</div>\n      <div class=\"ex\"><b>Trick:</b> $\\Phi_n$ may only use information up to time $n-1$ (you choose the portfolio <em>before</em> the price moves). That is what &ldquo;predictable&rdquo; means here, as in &sect;M7.2.</div>", "D2.2": "\n      <h4>D2.2 &nbsp;⭐ Self-financing</h4>\n      <div class=\"eq\">$\\Phi_n\\cdot S_n=\\Phi_{n+1}\\cdot S_n$ &nbsp; (rebalance at today&rsquo;s prices, no cash in or out)</div>\n      <div class=\"eq\">$\\Rightarrow\\;\\Delta V_n=V_{n+1}-V_n=\\Phi_{n+1}\\cdot\\Delta S_n$</div>\n      <div class=\"use\"><b>Use when:</b> &ldquo;verify the strategy is self-financing&rdquo;: value the <em>old</em> holdings at the <em>new</em> prices, then check it equals the cost of the <em>new</em> holdings. Gains then come only from price moves.</div>\n      <div class=\"ex\"><b>Trick:</b> the binomial version is $\\Delta_tS_t+b_t(1+r)^t=\\Delta_{t+1}S_t+b_{t+1}(1+r)^t$ (&sect;D4.1). &sect;M4.7 is the same idea with cash $B$ instead of bond units $b$.</div>\n      <details class=\"proof\"><summary>Proof</summary><div>$V_{n+1}-V_n=\\Phi_{n+1}\\cdot S_{n+1}-\\Phi_n\\cdot S_n=\\Phi_{n+1}\\cdot S_{n+1}-\\Phi_{n+1}\\cdot S_n=\\Phi_{n+1}\\cdot\\Delta S_n$, using self-financing to swap $\\Phi_n\\cdot S_n$ for $\\Phi_{n+1}\\cdot S_n$.</div></details>", "D2.3": "\n      <h4>D2.3 &nbsp;No-arbitrage principle</h4>\n      <div class=\"eq\">there is <b>no</b> self-financing $\\Phi$ with $\\;V_0(\\Phi)=0,\\quad V_n(\\Phi)\\ge0,\\quad P\\big(V_n(\\Phi)&gt;0\\big)&gt;0$</div>\n      <div class=\"use\"><b>Use when:</b> &ldquo;define arbitrage / the NA condition formally&rdquo;. Three parts: costs nothing, never loses, sometimes wins.</div>\n      <div class=\"ex\"><b>Trick:</b> to <em>check</em> NA never search all strategies; find an EMM instead (&sect;D3.3). To <em>show</em> arbitrage, exhibit one $\\Phi$ meeting all three parts.</div>", "D3.1": "\n      <h4>D3.1 &nbsp;⭐ Model &amp; EMM</h4>\n      <div class=\"eq\">$\\Omega=\\{\\omega_1,\\dots,\\omega_N\\}$, &nbsp; $n$ stocks $S_t=(S_{t,1},\\dots,S_{t,n})$, &nbsp; bond $B_t=(1+r)^t$, &nbsp; $t=0,1$</div>\n      <div class=\"eq\">EMM $P^{*}$: &nbsp; $p_k^{*}=P^{*}(\\omega_k)&gt;0$ for every $k$ &nbsp;and&nbsp; $E^{*}\\!\\Big[\\dfrac{S_1}{1+r}\\Big]=S_0$</div>\n      <div class=\"use\"><b>Use when:</b> &ldquo;find all EMMs&rdquo;: unknowns $p_1^{*},\\dots,p_N^{*}$; one equation per stock, plus $\\sum_kp_k^{*}=1$; then keep only solutions with <em>every</em> $p_k^{*}&gt;0$.</div>\n      <div class=\"ex\"><b>Paper:</b> binomial ($n=1$, $N=2$): NA $\\iff S_0\\in\\Big(\\dfrac{S_1(d)}{1+r},\\dfrac{S_1(u)}{1+r}\\Big)\\iff d&lt;1+r&lt;u$; unique $p^{*}=\\frac{1+r-d}{u-d}$. Three states, one stock: a whole line of EMMs (&sect;A2.4).</div>\n      <details class=\"proof\"><summary>Proof</summary><div>One stock, two states: by &sect;D3.3 NA iff $S_0\\in D^0$, and $D^0$ is the open interval between $\\frac{S_1(d)}{1+r}$ and $\\frac{S_1(u)}{1+r}$. With $S_1(u)=uS_0$, $S_1(d)=dS_0$, divide by $S_0$: $\\frac d{1+r}&lt;1&lt;\\frac u{1+r}$, i.e. $d&lt;1+r&lt;u$.</div></details>", "D3.2": "\n      <h4>D3.2 &nbsp;Convex hull &amp; separation</h4>\n      <div class=\"eq\">$\\operatorname{conv}\\{y_1..y_N\\}=\\Big\\{\\sum_j\\alpha_jy_j:\\ \\alpha_j\\ge0,\\ \\sum_j\\alpha_j=1\\Big\\}$; &nbsp; relative interior $D^0$: all $\\alpha_j&gt;0$</div>\n      <div class=\"eq\"><b>Separation:</b> $D$ convex, $x_0\\notin D^0$ $\\Rightarrow$ $\\exists\\,a\\ne0$: $\\;a\\cdot(x-x_0)&gt;0\\;\\;\\forall x\\in D^0$</div>\n      <div class=\"use\"><b>Use when:</b> only inside the proof of &sect;D3.3. Picture: the hull is the polygon spanned by the discounted payoff vectors; $D^0$ is &ldquo;strictly inside&rdquo;.</div>\n      <div class=\"ex\"><b>Trick:</b> one stock &rArr; the hull is the interval $\\big[\\min_k\\frac{S_1(\\omega_k)}{1+r},\\max_k\\frac{S_1(\\omega_k)}{1+r}\\big]$ and $D^0$ the open interval. Separation then just says &ldquo;a point outside is to one side&rdquo;.</div>\n      <details class=\"proof\"><summary>Proof</summary><div>One dimension: $D^0$ is an open interval $(\\alpha,\\beta)$; a point $x_0\\notin D^0$ has $x_0\\le\\alpha$ (take $a=+1$) or $x_0\\ge\\beta$ (take $a=-1$).<br>General idea when $x_0$ is outside the closed hull: let $y$ be the nearest hull point and $a=y-x_0$. Convexity gives $a\\cdot(x-y)\\ge0$ for hull points $x$, so $a\\cdot(x-x_0)\\ge|a|^2&gt;0$. (The boundary case is quoted.)</div></details>", "D3.3": "\n      <h4>D3.3 &nbsp;⭐ First fundamental theorem (Theorem 2)</h4>\n      <div class=\"eq\">$\\text{NA}\\iff\\exists\\ \\text{EMM }P^{*}\\iff S_0\\in D^0,\\qquad D=\\operatorname{conv}\\Big\\{\\dfrac{S_1(\\omega_1)}{1+r},\\dots,\\dfrac{S_1(\\omega_N)}{1+r}\\Big\\}$</div>\n      <div class=\"use\"><b>Use when:</b> &ldquo;show the market is arbitrage-free&rdquo; &rArr; exhibit one EMM with all $p_k^{*}&gt;0$. &ldquo;Show there is arbitrage&rdquo; &rArr; show the EMM equations have no positive solution, then build $\\Delta$.</div>\n      <div class=\"ex\"><b>Trick:</b> &ldquo;discounted stock price is a martingale under $P^{*}$&rdquo; is the same sentence as $E^{*}[S_1/(1+r)]=S_0$.</div>\n      <details class=\"proof\"><summary>Proof</summary><div><b>Step 1 (EMM &hArr; $S_0\\in D^0$).</b> $S_0=\\sum_kp_k^{*}\\frac{S_1(\\omega_k)}{1+r}$ with $p_k^{*}&gt;0$, $\\sum p_k^{*}=1$ is word-for-word the definition of $D^0$ with $\\alpha_k=p_k^{*}$.<br><b>Step 2 (EMM &rArr; NA).</b> Suppose $(\\Delta,b)$ is an arbitrage: $V_0=\\Delta\\cdot S_0+b=0$, so $b=-\\Delta\\cdot S_0$ and $\\frac{V_1}{1+r}=\\Delta\\cdot\\big(\\frac{S_1}{1+r}-S_0\\big)\\ge0$ always, $&gt;0$ in some state. Take $E^{*}$: $\\Delta\\cdot\\big(E^{*}[\\frac{S_1}{1+r}]-S_0\\big)=0$. But a variable $\\ge0$ that is $&gt;0$ on a state with $p_k^{*}&gt;0$ has positive mean. Contradiction.<br><b>Step 3 (NA &rArr; $S_0\\in D^0$).</b> If $S_0\\notin D^0$, separation (&sect;D3.2) gives $\\Delta$ with $\\Delta\\cdot\\big(\\frac{S_1(\\omega)}{1+r}-S_0\\big)\\ge0$ for every $\\omega$, $&gt;0$ for some. Then $(\\Delta,\\,b=-\\Delta\\cdot S_0)$ costs $0$ and pays $(1+r)\\Delta\\cdot(\\frac{S_1}{1+r}-S_0)\\ge0$, sometimes $&gt;0$: an arbitrage. Contradiction.</div></details>", "D3.4": "\n      <h4>D3.4 &nbsp;⭐ Pricing attainable claims (Theorem 3)</h4>\n      <div class=\"eq\">any strategy $(\\Delta,b)$: $\\;E^{*}\\Big[\\dfrac{V_1}{1+r}\\Big]=V_0$</div>\n      <div class=\"eq\">$X$ attainable ($\\exists(\\Delta,b)$ with $V_1=X$ in every state) $\\Rightarrow$ $\\;X^{*}=V_0=E^{*}\\Big[\\dfrac{X}{1+r}\\Big]$, the <b>same for every EMM</b></div>\n      <div class=\"use\"><b>Use when:</b> &ldquo;price the claim&rdquo; when there are several EMMs. If $E^{*}[X/(1+r)]$ changes as you move along the EMM line, $X$ is <em>not</em> attainable and you get an interval (&sect;A2.4).</div>\n      <div class=\"ex\"><b>Trick:</b> to test attainability, compute $E^{*}[X/(1+r)]$ with the free parameter left in. Parameter cancels &rArr; attainable, one price.</div>\n      <details class=\"proof\"><summary>Proof</summary><div>$\\frac{V_1}{1+r}=\\Delta\\cdot\\frac{S_1}{1+r}+b$. Take $E^{*}$ and use $E^{*}[\\frac{S_1}{1+r}]=S_0$: $E^{*}[\\frac{V_1}{1+r}]=\\Delta\\cdot S_0+b=V_0$.<br>If $X$ is attainable, $V_1=X$ in every state, so $E^{*}[\\frac{X}{1+r}]=V_0$ for <em>every</em> EMM, and $V_0$ does not depend on which $P^{*}$ you used. Any other price: sell the dearer of {claim, replicating portfolio}, buy the cheaper, pocket the gap.</div></details>", "D3.5": "\n      <h4>D3.5 &nbsp;⭐ Completeness (Theorem 4)</h4>\n      <div class=\"eq\">complete (every claim attainable) $\\iff$ the EMM is <b>unique</b></div>\n      <div class=\"eq\">counting rule: unique EMM needs <b>states $=$ assets</b>, $N=n+1$, and a non-singular matrix $A$</div>\n      <div class=\"use\"><b>Use when:</b> &ldquo;is the market complete?&rdquo; Count $N$ states vs $n+1$ assets (stocks + bond), then solve the EMM system: one solution &rArr; complete; a line of solutions &rArr; incomplete.</div>\n      <div class=\"ex\"><b>Paper:</b> binomial: $N=2$, $n+1=2$ &rArr; complete. One stock, three prices (&sect;A2.4): $N=3&gt;2$ &rArr; incomplete, price interval $(\\frac{50}3,30)$.</div>\n      <details class=\"proof\"><summary>Proof</summary><div>(&lArr;) Unique EMM &rArr; the EMM system is square with $\\det A\\ne0$. The replication system $\\Delta\\cdot S_1(\\omega_k)+b(1+r)=X(\\omega_k)$, $k=1..N$, has matrix $A^{\\mathsf T}$, also non-singular, so it has a solution for <em>every</em> $X$: complete.<br>(&rArr;) Complete, and suppose two EMMs $P^{*},\\widetilde P^{*}$. Take the claim $X_i$ that pays $1$ in state $\\omega_i$ and $0$ elsewhere. It is attainable, so by Theorem 3 $E^{*}[X_i]=\\widetilde E^{*}[X_i]$, i.e. $p_i^{*}=\\widetilde p_i^{*}$. True for every $i$, so $P^{*}=\\widetilde P^{*}$.</div></details>", "D4.1": "\n      <h4>D4.1 &nbsp;Setup &amp; self-financing</h4>\n      <div class=\"eq\">$S_t=uS_{t-1}$ or $dS_{t-1}$, &nbsp; $B_t=(1+r)^t$, &nbsp; $(\\Delta_t,b_t)$ held over $(t-1,t]$, chosen from $S_0..S_{t-1}$</div>\n      <div class=\"eq\">self-financing: $\\;\\Delta_tS_t+b_t(1+r)^t=\\Delta_{t+1}S_t+b_{t+1}(1+r)^t$, &nbsp; $V_0=\\Delta_1S_0+b_1$</div>\n      <div class=\"use\"><b>Use when:</b> &ldquo;verify self-financing&rdquo; at a node: old portfolio at the new price $=$ cost of the new portfolio. $b_t$ counts <em>bonds</em>; their value is $b_t(1+r)^t$.</div>\n      <div class=\"ex\"><b>Trick:</b> at $t=1$: $V_1=\\Delta_1S_1+b_1(1+r)=\\Delta_2S_1+b_2(1+r)$ &mdash; you only reshuffle $(\\Delta_1,b_1)$ into $(\\Delta_2,b_2)$.</div>", "D4.2": "\n      <h4>D4.2 &nbsp;⭐ Backward induction (price + hedge at every node)</h4>\n      <div class=\"eq\">at node $S_{t-1}=s$: &nbsp; $\\Delta_t(s)=\\dfrac{V_t[us]-V_t[ds]}{us-ds},\\qquad b_t(s)=\\dfrac{u\\,V_t[ds]-d\\,V_t[us]}{(1+r)^t(u-d)}$</div>\n      <div class=\"eq\">$V_{t-1}[s]=\\dfrac{p^{*}V_t[us]+(1-p^{*})V_t[ds]}{1+r},\\qquad V_T=g(S_T),\\qquad p^{*}=\\dfrac{1+r-d}{u-d}$</div>\n      <div class=\"use\"><b>Use when:</b> any tree question: &ldquo;price / replicating strategy at each node&rdquo;. Start at the leaves (payoffs), step back one period at a time.</div>\n      <div class=\"ex\"><b>Worked:</b> $S_0=100$, $u=2$, $d=\\tfrac12$, $r=\\tfrac14$ &rArr; $p^{*}=\\tfrac12$. Call $K=100$, $T=2$. Leaves $400,100,25$ &rarr; payoffs $300,0,0$. $V_1[200]=\\frac{150}{1.25}=120$, $V_1[50]=0$, $V_0=\\frac{60}{1.25}=\\mathbf{48}$. Hedge: $\\Delta_1=\\frac{120}{150}=0.8$, $b_1=\\frac{-60}{1.875}=-32$ (check $80-32=48$ ✓); at $200$: $\\Delta_2=1$, $b_2=-64$ (check $200-80=120$ ✓).</div>\n      <details class=\"proof\"><summary>Proof</summary><div>At a node it is the one-period problem (&sect;A1.5) with bond worth $(1+r)^t$: solve $\\Delta us+b(1+r)^t=V_u$, $\\Delta ds+b(1+r)^t=V_d$. Subtract for $\\Delta$, back-substitute for $b$.<br>Cost at $t-1$: $\\Delta s+b(1+r)^{t-1}=\\frac{V_u-V_d}{u-d}+\\frac{uV_d-dV_u}{(1+r)(u-d)}=\\frac{(1+r-d)V_u+(u-1-r)V_d}{(1+r)(u-d)}=\\frac{p^{*}V_u+(1-p^{*})V_d}{1+r}$.</div></details>", "D4.3": "\n      <h4>D4.3 &nbsp;⭐ General value formula &amp; martingale</h4>\n      <div class=\"eq\">$V_t[s]=\\dfrac{1}{(1+r)^{T-t}}\\displaystyle\\sum_{j=0}^{T-t}\\binom{T-t}{j}(p^{*})^j(1-p^{*})^{T-t-j}\\,g\\big(u^jd^{T-t-j}s\\big)$</div>\n      <div class=\"eq\">$V_0=E^{*}\\Big[\\dfrac{g(S_0e^{Z_T})}{(1+r)^T}\\Big]$, &nbsp; $Z_T=Y_1+\\dots+Y_T$, &nbsp; $Y_k=\\ln u$ (prob $p^{*}$) or $\\ln d$</div>\n      <div class=\"eq\">$S^{*}_t=S_t(1+r)^{-t}$ is a MG under $p^{*}$, and $p^{*}$ is the <b>only</b> such measure &rArr; arbitrage-free and complete</div>\n      <div class=\"use\"><b>Use when:</b> pricing $g(S_T)$ straight from $t=0$ without drawing the tree. Only the <em>number</em> of up-moves $j$ matters, not their order, hence $\\binom Tj$.</div>\n      <div class=\"ex\"><b>Check:</b> worked example of &sect;D4.2: $\\frac{1}{1.25^2}\\big[\\tfrac14\\cdot300\\big]=\\frac{75}{1.5625}=\\mathbf{48}$ ✓.</div>\n      <details class=\"proof\"><summary>Proof</summary><div>Apply &sect;D4.2 repeatedly. Each step back divides by $(1+r)$ and averages the two children with weights $p^{*},1-p^{*}$. After $T-t$ steps you are averaging over $T-t$ independent up/down moves; the number of ups is $\\mathrm{Bin}(T-t,p^{*})$, and $j$ ups land at $u^jd^{T-t-j}s$.</div></details>", "D4.4": "\n      <h4>D4.4 &nbsp;⭐ T-period call price (CRR)</h4>\n      <div class=\"eq\">$C=\\dfrac{1}{(1+r)^T}\\displaystyle\\sum_{j=m}^{T}\\binom Tj(p^{*})^j(1-p^{*})^{T-j}\\big(u^jd^{T-j}S_0-K\\big)=S_0\\,P(\\hat U\\ge m)-\\dfrac{K}{(1+r)^T}\\,P(U^{*}\\ge m)$</div>\n      <div class=\"eq\">$m=\\Big\\lfloor\\dfrac{\\ln K-\\ln S_0-T\\ln d}{\\ln u-\\ln d}\\Big\\rfloor+1,\\quad U^{*}\\sim\\mathrm{Bin}(T,p^{*}),\\quad \\hat U\\sim\\mathrm{Bin}(T,\\hat p),\\quad \\hat p=\\dfrac{u\\,p^{*}}{1+r}$</div>\n      <div class=\"use\"><b>Use when:</b> &ldquo;price a European call over $T$ periods&rdquo;. Find $m$ first (fewest ups to finish in the money); all terms with $j&lt;m$ are $0$.</div>\n      <div class=\"ex\"><b>Worked</b> (&sect;D4.2 data): $m=\\lfloor\\frac{0+2\\ln2}{2\\ln2}\\rfloor+1=2$; $\\hat p=\\frac{2\\cdot0.5}{1.25}=0.8$. $C=100(0.8)^2-\\frac{100}{1.5625}(0.5)^2=64-16=\\mathbf{48}$ ✓.</div>\n      <details class=\"proof\"><summary>Proof</summary><div>$(u^jd^{T-j}S_0-K)^+&gt;0\\iff j\\ln u+(T-j)\\ln d&gt;\\ln K-\\ln S_0\\iff j\\ge m$. Split the sum into the $S_0$ part and the $K$ part. In the $S_0$ part, $\\frac{u^jd^{T-j}(p^{*})^j(1-p^{*})^{T-j}}{(1+r)^T}=\\hat p^{\\,j}(1-\\hat p)^{T-j}$ with $1-\\hat p=\\frac{d(1-p^{*})}{1+r}$; these add to $1$ because $up^{*}+d(1-p^{*})=1+r$. So both parts are binomial tail probabilities.</div></details>", "D5.1": "\n      <h4>D5.1 &nbsp;Binomial &rarr; Black&ndash;Scholes (the limit)</h4>\n      <div class=\"eq\">$n$ steps of length $\\delta_n=T/n$: &nbsp; $u_n=e^{\\sigma\\sqrt{\\delta_n}},\\ d_n=e^{-\\sigma\\sqrt{\\delta_n}},\\ 1+r_n=e^{r\\delta_n}$</div>\n      <div class=\"eq\">$p^{*}_n=\\dfrac{e^{r\\delta_n}-e^{-\\sigma\\sqrt{\\delta_n}}}{e^{\\sigma\\sqrt{\\delta_n}}-e^{-\\sigma\\sqrt{\\delta_n}}}\\approx\\dfrac12+\\dfrac{r-\\sigma^2/2}{2\\sigma}\\sqrt{\\delta_n}\\;\\to\\;\\dfrac12$</div>\n      <div class=\"eq\">$S_T=S_0e^{Z_n}$, $Z_n$ = sum of $n$ i.i.d. jumps $\\pm\\sigma\\sqrt{T/n}$; CLT on the two tails of &sect;D4.4 gives &sect;D5.2</div>\n      <div class=\"use\"><b>Use when:</b> &ldquo;derive the Black&ndash;Scholes formula from the binomial model&rdquo;. Write the three choices ($u_n,d_n,r_n$), then $p^{*}_n$, then apply the CLT to $P(U^{*}\\ge m)$ and $P(\\hat U\\ge m)$.</div>\n      <div class=\"ex\"><b>Note:</b> the lecture writes $p_n^{*}\\approx\\frac12+\\frac{r}{2\\sigma}\\sqrt{\\delta_n}$ (first order). Keeping the $\\frac{\\sigma^2}{2}\\delta_n$ term from $e^{-\\sigma\\sqrt{\\delta_n}}$ is what produces the $-\\frac{\\sigma^2}2$ in $h-\\sigma\\sqrt T$; quote it if asked to show the limit in full.</div>\n      <details class=\"proof\"><summary>Proof</summary><div>$P(U^{*}\\ge m)\\approx\\Phi\\Big(\\frac{np^{*}_n-m}{\\sqrt{np_n^{*}(1-p_n^{*})}}\\Big)$ by the CLT. With $\\ln d_n=-\\sigma\\sqrt{\\delta_n}$: $m\\approx\\frac{\\ln(K/S_0)}{2\\sigma\\sqrt{\\delta_n}}+\\frac n2$ and $np^{*}_n\\approx\\frac n2+\\frac{n(r-\\sigma^2/2)\\sqrt{\\delta_n}}{2\\sigma}$. So $np^{*}_n-m\\approx\\frac{\\ln(S_0/K)+(r-\\sigma^2/2)T}{2\\sigma\\sqrt{\\delta_n}}$, and dividing by $\\sqrt{n}/2$ gives $\\frac{\\ln(S_0/K)+(r-\\sigma^2/2)T}{\\sigma\\sqrt T}=h-\\sigma\\sqrt T$.<br>The same steps with $\\hat p_n$ (one extra $+\\sigma^2$ from $u_n$) give $h$. Finally $(1+r_n)^{-n}=e^{-rT}$.</div></details>", "D5.2": "\n      <h4>D5.2 &nbsp;⭐ Black&ndash;Scholes formula</h4>\n      <div class=\"eq\">$C=S_0\\,\\Phi(d_1)-Ke^{-rT}\\,\\Phi(d_2)$</div>\n      <div class=\"eq\">$d_1=h=\\dfrac{\\ln(S_0/K)+(r+\\tfrac12\\sigma^2)T}{\\sigma\\sqrt T},\\qquad d_2=d_1-\\sigma\\sqrt T$</div>\n      <div class=\"eq\">put (by parity, &sect;M4.6 with $e^{-rT}$): $\\;P=Ke^{-rT}\\Phi(-d_2)-S_0\\Phi(-d_1)$</div>\n      <div class=\"use\"><b>Use when:</b> numeric option price in continuous time. The real drift $\\mu$ <b>does not appear</b> &mdash; only $r$ and $\\sigma$.</div>\n      <div class=\"ex\"><b>Worked:</b> $S_0=K=100$, $r=0.05$, $\\sigma=0.2$, $T=1$: $d_1=\\frac{0.07}{0.2}=0.35$, $d_2=0.15$; $\\Phi(0.35)=0.6368$, $\\Phi(0.15)=0.5596$. $C=63.68-95.12(0.5596)=\\mathbf{10.45}$; $P=10.45-100+95.12=\\mathbf{5.57}$.</div>\n      <details class=\"proof\"><summary>Proof</summary><div>Under $P^{*}$ (&sect;D5.4) $S_T=S_0e^{(r-\\sigma^2/2)T+\\sigma\\sqrt T Z}$, $Z\\sim N(0,1)$, and $C=e^{-rT}E^{*}[(S_T-K)^+]$.<br>$S_T&gt;K\\iff Z&gt;-d_2$. So $e^{-rT}E^{*}[K\\mathbf 1_{\\{Z&gt;-d_2\\}}]=Ke^{-rT}\\Phi(d_2)$.<br>$e^{-rT}E^{*}[S_T\\mathbf 1_{\\{Z&gt;-d_2\\}}]=S_0\\int_{-d_2}^{\\infty}e^{-\\sigma^2T/2+\\sigma\\sqrt Tz}\\varphi(z)\\,dz=S_0\\int_{-d_2}^{\\infty}\\varphi(z-\\sigma\\sqrt T)\\,dz=S_0\\Phi(d_2+\\sigma\\sqrt T)=S_0\\Phi(d_1)$ (complete the square, &sect;1.2). Subtract.</div></details>", "D5.3": "\n      <h4>D5.3 &nbsp;Black&ndash;Scholes market</h4>\n      <div class=\"eq\">bond $B_t=e^{rt}$ ($dB_t=rB_t\\,dt$); &nbsp; stock $dS_t=\\mu S_t\\,dt+\\sigma S_t\\,dW_t$</div>\n      <div class=\"eq\">$S_t=S_0\\exp\\Big\\{\\big(\\mu-\\tfrac{\\sigma^2}2\\big)t+\\sigma W_t\\Big\\}$ &nbsp; (GBM, &sect;I7.6)</div>\n      <div class=\"use\"><b>Use when:</b> &ldquo;solve the Black&ndash;Scholes SDE&rdquo; or as the starting line of any BS question.</div>\n      <div class=\"ex\"><b>Trick:</b> the $-\\frac{\\sigma^2}{2}$ is the Itô correction; under $P^{*}$ just replace $\\mu$ by $r$ (&sect;D5.4).</div>\n      <details class=\"proof\"><summary>Proof</summary><div>Itô on $\\ln S$: $d\\ln S_t=\\frac{dS_t}{S_t}-\\frac{(dS_t)^2}{2S_t^2}=\\mu\\,dt+\\sigma\\,dW_t-\\frac{\\sigma^2}{2}dt$. Integrate: $\\ln S_t-\\ln S_0=(\\mu-\\frac{\\sigma^2}2)t+\\sigma W_t$; exponentiate.</div></details>", "D5.4": "\n      <h4>D5.4 &nbsp;⭐ EMM in the Black&ndash;Scholes market</h4>\n      <div class=\"eq\">$Z_t=S_0\\exp\\Big\\{\\displaystyle\\int_0^ta_s\\,ds+\\sigma W_t\\Big\\}$: &nbsp; $e^{-rt}Z_t$ is a MG $\\iff a_t=r-\\dfrac{\\sigma^2}{2}$</div>\n      <div class=\"eq\">so under $P^{*}$: $\\;S_t=S_0\\exp\\big\\{(r-\\tfrac{\\sigma^2}2)t+\\sigma W_t^{*}\\big\\}$ &nbsp; ($\\mu\\to r$)</div>\n      <div class=\"eq\">arbitrage-free <b>and</b> complete; &nbsp; $P_t(X)=E^{*}\\big[e^{-r(T-t)}X\\mid\\mathcal F_t\\big]$, &nbsp; $P_0(X)=E^{*}[e^{-rT}X]$</div>\n      <div class=\"use\"><b>Use when:</b> &ldquo;is the BS market arbitrage-free? complete? find the EMM&rdquo;. Answer: yes, yes, the drift becomes $r$. Then price any claim as a discounted $E^{*}$.</div>\n      <div class=\"ex\"><b>Trick:</b> &ldquo;MG &hArr; zero $dt$ coefficient&rdquo; (&sect;I5.1) is the whole proof; the lecture writes it as a product of two Itô processes.</div>\n      <details class=\"proof\"><summary>Proof</summary><div>Write $e^{-rt}Z_t=S_0X_tY_t$ with $X_t=e^{\\int_0^ta_s\\,ds}$, $Y_t=e^{-rt+\\sigma W_t}$. Then $dX_t=a_tX_t\\,dt$ and $dY_t=Y_t(-r+\\tfrac{\\sigma^2}{2})\\,dt+\\sigma Y_t\\,dW_t$.<br>Product rule ($dX\\,dY=0$): $d(XY)=X_tY_t\\big(a_t-r+\\tfrac{\\sigma^2}2\\big)dt+\\sigma X_tY_t\\,dW_t$. Since $X_tY_t&gt;0$, the $dt$ coefficient is $0$ iff $a_t=r-\\frac{\\sigma^2}{2}$. Such a measure exists and is unique, so by the three facts (&sect;D5.5) the market is arbitrage-free and complete.</div></details>", "D5.5": "\n      <h4>D5.5 &nbsp;Martingales &amp; claim pricing: the three facts</h4>\n      <div class=\"eq\">(1) an EMM $P^{*}$ exists with $S^{*}_t=(1+r)^{-t}S_t$ / $e^{-rt}S_t$ a MG &rArr; <b>arbitrage-free</b></div>\n      <div class=\"eq\">(2) attainable $X$: $P_t(X)=E^{*}\\big[(1+r)^{t-T}X\\mid\\mathcal F_t\\big]$ / $E^{*}\\big[e^{-r(T-t)}X\\mid\\mathcal F_t\\big]$</div>\n      <div class=\"eq\">(3) $P^{*}$ unique &rArr; <b>complete</b>: every claim attainable, so every claim has one price</div>\n      <div class=\"use\"><b>Use when:</b> short note on &ldquo;martingales and claim pricing&rdquo;, or as the conclusion line after finding the EMM in any model.</div>\n      <div class=\"ex\"><b>Trick:</b> these are &sect;D3.3, &sect;D3.4, &sect;D3.5 restated for any time grid. Discrete: discount with $(1+r)$; continuous: with $e^{r}$.</div>\n      <details class=\"proof\"><summary>Proof</summary><div>(1) is Step 2 of &sect;D3.3 (continuous time: &sect;D5.4). (2) is &sect;D3.4 applied one period at a time, with the tower property joining the periods (&sect;D4.3). (3) is &sect;D3.5.</div></details>", "0": "<h4><span class=\"section-num\">0.</span>Routing Table — read the question, jump to the card</h4>", "F1": "<h4><span class=\"section-num\">F1.</span>Probability Space &amp; &sigma;-Fields</h4>", "F2": "<h4><span class=\"section-num\">F2.</span>Almost Surely &amp; Borel&ndash;Cantelli</h4>", "F3": "<h4><span class=\"section-num\">F3.</span>Random Variables &amp; Measurability</h4>", "F4": "<h4><span class=\"section-num\">F4.</span>Generated &sigma;-Fields &amp; the Doob&ndash;Dynkin Lemma</h4>", "F5": "<h4><span class=\"section-num\">F5.</span>Distribution Measure &amp; the d.f.</h4>", "F6": "<h4><span class=\"section-num\">F6.</span>Densities &amp; Joint Distributions</h4>", "F7": "<h4><span class=\"section-num\">F7.</span>Multivariate Normal &amp; Joint MGF</h4>", "E1": "<h4><span class=\"section-num\">E1.</span>Expectation as a Lebesgue Integral</h4>", "E2": "<h4><span class=\"section-num\">E2.</span>Change of Variables for Expectation</h4>", "E3": "<h4><span class=\"section-num\">E3.</span>Conditional Probability &amp; Independence</h4>", "E4": "<h4><span class=\"section-num\">E4.</span>Conditional Expectation &amp; &sigma;-Fields</h4>", "M1": "<h4><span class=\"section-num\">M1.</span>Filtrations &amp; Adapted Processes</h4>", "M2": "<h4><span class=\"section-num\">M2.</span>Discrete-Time Martingales</h4>", "M3": "<h4><span class=\"section-num\">M3.</span>The Binomial Market Model</h4>", "M4": "<h4><span class=\"section-num\">M4.</span>No-Arbitrage Pricing in the Binomial Model</h4>", "M5": "<h4><span class=\"section-num\">M5.</span>Stopping Times</h4>", "M6": "<h4><span class=\"section-num\">M6.</span>Stopped Process &amp; Optional Stopping</h4>", "M7": "<h4><span class=\"section-num\">M7.</span>Gambling Strategies &amp; Fairness</h4>", "1": "<h4><span class=\"section-num\">1.</span>Normal &amp; MVN Toolkit — the plug-in layer</h4>", "2": "<h4><span class=\"section-num\">2.</span>Random Walks — Additive &amp; Geometric</h4>", "3": "<h4><span class=\"section-num\">3.</span>Gambler&rsquo;s Ruin — probability <em>and</em> duration</h4>", "4": "<h4><span class=\"section-num\">4.</span>Random Walk &rarr; Brownian Motion (scaling limit)</h4>", "5": "<h4><span class=\"section-num\">5.</span>Wiener Process — Axioms &amp; Identification Tests</h4>", "6": "<h4><span class=\"section-num\">6.</span>Covariance &amp; Moments — the $\\min(s,t)$ machine</h4>", "7": "<h4><span class=\"section-num\">7.</span>Martingales of Brownian Motion &amp; the Drift Test</h4>", "8": "<h4><span class=\"section-num\">8.</span>Density, Transition Kernel &amp; the Diffusion Equation</h4>", "9": "<h4><span class=\"section-num\">9.</span>Conditioning &amp; the Brownian Bridge</h4>", "10": "<h4><span class=\"section-num\">10.</span>BM with Drift — Exit Probabilities &amp; Optimal Stopping</h4>", "11": "<h4><span class=\"section-num\">11.</span>Geometric Brownian Motion</h4>", "12": "<h4><span class=\"section-num\">12.</span>Hitting Times &amp; the Reflection Principle</h4>", "13": "<h4><span class=\"section-num\">13.</span>The Running Maximum $M(t)$ — alone</h4>", "14": "<h4><span class=\"section-num\">14.</span>Joint Law of $\\big(W(t),M(t)\\big)$ — the master recipe</h4>", "I1": "<h4><span class=\"section-num\">I1.</span>The It&ocirc; Integral</h4>", "I2": "<h4><span class=\"section-num\">I2.</span>It&ocirc; Processes &amp; the Multiplication Table</h4>", "I3": "<h4><span class=\"section-num\">I3.</span>It&ocirc;&rsquo;s Formula — the three forms</h4>", "I4": "<h4><span class=\"section-num\">I4.</span>⭐ The Differential Dictionary — memorize these</h4>", "I5": "<h4><span class=\"section-num\">I5.</span>Martingale Test &amp; Computing Expectations</h4>", "I6": "<h4><span class=\"section-num\">I6.</span>Stochastic Differential Equations — setup &amp; verification</h4>", "I7": "<h4><span class=\"section-num\">I7.</span>The Solvable SDEs</h4>", "I8": "<h4><span class=\"section-num\">I8.</span>Worked Past-Paper Walkthroughs</h4>", "A1": "<h4><span class=\"section-num\">A1.</span>Arbitrage &amp; the Risk-Neutral Measure</h4>", "A2": "<h4><span class=\"section-num\">A2.</span>Contingent Claims, the Arbitrage Theorem &amp; Incompleteness</h4>", "D1": "<h4><span class=\"section-num\">D1.</span>Discounting &amp; Forwards</h4>", "D2": "<h4><span class=\"section-num\">D2.</span>General Discrete Market Model</h4>", "D3": "<h4><span class=\"section-num\">D3.</span>Finite Single-Period Market (no-arbitrage, pricing, completeness)</h4>", "D4": "<h4><span class=\"section-num\">D4.</span>Multi-Period Binomial Market</h4>", "D5": "<h4><span class=\"section-num\">D5.</span>Black&ndash;Scholes</h4>"};
